1
00:00:04,374 --> 00:00:07,874
(upbeat orchestral music)

2
00:01:10,011 --> 00:01:12,678
(babies crying)

3
00:01:19,349 --> 00:01:22,182
(chisel pounding)

4
00:01:51,053 --> 00:01:53,674
(whistling)

5
00:01:53,674 --> 00:01:56,555
(tongue clicking)

6
00:01:56,555 --> 00:01:58,888
(whistling)

7
00:02:02,022 --> 00:02:04,855
(tongue clicking)

8
00:02:22,924 --> 00:02:24,410
- Yeah, D.B.

9
00:02:24,410 --> 00:02:26,210
Oh, just cleaning out the deadwood.

10
00:02:26,210 --> 00:02:27,043
Okay.

11
00:02:28,020 --> 00:02:30,010
- Look, Mr. Connell, I just can't afford

12
00:02:30,010 --> 00:02:32,410
to be without work right
now, not even for a day.

13
00:02:32,410 --> 00:02:34,060
I've got a mother and two kid sisters to--

14
00:02:34,060 --> 00:02:36,290
- [Secretary] More good luck telegrams.

15
00:02:36,290 --> 00:02:38,310
- Well, you know how it is.

16
00:02:38,310 --> 00:02:40,350
I've just got to keep on working, see?

17
00:02:40,350 --> 00:02:42,730
- Sorry, sister, I was sent
down here to clean house.

18
00:02:42,730 --> 00:02:44,918
I told you, I can't use
your column anymore.

19
00:02:44,918 --> 00:02:46,114
It's lavender and old lace.

20
00:02:46,114 --> 00:02:47,633
- Sir?
- Send those other people in.

21
00:02:47,633 --> 00:02:48,990
- I'll tell you what I'll do.

22
00:02:48,990 --> 00:02:50,650
I get $30.

23
00:02:50,650 --> 00:02:52,930
I'll take $25, $20 if necessary.

24
00:02:52,930 --> 00:02:53,870
I'll do anything you say.

25
00:02:53,870 --> 00:02:55,370
- It isn't the money.

26
00:02:55,370 --> 00:02:56,440
We're after circulation.

27
00:02:56,440 --> 00:02:58,030
What we need is fireworks,

28
00:02:58,030 --> 00:02:59,881
people who can hit with
sledgehammers, start arguments.

29
00:02:59,881 --> 00:03:00,714
- Oh, I can do that.

30
00:03:00,714 --> 00:03:02,300
I know this town inside-out.

31
00:03:02,300 --> 00:03:03,133
Oh, give me a chance, please.

32
00:03:03,133 --> 00:03:05,790
- All right, come in, come in, come in.

33
00:03:05,790 --> 00:03:07,370
Cashier's got your check.

34
00:03:07,370 --> 00:03:08,950
Who are these people?

35
00:03:08,950 --> 00:03:10,990
Gibbs, Crawley, Cunningham, Giles

36
00:03:12,140 --> 00:03:13,099
Hey you, sister.

37
00:03:13,099 --> 00:03:14,187
Don't forget to get out your last column

38
00:03:14,187 --> 00:03:15,713
before you pick up your check.

39
00:03:17,803 --> 00:03:20,470
(door slamming)

40
00:03:27,271 --> 00:03:30,438
(trashcan clattering)

41
00:03:34,790 --> 00:03:37,070
- You're a couple of sticks
shy in your column, Ann.

42
00:03:37,070 --> 00:03:39,110
- Big, rich slob like
D.B. Norton buys a paper

43
00:03:39,110 --> 00:03:40,890
and 40 heads are chopped off.

44
00:03:40,890 --> 00:03:43,210
- Did you get it, too?
- Yeah.

45
00:03:43,210 --> 00:03:44,043
You too?

46
00:03:45,015 --> 00:03:46,330
Oh, Joe.

47
00:03:46,330 --> 00:03:47,430
Oh, I'm sorry darling.

48
00:03:48,600 --> 00:03:50,580
Why don't we tear the building down?

49
00:03:50,580 --> 00:03:51,760
- Before you do, Ann,

50
00:03:51,760 --> 00:03:53,530
perhaps you'd better finish this column.

51
00:03:53,530 --> 00:03:55,083
- Yeah, lavender and old lace.

52
00:03:57,170 --> 00:03:58,430
Wait, Joe.

53
00:03:58,430 --> 00:03:59,263
Wait.

54
00:04:02,979 --> 00:04:04,979
He wants fireworks, huh?

55
00:04:05,957 --> 00:04:07,089
Okay.

56
00:04:07,089 --> 00:04:10,672
(typewriter keys clacking)

57
00:04:24,267 --> 00:04:25,100
Here.

58
00:04:26,360 --> 00:04:28,863
- Below is a letter which
reached my desk this morning.

59
00:04:28,863 --> 00:04:30,350
It's a commentary on what we

60
00:04:30,350 --> 00:04:32,490
laughingly call a civilized world.

61
00:04:32,490 --> 00:04:34,610
Dear Miss Mitchell, four years ago,

62
00:04:34,610 --> 00:04:36,310
I was fired outta my job.

63
00:04:36,310 --> 00:04:38,650
Since then, I haven't been
able to get another one.

64
00:04:38,650 --> 00:04:41,070
At first I was sore at
the state administration

65
00:04:41,070 --> 00:04:43,590
because it's on account
of the slimy politics here

66
00:04:43,590 --> 00:04:45,921
we have all this unemployment.

67
00:04:45,921 --> 00:04:47,280
But in looking around,

68
00:04:47,280 --> 00:04:49,280
it seems the whole world's going to pot.

69
00:04:49,280 --> 00:04:51,450
So, in protest, I'm
going to commit suicide

70
00:04:51,450 --> 00:04:53,380
by jumping off the City Hall roof.

71
00:04:53,380 --> 00:04:57,040
Signed, a disgusted
American citizen, John Doe.

72
00:04:57,040 --> 00:04:59,760
Editor's note, if you ask this column,

73
00:04:59,760 --> 00:05:02,890
the wrong people are
jumping off the roofs.

74
00:05:02,890 --> 00:05:05,940
Hey, Ann, this is the
old fak-a-roo, isn't it?

75
00:05:05,940 --> 00:05:07,947
- Nevermind that, Joe, go ahead.

76
00:05:07,947 --> 00:05:10,682
(laughing)

77
00:05:10,682 --> 00:05:13,099
(chattering)

78
00:05:19,593 --> 00:05:20,426
(glass shattering)

79
00:05:20,426 --> 00:05:23,343
(workers laughing)

80
00:05:30,100 --> 00:05:31,943
- And it's because of the slimy politics

81
00:05:31,943 --> 00:05:33,910
that we have all this unemployment here.

82
00:05:33,910 --> 00:05:35,670
There it is.

83
00:05:35,670 --> 00:05:37,840
That's D.B. Norton's opening
attack on the governor.

84
00:05:37,840 --> 00:05:40,280
- Why, Jim, it's just a
letter sent into a column.

85
00:05:40,280 --> 00:05:42,490
- No, no, I can smell it, that's Norton.

86
00:05:42,490 --> 00:05:43,435
- [Governor] Good morning, gentlemen.

87
00:05:43,435 --> 00:05:44,711
- Good morning, Governor.
- Good morning, Governor.

88
00:05:44,711 --> 00:05:45,700
- [Governor] You're here rather early.

89
00:05:45,700 --> 00:05:48,550
- Governor, did you happen to
see this in the New Bulletin?

90
00:05:48,550 --> 00:05:49,780
- Yes.

91
00:05:49,780 --> 00:05:52,000
I had it served with my
breakfast this morning.

92
00:05:52,000 --> 00:05:53,870
- Jim thinks it's D.B. Norton at work.

93
00:05:53,870 --> 00:05:54,820
- Of course it is.

94
00:05:54,820 --> 00:05:56,300
- Oh, come, Jim.

95
00:05:56,300 --> 00:05:57,410
That little item?

96
00:05:57,410 --> 00:05:59,727
D.B. Norton does things
in a much bigger way.

97
00:05:59,727 --> 00:06:01,263
- {Jim] This is his opening
attack on you, Governor,

98
00:06:01,263 --> 00:06:03,144
take my word for it.

99
00:06:03,144 --> 00:06:04,820
What did he buy a paper for?

100
00:06:04,820 --> 00:06:07,600
Why did he engage a high-pressure
editor like Connell for?

101
00:06:07,600 --> 00:06:09,100
He's in the oil business.

102
00:06:09,100 --> 00:06:11,080
I tell you, Governor,
he's after your scalp.

103
00:06:11,080 --> 00:06:13,760
- All right, Jim, all right,
don't burst a blood vessel.

104
00:06:13,760 --> 00:06:16,145
I'll look into it.

105
00:06:16,145 --> 00:06:17,157
- [Secretary] Yes sir?

106
00:06:17,157 --> 00:06:19,940
- Get me Spencer of the
Daily Chronicle please.

107
00:06:19,940 --> 00:06:21,820
- Yes, yes, I saw it, Governor.

108
00:06:21,820 --> 00:06:23,750
If you ask me, that's a phony letter.

109
00:06:23,750 --> 00:06:26,060
Why, that gag has got whiskers on it.

110
00:06:26,060 --> 00:06:26,893
Hmm?

111
00:06:26,893 --> 00:06:27,920
Okay, I'll get the mayor

112
00:06:27,920 --> 00:06:30,850
and maybe the Chamber of
Commerce to go after them.

113
00:06:30,850 --> 00:06:32,400
Get Mayor Lovett on the phone.

114
00:06:32,400 --> 00:06:34,850
- Sorry, the mayor's
busy on the other phone.

115
00:06:34,850 --> 00:06:35,913
- Yes, I know, Mrs. Brewster.

116
00:06:35,913 --> 00:06:38,180
It's a terrible reflection on our city.

117
00:06:38,180 --> 00:06:39,353
I've had a dozen calls already.

118
00:06:39,353 --> 00:06:40,780
- [Man] Spencer at the Chronicle.

119
00:06:40,780 --> 00:06:42,240
- Hold him.
- Just a minute.

120
00:06:42,240 --> 00:06:44,160
- Yes, Mrs. Brewster, I'm listening.

121
00:06:44,160 --> 00:06:46,820
- I insist that this John Doe man be found

122
00:06:46,820 --> 00:06:48,120
and given a job at once.

123
00:06:48,120 --> 00:06:49,620
Now, if something isn't done about it,

124
00:06:49,620 --> 00:06:51,295
I'll call out the whole auxiliary.

125
00:06:51,295 --> 00:06:53,302
and the junior auxiliary.

126
00:06:53,302 --> 00:06:55,281
We'll hold a meeting and see that--

127
00:06:55,281 --> 00:06:56,649
- Yes, Spency?

128
00:06:56,649 --> 00:06:57,580
Who?

129
00:06:57,580 --> 00:06:58,720
The governor?

130
00:06:58,720 --> 00:06:59,553
Well, what about me?

131
00:06:59,553 --> 00:07:01,150
It's my building he's jumping off of,

132
00:07:01,150 --> 00:07:02,319
and I'm up for reelection too.

133
00:07:02,319 --> 00:07:03,465
(shushing)
What are you doing?

134
00:07:03,465 --> 00:07:04,656
Get Connell at the Bulletin.

135
00:07:04,656 --> 00:07:06,620
Why, he's liable to go right past my--

136
00:07:06,620 --> 00:07:08,425
- Get me Connell.
- What was that?

137
00:07:08,425 --> 00:07:09,450
- What?
- Out the window.

138
00:07:09,450 --> 00:07:11,560
Something just flew by.
- I didn't see anything.

139
00:07:11,560 --> 00:07:13,390
- Well, don't stand there,
you idiot, go and look.

140
00:07:13,390 --> 00:07:14,223
Open the window.

141
00:07:15,300 --> 00:07:17,570
Oh, why did he have to
pick on my building?

142
00:07:17,570 --> 00:07:18,690
Is there a crowd in the street?

143
00:07:18,690 --> 00:07:19,523
- No, sir.

144
00:07:19,523 --> 00:07:21,630
- Then maybe he's caught
on a ledge, look again.

145
00:07:23,010 --> 00:07:24,600
- I think it must've been a seagull.

146
00:07:24,600 --> 00:07:25,520
- A seagull?

147
00:07:25,520 --> 00:07:28,410
What's a seagull doing
around the City Hall?

148
00:07:28,410 --> 00:07:29,630
That's a bad omen, isn't it?

149
00:07:29,630 --> 00:07:32,400
- Oh, no sir, the
seagull is a lovely bird.

150
00:07:32,400 --> 00:07:34,430
- It's all right, Mrs.
Brewster, it was just a seagull.

151
00:07:34,430 --> 00:07:36,270
- Connell?
- Nothing's happened yet.

152
00:07:36,270 --> 00:07:37,130
No, I'm watching.

153
00:07:37,130 --> 00:07:38,397
Don't worry, Mrs. Brewster.

154
00:07:38,397 --> 00:07:39,870
Just leave it all to me.

155
00:07:39,870 --> 00:07:41,990
Spencer, I'll call you back.

156
00:07:41,990 --> 00:07:44,014
Hello, Connell?

157
00:07:44,014 --> 00:07:44,847
This is--

158
00:07:44,847 --> 00:07:45,680
What are you doing?

159
00:07:45,680 --> 00:07:46,550
This is the mayor.

160
00:07:46,550 --> 00:07:47,780
- Yes, Mayor Lovett.

161
00:07:47,780 --> 00:07:49,320
How many times are you gonna call me?

162
00:07:49,320 --> 00:07:50,217
I've got everybody and his brother

163
00:07:50,217 --> 00:07:52,475
and sister out looking for him.

164
00:07:52,475 --> 00:07:54,075
Did you see the box I'm running?

165
00:07:56,050 --> 00:07:57,130
An appeal to John Doe.

166
00:07:57,130 --> 00:07:58,780
Think it over, John.

167
00:07:58,780 --> 00:08:01,130
Life can be beautiful, says mayor.

168
00:08:01,130 --> 00:08:03,140
If you need a job, apply
to editor to this paper,

169
00:08:03,140 --> 00:08:05,180
and so forth and so forth.

170
00:08:05,180 --> 00:08:08,050
Okay, Mayor, I'll let you know
as soon as I have something.

171
00:08:08,050 --> 00:08:08,883
What?

172
00:08:09,820 --> 00:08:11,519
Well, pull down the blinds.

173
00:08:11,519 --> 00:08:13,070
- Went up to Miss Mitchell's house, boss.

174
00:08:13,070 --> 00:08:14,383
Boy, she's in a bad way.
- Where is she?

175
00:08:14,383 --> 00:08:15,700
- Hey, do you know something?

176
00:08:15,700 --> 00:08:16,980
She supports her mother and two kids.

177
00:08:16,980 --> 00:08:17,975
What do you know about that?

178
00:08:17,975 --> 00:08:19,177
- Did you find her?
- No.

179
00:08:19,177 --> 00:08:20,610
Her mother's awful worried about her.

180
00:08:20,610 --> 00:08:21,443
When she left the house,

181
00:08:21,443 --> 00:08:23,170
said she was going on a roaring drunk.

182
00:08:23,170 --> 00:08:24,003
The girl, I mean.

183
00:08:24,003 --> 00:08:25,642
- Go out and find her.
- Sure.

184
00:08:25,642 --> 00:08:26,475
(phone ringing)

185
00:08:26,475 --> 00:08:27,770
Hey, but the biggest
thing I didn't tell you.

186
00:08:27,770 --> 00:08:28,912
Her old man's Doc Mitchell.
- Hello?

187
00:08:28,912 --> 00:08:30,024
- You know, the doc that
saved my mother's life

188
00:08:30,024 --> 00:08:31,230
and wouldn't take any money for it?

189
00:08:31,230 --> 00:08:32,330
You remember that?

190
00:08:32,330 --> 00:08:34,375
Okay, boss, I'll go and look for her.

191
00:08:34,375 --> 00:08:36,625
(clanging)

192
00:08:37,980 --> 00:08:40,940
- Holy smokes, Commissioner,
you've had 24 hours.

193
00:08:40,940 --> 00:08:44,543
Okay, Hawkshaw, grab a
pencil, here it is again.

194
00:08:45,580 --> 00:08:50,580
About five foot five, brown
eyes, light chestnut hair,

195
00:08:51,450 --> 00:08:55,953
and as fine a pair of legs as
ever walked into this office.

196
00:08:59,040 --> 00:08:59,873
- Did you wanna see me?

197
00:08:59,873 --> 00:09:03,142
- No, I've had the whole Army
and Navy out searching for you

198
00:09:03,142 --> 00:09:04,900
because that's a game
we play here every day.

199
00:09:04,900 --> 00:09:06,610
- I remember distinctly being fired.

200
00:09:06,610 --> 00:09:07,730
- That's right.

201
00:09:07,730 --> 00:09:08,770
But you have a piece of property

202
00:09:08,770 --> 00:09:10,260
that still belongs to this newspaper

203
00:09:10,260 --> 00:09:11,093
and I'd like to have it.

204
00:09:11,093 --> 00:09:11,926
- What's that?
- The letter.

205
00:09:11,926 --> 00:09:12,759
- What letter?

206
00:09:12,759 --> 00:09:14,710
- The letter from John Doe.
- Oh.

207
00:09:14,710 --> 00:09:16,260
- [Henry] The whole town's in an uproar.

208
00:09:16,260 --> 00:09:18,486
We've gotta find him and
the letter's the only clue.

209
00:09:18,486 --> 00:09:19,319
- There is no letter.

210
00:09:19,319 --> 00:09:20,897
- We'll get a handwriting expert to--

211
00:09:22,520 --> 00:09:24,707
What?
- There is no letter.

212
00:09:28,330 --> 00:09:29,163
- Say that again.

213
00:09:29,163 --> 00:09:30,513
- There's no letter, I made it up.

214
00:09:31,550 --> 00:09:32,383
- You made it up?
- Mm-hmm.

215
00:09:32,383 --> 00:09:34,928
You said you wanted fireworks.

216
00:09:34,928 --> 00:09:37,930
- Don't you know there are
nine jobs waiting for this guy,

217
00:09:37,930 --> 00:09:39,950
22 families wanna board him free,

218
00:09:39,950 --> 00:09:41,527
five women wanna marry him,

219
00:09:41,527 --> 00:09:43,660
and the mayor's practically
ready to adopt him?

220
00:09:43,660 --> 00:09:44,493
And you--

221
00:09:44,493 --> 00:09:45,326
- Just called the morgue, boss.

222
00:09:45,326 --> 00:09:46,200
They say there's a girl there--

223
00:09:46,200 --> 00:09:47,100
- [Henry] Shut up.

224
00:09:47,940 --> 00:09:48,830
- Ann.

225
00:09:48,830 --> 00:09:49,770
Say, why didn't you--
- Beany.

226
00:09:49,770 --> 00:09:51,440
- Only one thing to do, Hank,

227
00:09:51,440 --> 00:09:53,033
drop the whole business quickly.

228
00:09:53,033 --> 00:09:54,250
- How?
- Run a story.

229
00:09:54,250 --> 00:09:57,245
Say John Doe was in here and
he's sorry he wrote the letter.

230
00:09:57,245 --> 00:09:58,078
- That's right.

231
00:09:58,078 --> 00:09:58,955
You got it, sure.

232
00:09:58,955 --> 00:10:00,803
He came in here and I
let him change his mind.

233
00:10:00,803 --> 00:10:02,550
Bulletin editor saves John Doe's life.

234
00:10:02,550 --> 00:10:03,530
Why, it's perfect.

235
00:10:03,530 --> 00:10:05,090
I'll have Ned write it up.

236
00:10:05,090 --> 00:10:05,923
Oh, Ned?

237
00:10:05,923 --> 00:10:06,865
I've got a story I want you to--

238
00:10:06,865 --> 00:10:08,210
- Wait a minute.

239
00:10:08,210 --> 00:10:11,210
Listen, you great big wonderful
genius of a newspaper man.

240
00:10:11,210 --> 00:10:12,323
You came down here to shoot some life

241
00:10:12,323 --> 00:10:14,010
into this dying paper, didn't you?

242
00:10:14,010 --> 00:10:15,980
Well, the whole town's
curious about John Doe,

243
00:10:15,980 --> 00:10:18,100
and, boom, just like that,
you're going to bury him.

244
00:10:18,100 --> 00:10:19,100
There's enough circulation in that man

245
00:10:19,100 --> 00:10:21,210
to start a shortage in the ink market.

246
00:10:21,210 --> 00:10:22,690
- In what man?
- John Doe.

247
00:10:22,690 --> 00:10:23,523
- What John Doe?

248
00:10:23,523 --> 00:10:26,140
- Our John Doe, the one I made up.

249
00:10:26,140 --> 00:10:28,018
Look, genius, now look.

250
00:10:28,018 --> 00:10:31,010
Suppose there was a John Doe
and he walked into this office.

251
00:10:31,010 --> 00:10:31,970
What would you do?

252
00:10:31,970 --> 00:10:33,090
Find him a job and forget about

253
00:10:33,090 --> 00:10:34,700
the whole business, I suppose, huh?

254
00:10:34,700 --> 00:10:35,533
Not me.

255
00:10:35,533 --> 00:10:36,400
I'd make a deal.

256
00:10:36,400 --> 00:10:37,380
- A deal?

257
00:10:37,380 --> 00:10:39,270
- Sure, when you get ahold
of a stunt that sells papers,

258
00:10:39,270 --> 00:10:41,170
you don't drop it like a hot potato.

259
00:10:41,170 --> 00:10:42,640
Why, this is good for at
least a couple of months.

260
00:10:42,640 --> 00:10:43,710
You know what I'd do?

261
00:10:43,710 --> 00:10:45,980
Between now and let's say
Christmas when he's gonna jump,

262
00:10:45,980 --> 00:10:48,330
I'd run a daily yarn,
starting with his boyhood,

263
00:10:48,330 --> 00:10:50,030
his schooling, his first job.

264
00:10:50,030 --> 00:10:52,570
A wide-eyed youngster
facing a chaotic world.

265
00:10:52,570 --> 00:10:53,880
The problem of the average man,

266
00:10:53,880 --> 00:10:55,347
of all the John Does in the world.

267
00:10:55,347 --> 00:10:57,480
Now, then comes the drama.

268
00:10:57,480 --> 00:10:58,580
He meets discouragement.

269
00:10:58,580 --> 00:11:00,050
He finds the world has feet of clay.

270
00:11:00,050 --> 00:11:01,080
His ideals crumble.

271
00:11:01,080 --> 00:11:02,050
So what does he do?

272
00:11:02,050 --> 00:11:03,900
He decides to commit suicide in protest

273
00:11:03,900 --> 00:11:05,420
against the state of civilization.

274
00:11:05,420 --> 00:11:06,290
He thinks of the river.

275
00:11:06,290 --> 00:11:08,380
But no, no, he has a better idea.

276
00:11:08,380 --> 00:11:09,213
The City Hall.

277
00:11:09,213 --> 00:11:10,046
Why?

278
00:11:10,046 --> 00:11:11,310
Because he wants to attract attention.

279
00:11:11,310 --> 00:11:12,842
He wants to get a few
things off his chest,

280
00:11:12,842 --> 00:11:14,965
and that's the only way
he can get himself heard.

281
00:11:14,965 --> 00:11:16,140
- So?
- So?

282
00:11:16,140 --> 00:11:18,340
So he writes me a letter and I dig him up.

283
00:11:18,340 --> 00:11:20,120
He pours out his soul to me.

284
00:11:20,120 --> 00:11:23,200
And from now on, we quote,
I protest, by John Doe.

285
00:11:23,200 --> 00:11:25,650
He protests against all
the evils in the world,

286
00:11:25,650 --> 00:11:27,470
the greed, the lust, the hate, the fear,

287
00:11:27,470 --> 00:11:29,410
all of man's inhumanity to man.

288
00:11:29,410 --> 00:11:30,700
Arguments will start.

289
00:11:30,700 --> 00:11:32,387
Should he commit suicide or should he not?

290
00:11:32,387 --> 00:11:34,230
People will write in pleading with him.

291
00:11:34,230 --> 00:11:35,063
But no.

292
00:11:35,063 --> 00:11:35,896
No, sir.

293
00:11:35,896 --> 00:11:37,270
John Doe will remain adamant.

294
00:11:37,270 --> 00:11:40,287
On Christmas Eve, hot or cold, he goes.

295
00:11:40,287 --> 00:11:41,120
See?

296
00:11:43,160 --> 00:11:44,430
- Very pretty.

297
00:11:44,430 --> 00:11:46,330
Very pretty indeed, Miss Mitchell.

298
00:11:46,330 --> 00:11:49,100
But would you mind telling
me who goes on Christmas Eve?

299
00:11:49,100 --> 00:11:50,472
- John Doe.
- What John Doe?.

300
00:11:50,472 --> 00:11:52,628
- The one we hire for
the job, you lunkhead.

301
00:11:52,628 --> 00:11:53,939
- Wait a minute, wait a minute.

302
00:11:53,939 --> 00:11:56,260
Let me get this through
this lame brain of mine.

303
00:11:56,260 --> 00:11:58,050
Are you suggesting we
go out and hire someone

304
00:11:58,050 --> 00:11:59,680
to say he's gonna commit
suicide on Christmas Eve?

305
00:11:59,680 --> 00:12:01,518
Is that it?
- Why, you're catching on.

306
00:12:01,518 --> 00:12:03,210
- Who, for instance?
- Anybody.

307
00:12:03,210 --> 00:12:04,370
Beany'll do.

308
00:12:04,370 --> 00:12:05,940
- Why, sure--

309
00:12:05,940 --> 00:12:06,900
Who, me?

310
00:12:06,900 --> 00:12:08,030
Jump off a--

311
00:12:08,030 --> 00:12:08,863
Oh, no.

312
00:12:08,863 --> 00:12:10,270
Any time but Christmas, I'm superstitious.

313
00:12:10,270 --> 00:12:13,146
(clanging)

314
00:12:13,146 --> 00:12:15,739
(sighing)

315
00:12:15,739 --> 00:12:16,727
- Miss Mitchell, do me a favor, will you?

316
00:12:16,727 --> 00:12:19,120
Go on out and get married
and have a lotta babies,

317
00:12:19,120 --> 00:12:20,607
but stay outta newspaper business.

318
00:12:20,607 --> 00:12:23,410
- [Man] Better get that story
in, Hank, it's getting late.

319
00:12:23,410 --> 00:12:24,790
- You're supposed to be a smart guy.

320
00:12:24,790 --> 00:12:26,110
If it was raining $100 bills,

321
00:12:26,110 --> 00:12:29,060
you'd be out looking for
a dime you lost someplace.

322
00:12:29,060 --> 00:12:31,680
- Holy smokes, wasting my time
listening to this mad woman.

323
00:12:31,680 --> 00:12:32,513
- [Ned] Look, Chief.

324
00:12:32,513 --> 00:12:33,870
Look what the Chronicle's
running on John Doe.

325
00:12:33,870 --> 00:12:35,130
They say it's a fake.

326
00:12:35,130 --> 00:12:36,187
- Why, the no good--

327
00:12:37,730 --> 00:12:40,430
John Doe story amateur journalism.

328
00:12:40,430 --> 00:12:41,340
It's palpably phony.

329
00:12:41,340 --> 00:12:43,860
It's a wonder anyone
is taking it seriously.

330
00:12:43,860 --> 00:12:44,830
What do you think of those guys?

331
00:12:44,830 --> 00:12:46,180
- That's fine, that's fine.

332
00:12:46,180 --> 00:12:47,810
Now, fall right into their lap.

333
00:12:47,810 --> 00:12:49,790
Go ahead, say John Doe walked in

334
00:12:49,790 --> 00:12:50,880
and called the whole thing off.

335
00:12:50,880 --> 00:12:53,310
You know what that's gonna
sound like on top of this.

336
00:12:53,310 --> 00:12:54,379
- That's all, Ned, thank you.

337
00:12:54,379 --> 00:12:55,379
- All right.

338
00:12:56,840 --> 00:12:58,960
- Amateur journalism, huh?

339
00:12:58,960 --> 00:13:00,770
Why, that bunch of sophomores.

340
00:13:00,770 --> 00:13:02,330
I can teach them more about--

341
00:13:02,330 --> 00:13:04,300
- Hey, boss, get a load of this.

342
00:13:04,300 --> 00:13:05,467
- What?
- Look.

343
00:13:10,040 --> 00:13:10,873
- [Henry] What do they want?

344
00:13:10,873 --> 00:13:12,377
- [Beany] They all say they
wrote the John Doe letter.

345
00:13:12,377 --> 00:13:14,010
(talking over each other)

346
00:13:14,010 --> 00:13:15,470
- Oh, they all wrote the letter.

347
00:13:15,470 --> 00:13:16,534
- Tell them all to wait.

348
00:13:16,534 --> 00:13:19,100
Look, Mr. Connell, one of
those men is your John Doe.

349
00:13:19,100 --> 00:13:21,330
They're desperate and will do
anything for a cup of coffee.

350
00:13:21,330 --> 00:13:24,530
Pick one out and you can make
the chronicle eat their words.

351
00:13:24,530 --> 00:13:25,995
- I'm beginning to like this.

352
00:13:25,995 --> 00:13:29,040
- If you ask me, Hank, you're
playing around with dynamite.

353
00:13:29,040 --> 00:13:30,580
- No, no, no, no, no, the gal's right.

354
00:13:30,580 --> 00:13:32,550
We can't let the Chronicle
get the laugh on us.

355
00:13:32,550 --> 00:13:34,340
We've gotta produce a John Doe now.

356
00:13:34,340 --> 00:13:36,130
Amateur journalism, huh?

357
00:13:36,130 --> 00:13:37,120
I'll show those guys.

358
00:13:37,120 --> 00:13:38,380
- Sure, and there's no reason

359
00:13:38,380 --> 00:13:39,940
for them to find out the truth either

360
00:13:39,940 --> 00:13:42,253
because, naturally, I won't say anything.

361
00:13:44,360 --> 00:13:46,013
- Okay, sister, you get your job back.

362
00:13:46,013 --> 00:13:47,290
- Plus a bonus.

363
00:13:47,290 --> 00:13:48,123
- What bonus?

364
00:13:48,123 --> 00:13:50,670
- Oh, the bonus of $1,000 the
Chronicle was going to pay me

365
00:13:50,670 --> 00:13:52,040
for this little document.

366
00:13:52,040 --> 00:13:53,010
You'll find that it says that,

367
00:13:53,010 --> 00:13:54,460
I, Ann Mitchell, hereby certify

368
00:13:54,460 --> 00:13:56,278
that the John Doe letter
was created by me--

369
00:13:56,278 --> 00:13:57,660
- I can read, I can read.

370
00:13:57,660 --> 00:13:58,493
- Sorry.

371
00:14:00,480 --> 00:14:02,067
- You think this is worth $1,000, do you?

372
00:14:02,067 --> 00:14:05,170
- Oh, the Chronicle would
consider it dirt cheap.

373
00:14:05,170 --> 00:14:07,630
- Packs everything, including a gun.

374
00:14:07,630 --> 00:14:09,600
Okay, sister, you got yourself a deal.

375
00:14:09,600 --> 00:14:11,270
Now, let's take a look at the candidates.

376
00:14:11,270 --> 00:14:13,840
The one we pick has gotta
be the typical average man,

377
00:14:13,840 --> 00:14:15,664
typical American that
can keep his mouth shut.

378
00:14:15,664 --> 00:14:16,729
- Show me an American who can

379
00:14:16,729 --> 00:14:17,590
keep his mouth shut and I'll eat him.

380
00:14:20,945 --> 00:14:22,344
- Okay, Beany, bring 'em in one at a time.

381
00:14:22,344 --> 00:14:25,343
(angry chattering)

382
00:14:25,343 --> 00:14:28,926
(whimsical big band music)

383
00:15:26,453 --> 00:15:28,183
Did you write that
letter to Miss Mitchell?

384
00:15:29,060 --> 00:15:30,208
- No, I didn't.

385
00:15:30,208 --> 00:15:32,466
- What are you doin' up here then?

386
00:15:32,466 --> 00:15:34,410
- Well, the paper said there
were some jobs around loose.

387
00:15:34,410 --> 00:15:36,106
Thought there might be one left over.

388
00:15:36,106 --> 00:15:37,106
- Had any schooling?

389
00:15:38,060 --> 00:15:39,810
- Yeah, a little.

390
00:15:39,810 --> 00:15:41,640
- What do you do when you work?

391
00:15:41,640 --> 00:15:43,190
- I used to pitch.
- Baseball?

392
00:15:44,396 --> 00:15:46,650
- Yeah, till my wing went bad.

393
00:15:46,650 --> 00:15:47,600
- Where'd you play?

394
00:15:48,840 --> 00:15:50,500
- Bush leagues, mostly.

395
00:15:50,500 --> 00:15:52,570
- How about family, got any family?

396
00:15:52,570 --> 00:15:53,560
- No.

397
00:15:53,560 --> 00:15:55,170
- Oh, just traveling through, huh?

398
00:15:55,170 --> 00:15:57,210
- Yep, me and a friend of mine.

399
00:15:57,210 --> 00:15:58,073
He's outside.

400
00:16:02,760 --> 00:16:03,593
- Looks all right.

401
00:16:03,593 --> 00:16:05,164
- He's perfect, a baseball player.

402
00:16:05,164 --> 00:16:06,978
What could be more American?

403
00:16:06,978 --> 00:16:07,962
- [Henry] Wish he had a family though.

404
00:16:07,962 --> 00:16:10,186
- [Man] Be less complicated
without a family.

405
00:16:10,186 --> 00:16:12,000
- Look at that face, it's wonderful.

406
00:16:12,000 --> 00:16:12,833
They'll believe him.

407
00:16:12,833 --> 00:16:16,040
Come on.

408
00:16:16,040 --> 00:16:17,000
- [Henry] What's your name?

409
00:16:17,000 --> 00:16:18,740
- Willoughby, John Willoughby.

410
00:16:18,740 --> 00:16:21,140
Long John Willoughby they
called me in baseball.

411
00:16:22,813 --> 00:16:24,433
- Would you like to make some money?

412
00:16:26,100 --> 00:16:28,770
- Yeah, maybe.

413
00:16:28,770 --> 00:16:30,070
- Would you be willing to say you wrote

414
00:16:30,070 --> 00:16:32,443
that letter and stick by it?

415
00:16:37,430 --> 00:16:38,483
- I get the idea.

416
00:16:39,875 --> 00:16:41,713
Yeah, maybe.

417
00:16:46,459 --> 00:16:47,840
- That's our man, he's made to order.

418
00:16:47,840 --> 00:16:48,860
- [Henry] I don't know,

419
00:16:48,860 --> 00:16:49,880
he don't seem like the kind of a guy

420
00:16:49,880 --> 00:16:50,836
that would fall in line.

421
00:16:50,836 --> 00:16:51,669
- When you're desperate for money,

422
00:16:51,669 --> 00:16:53,150
you do a lotta things, Mr. Connell.

423
00:16:53,150 --> 00:16:55,165
He's our man, I tell you.

424
00:16:55,165 --> 00:16:56,248
He's fainted!

425
00:16:57,090 --> 00:16:58,230
Get some water, quickly.

426
00:16:58,230 --> 00:16:59,910
- [Henry] Hurry up, Pop.

427
00:16:59,910 --> 00:17:00,743
Why don't you sit down?

428
00:17:00,743 --> 00:17:02,340
- Are you all right?

429
00:17:02,340 --> 00:17:03,733
- Yeah, I'm all right.

430
00:17:04,613 --> 00:17:06,083
- How many is that, six?

431
00:17:16,550 --> 00:17:18,227
Pretty hungry, weren't you?

432
00:17:19,787 --> 00:17:21,280
- [Man] Say all this John Doe

433
00:17:21,280 --> 00:17:22,700
business is batty if you ask me.

434
00:17:22,700 --> 00:17:23,950
- Well, nobody asked you.

435
00:17:24,870 --> 00:17:27,550
- Trying to improve the world
by jumping off buildings.

436
00:17:27,550 --> 00:17:28,383
You couldn't improve the world

437
00:17:28,383 --> 00:17:30,023
if the buildings jumped on you.

438
00:17:30,890 --> 00:17:32,160
- Don't mind the Colonel, he hates people.

439
00:17:32,160 --> 00:17:35,010
- [Ann] He likes you well
enough to stick around.

440
00:17:35,010 --> 00:17:37,210
- Oh, that's 'cause we
both play doohickeys.

441
00:17:38,780 --> 00:17:40,690
I met him in a boxcar
a couple of years ago.

442
00:17:40,690 --> 00:17:41,800
I was fooling around with my harmonica,

443
00:17:41,800 --> 00:17:43,950
and he comes over and joins in.

444
00:17:43,950 --> 00:17:45,900
I haven't been able to shake him since.

445
00:17:47,248 --> 00:17:50,081
(harmonica music)

446
00:17:51,685 --> 00:17:56,685
("William Tell Overture" by
Gioachino Rossini on harmonicas)

447
00:18:07,690 --> 00:18:08,610
- All right, boys, here he is.

448
00:18:08,610 --> 00:18:10,870
- No, no, no, you can't take
pictures of him like that,

449
00:18:10,870 --> 00:18:12,733
eating a sandwich and with a beard.

450
00:18:12,733 --> 00:18:13,566
- But he's gonna jump off a building.

451
00:18:13,566 --> 00:18:15,220
- Yes, but not because he's out of a job.

452
00:18:15,220 --> 00:18:16,053
That's not news.

453
00:18:16,053 --> 00:18:18,410
This man's going to jump
as a matter of principle.

454
00:18:18,410 --> 00:18:19,243
- Maybe you're right.
- We'll clean him up

455
00:18:19,243 --> 00:18:20,720
and put him a hotel
room, under bodyguards.

456
00:18:20,720 --> 00:18:22,250
We'll make a mystery out of him.

457
00:18:22,250 --> 00:18:23,300
- Do you speak to Mr. Norton?

458
00:18:23,300 --> 00:18:24,160
- Thinks it's terrific.

459
00:18:24,160 --> 00:18:25,250
Says for us to go the limit.

460
00:18:25,250 --> 00:18:26,083
Wants us to build a bonfire

461
00:18:26,083 --> 00:18:27,995
under every big shot in the state.

462
00:18:27,995 --> 00:18:29,120
- Oh, swell, is that the contract?

463
00:18:29,120 --> 00:18:30,182
- Yes.

464
00:18:30,182 --> 00:18:31,786
What's he doing here?
- Friend of his.

465
00:18:31,786 --> 00:18:33,592
They play duets together.

466
00:18:33,592 --> 00:18:34,425
- Duets?

467
00:18:34,425 --> 00:18:35,258
But can we trust him?

468
00:18:35,258 --> 00:18:36,800
- Oh.
- I trust him.

469
00:18:36,800 --> 00:18:37,810
- Oh, you trust him, eh?

470
00:18:37,810 --> 00:18:38,840
Well, that's fine.

471
00:18:38,840 --> 00:18:39,910
I suppose he trusts you, too?

472
00:18:39,910 --> 00:18:40,978
- [Ann] Oh, stop worrying.

473
00:18:40,978 --> 00:18:41,811
He's all right.

474
00:18:41,811 --> 00:18:43,120
- Well, okay, but we don't want more

475
00:18:43,120 --> 00:18:45,142
than a couple of hundred
people in on this thing.

476
00:18:45,142 --> 00:18:47,160
Now, the first thing I
want is an exact copy

477
00:18:47,160 --> 00:18:49,170
of the John Doe letter
in your own handwriting.

478
00:18:49,170 --> 00:18:50,904
- I got it all ready, here.

479
00:18:50,904 --> 00:18:52,650
- Now, that's fine.

480
00:18:52,650 --> 00:18:54,910
Now, I want you to sign this agreement.

481
00:18:54,910 --> 00:18:57,500
It gives us an exclusive
story under your name

482
00:18:57,500 --> 00:18:59,663
day by day from now until Christmas.

483
00:19:00,520 --> 00:19:04,570
On December 26th you get one
railroad ticket out of town,

484
00:19:04,570 --> 00:19:07,120
and the Bulletin agrees to
pay to have your arm fixed.

485
00:19:07,120 --> 00:19:08,025
That's what you want, isn't it?

486
00:19:08,025 --> 00:19:09,890
- Yeah, but it's got to
be by Bone-Setter Brown.

487
00:19:09,890 --> 00:19:11,750
- Okay, Bone-Setter Brown goes.

488
00:19:11,750 --> 00:19:12,980
Here, sign it.

489
00:19:12,980 --> 00:19:15,053
Meanwhile, here's $50 for spending money.

490
00:19:16,110 --> 00:19:17,050
That's fine.

491
00:19:17,050 --> 00:19:18,130
Beany?
- Yes, boss?

492
00:19:18,130 --> 00:19:19,087
- Take charge of him.

493
00:19:19,087 --> 00:19:21,470
Get him a suite at the Imperial
and hire some bodyguards.

494
00:19:21,470 --> 00:19:23,137
- Yeah, and some new clothes, Beany.

495
00:19:23,137 --> 00:19:24,630
- [Beany] Do you think we
better have him deloused?

496
00:19:24,630 --> 00:19:25,760
- [Henry] Yeah, yeah, yeah.

497
00:19:25,760 --> 00:19:26,593
- [Beany] Both of 'em?

498
00:19:26,593 --> 00:19:27,426
- [Henry] Yes, both of 'em.

499
00:19:27,426 --> 00:19:28,800
But don't let 'em out of your sight.

500
00:19:28,800 --> 00:19:30,170
- Hey, Beany, gray suit, huh?

501
00:19:30,170 --> 00:19:31,003
- Yeah.

502
00:19:31,003 --> 00:19:31,836
- Okay, fellas.

503
00:19:36,660 --> 00:19:39,310
And you, start pounding that typewriter.

504
00:19:39,310 --> 00:19:40,970
Oh boy, this is terrific.

505
00:19:40,970 --> 00:19:42,290
No responsibilities on our part.

506
00:19:42,290 --> 00:19:43,580
Just statements from John Doe

507
00:19:43,580 --> 00:19:44,477
and we can blast our heads off.

508
00:19:44,477 --> 00:19:46,130
- Before you pop too many buttons,

509
00:19:46,130 --> 00:19:49,080
don't forget to make out
that check for 1,000.

510
00:19:49,080 --> 00:19:51,330
(groaning)

511
00:19:52,393 --> 00:19:54,250
- Okay fellas, now let me see.

512
00:19:54,250 --> 00:19:55,220
You sit outside the door.

513
00:19:55,220 --> 00:19:56,303
Nobody comes in, see.

514
00:19:56,303 --> 00:19:57,810
You two fellas sit in here.

515
00:19:57,810 --> 00:19:59,270
- Hey, pretty nifty, huh?

516
00:20:01,220 --> 00:20:02,690
- You ain't gonna get me to stay here.

517
00:20:02,690 --> 00:20:03,523
- [Beany] Sure you are.

518
00:20:03,523 --> 00:20:04,960
- No sir, that spot under the bridge

519
00:20:04,960 --> 00:20:07,870
where we slept last
night's good enough for me.

520
00:20:07,870 --> 00:20:09,280
- Hey, what do I do with this baggage?

521
00:20:09,280 --> 00:20:10,113
- [Beany] Oh, stick 'em in the bedroom.

522
00:20:10,113 --> 00:20:11,490
- Give me mine, I ain't stayin'.

523
00:20:13,270 --> 00:20:15,180
You know, we were headed for
the Columbia River country

524
00:20:15,180 --> 00:20:17,260
before all this John Doe business came up.

525
00:20:17,260 --> 00:20:18,400
You remember that, don't you?

526
00:20:18,400 --> 00:20:19,760
- Sure, I remember.

527
00:20:19,760 --> 00:20:21,499
- Say, did your ears pop
coming up in the elevator?

528
00:20:21,499 --> 00:20:22,332
Mine did.

529
00:20:22,332 --> 00:20:23,754
- Oh, Long John, I tell ya, it's no good.

530
00:20:23,754 --> 00:20:24,987
You're gonna get used to a lotta stuff

531
00:20:24,987 --> 00:20:26,420
that's gonna wreck you.

532
00:20:26,420 --> 00:20:27,840
Why, that $50 in your pocket's

533
00:20:27,840 --> 00:20:29,046
beginning to show up on you already.

534
00:20:29,046 --> 00:20:29,879
(harmonica music)

535
00:20:29,879 --> 00:20:31,540
And don't pull that on me neither.

536
00:20:31,540 --> 00:20:32,373
- Stop worrying, Colonel.

537
00:20:32,373 --> 00:20:34,000
I'm gonna get my arm fixed outta this.

538
00:20:34,000 --> 00:20:36,310
- Hey, here's some
cigars the boss sent us.

539
00:20:36,310 --> 00:20:37,160
Have one.
- Hey, cigars.

540
00:20:37,160 --> 00:20:38,310
- Help yourself.
- No.

541
00:20:48,270 --> 00:20:49,650
- Hey, I'll bet ya even the Major Leaguers

542
00:20:49,650 --> 00:20:51,170
don't rate an outfit like this.

543
00:20:51,170 --> 00:20:52,490
- [Man] Here, make yourself comfortable.

544
00:20:52,490 --> 00:20:54,003
Paper?

545
00:20:54,003 --> 00:20:55,016
- No, I don't read no papers

546
00:20:55,016 --> 00:20:57,090
and I don't listen to radios either.

547
00:20:57,090 --> 00:20:59,030
I know the world's been
shaved by a drunken barber

548
00:20:59,030 --> 00:21:00,430
and I don't have to read it.

549
00:21:03,515 --> 00:21:05,840
I've seen guys like you go under before.

550
00:21:05,840 --> 00:21:07,450
Guys that never had a worry,

551
00:21:07,450 --> 00:21:09,360
then they got ahold of
some dough and went goofy.

552
00:21:09,360 --> 00:21:10,556
The first thing that happens to a guy--

553
00:21:10,556 --> 00:21:11,950
- [Beany] Hey, did you
get a load of the bedroom?

554
00:21:11,950 --> 00:21:12,783
- No.

555
00:21:13,790 --> 00:21:15,660
- The first thing that
happens to a guy like that,

556
00:21:15,660 --> 00:21:17,037
he starts wanting to go into restaurants

557
00:21:17,037 --> 00:21:22,037
and sit down at a table and eat
salads and cupcakes and tea.

558
00:21:22,140 --> 00:21:24,643
Boy, what that kinda
food does to your system.

559
00:21:29,420 --> 00:21:31,730
The next thing the dope wants is a room.

560
00:21:31,730 --> 00:21:35,244
Yes sir, a room with steam
heat, and curtains and rugs.

561
00:21:35,244 --> 00:21:36,557
And before you know it,
he's all softened up

562
00:21:36,557 --> 00:21:39,350
and he can't sleep unless he has a bed.

563
00:21:39,350 --> 00:21:40,370
- Hey, stop worrying, Colonel.

564
00:21:40,370 --> 00:21:41,820
50 bucks ain't gonna ruin me.

565
00:21:41,820 --> 00:21:44,140
- I've seen plenty of fellas
start out with 50 bucks

566
00:21:44,140 --> 00:21:45,750
and wind up with a bank account.

567
00:21:45,750 --> 00:21:47,610
- Hey, what's a matter
with a bank account anyway?

568
00:21:47,610 --> 00:21:49,380
- You let me tell you, Long John,

569
00:21:49,380 --> 00:21:53,100
when you become a guy with
a bank account, they gotcha.

570
00:21:53,100 --> 00:21:54,687
Yes sir, they gotcha.

571
00:21:56,340 --> 00:21:57,173
- Who's got him?

572
00:21:57,173 --> 00:21:58,443
- The heelots.

573
00:21:58,443 --> 00:21:59,276
- Who?

574
00:21:59,276 --> 00:22:01,050
- Hey, there's the City Hall tower

575
00:22:01,050 --> 00:22:02,373
I'm supposed to jump off of.

576
00:22:02,373 --> 00:22:04,150
It's even higher than this.

577
00:22:04,150 --> 00:22:05,237
- Who's got him?

578
00:22:05,237 --> 00:22:06,959
- The heelots.

579
00:22:06,959 --> 00:22:08,390
- [John Doe] Woo.

580
00:22:08,390 --> 00:22:10,390
- Hey, wait a minute.

581
00:22:10,390 --> 00:22:11,953
You're not supposed to
jump till Christmas Eve.

582
00:22:11,953 --> 00:22:14,088
Do you wanna get me in a jam?.

583
00:22:14,088 --> 00:22:16,210
- If it's gonna get you in
a jam, I'll do you a favor.

584
00:22:16,210 --> 00:22:17,043
I won't jump.

585
00:22:18,790 --> 00:22:20,013
- And when they got you,

586
00:22:20,013 --> 00:22:22,530
you got no more chance than a road rabbit.

587
00:22:22,530 --> 00:22:24,232
- Hey, who'd you say was gonna get him?

588
00:22:24,232 --> 00:22:25,170
- Say, is this one of those places

589
00:22:25,170 --> 00:22:26,460
where you ring if you want something?

590
00:22:26,460 --> 00:22:28,500
- Yeah, yeah, just use the phone.

591
00:22:28,500 --> 00:22:30,260
- Boy, I've always wanted to do this.

592
00:22:30,260 --> 00:22:31,470
- Hey, Doc, look.

593
00:22:31,470 --> 00:22:32,303
Look, Doc.

594
00:22:33,170 --> 00:22:35,040
Gimme that again, will ya?

595
00:22:35,040 --> 00:22:36,403
Who's gonna get him?

596
00:22:36,403 --> 00:22:38,260
- The heelots.

597
00:22:38,260 --> 00:22:39,700
- Who are they?

598
00:22:39,700 --> 00:22:41,340
- Listen, sucker, you ever been broke?

599
00:22:41,340 --> 00:22:43,267
- Sure, mostly often.
- All right.

600
00:22:43,267 --> 00:22:46,100
You're walking along, not
a nickel in your jeans,

601
00:22:46,100 --> 00:22:47,930
you're free as the wind,
nobody bothers you.

602
00:22:47,930 --> 00:22:50,390
Hundreds of people pass you
by in every line of business,

603
00:22:50,390 --> 00:22:53,293
shoes, hats, automobiles,
radios, furniture, everything.

604
00:22:53,293 --> 00:22:55,980
They're all nice lovable
people and they let you alone.

605
00:22:55,980 --> 00:22:57,393
Is that right?

606
00:22:58,536 --> 00:23:01,036
Then you get ahold of some
dough and what happens?

607
00:23:01,990 --> 00:23:06,010
All those nice, sweet,
lovable people become heelots.

608
00:23:06,010 --> 00:23:07,463
A lotta heels.

609
00:23:08,720 --> 00:23:12,646
They begin creeping up on you,
trying to sell you something.

610
00:23:12,646 --> 00:23:16,247
They got long claws and they
get a stranglehold on you.

611
00:23:16,247 --> 00:23:18,460
And you squirm and you duck and you holler

612
00:23:18,460 --> 00:23:19,390
and you try to push 'em away,

613
00:23:19,390 --> 00:23:21,910
but you haven't got a
chance, they've got you.

614
00:23:21,910 --> 00:23:23,870
First thing you know, you own things.

615
00:23:23,870 --> 00:23:25,730
A car, for instance.

616
00:23:25,730 --> 00:23:27,100
Now your whole life is messed up

617
00:23:27,100 --> 00:23:28,780
with a lot more stuff.

618
00:23:28,780 --> 00:23:31,530
You got license fees and number plates

619
00:23:31,530 --> 00:23:34,910
and gas and oil and taxes and insurance

620
00:23:34,910 --> 00:23:37,150
and identification cards and letters

621
00:23:37,150 --> 00:23:39,970
and bills and flat tires and
dents and traffic tickets

622
00:23:39,970 --> 00:23:43,140
and motorcycle cops and
courtrooms and lawyers and fines

623
00:23:43,140 --> 00:23:45,938
and a-million-and-one other things.

624
00:23:45,938 --> 00:23:46,838
Then what happens?

625
00:23:48,500 --> 00:23:51,240
You're not the free and
happy guy you used to be.

626
00:23:51,240 --> 00:23:54,100
You gotta have money to
pay for all those things.

627
00:23:54,100 --> 00:23:56,990
So, you go after what
the other fella's got.

628
00:23:56,990 --> 00:23:59,863
And there you are,
you're a heelot yourself.

629
00:24:07,530 --> 00:24:09,433
- You win, Colonel, here, take the 50.

630
00:24:10,972 --> 00:24:11,920
Go out and get rid of it.

631
00:24:11,920 --> 00:24:14,305
- You bet I will, just as fast as I can.

632
00:24:14,305 --> 00:24:16,310
I'm gonna get some canned
goods, a fishing rod,

633
00:24:16,310 --> 00:24:18,620
and the rest I'm gonna give away.

634
00:24:18,620 --> 00:24:20,030
- Give away?

635
00:24:20,030 --> 00:24:21,570
- Hey, get me a pitcher's glove.

636
00:24:21,570 --> 00:24:23,820
I gotta get me some practice.

637
00:24:23,820 --> 00:24:26,260
- Say, he's giving it away,
I'm gonna get me some of that.

638
00:24:26,260 --> 00:24:28,147
- Hey, come back here, you heelot.

639
00:24:30,863 --> 00:24:34,840
- Will you send up five
hamburgers with all the trimmings,

640
00:24:34,840 --> 00:24:37,620
five chocolate ice cream sodas,

641
00:24:37,620 --> 00:24:39,070
and five pieces of apple pie?

642
00:24:39,990 --> 00:24:41,893
No, apple, with cheese.

643
00:24:42,920 --> 00:24:43,980
Yeah, thanks.

644
00:24:57,790 --> 00:24:58,643
- Hello there.

645
00:25:01,304 --> 00:25:05,170
Well, if it isn't the man about town.

646
00:25:05,170 --> 00:25:06,130
- All set, Miss Ann?

647
00:25:06,130 --> 00:25:06,963
- Hmm?

648
00:25:08,040 --> 00:25:10,503
Oh, oh yes, let's go.

649
00:25:11,850 --> 00:25:13,720
Let's see, we want some
action in these pictures.

650
00:25:13,720 --> 00:25:14,913
- Action?
- Mm-hmm.

651
00:25:17,476 --> 00:25:18,450
- That's good.
- No, no, no.

652
00:25:18,450 --> 00:25:20,780
This man's going to jump off a roof.

653
00:25:20,780 --> 00:25:23,090
Here, wait a minute,
let me comb your hair.

654
00:25:23,090 --> 00:25:23,923
Sit down.

655
00:25:28,330 --> 00:25:29,823
There, that's better.

656
00:25:37,297 --> 00:25:39,670
You know, he's got a nice face, hasn't he?

657
00:25:39,670 --> 00:25:42,994
- Yeah, he's pretty. (laughing)

658
00:25:42,994 --> 00:25:44,250
- Here, sit down.

659
00:25:44,250 --> 00:25:45,373
Quiet, egghead.

660
00:25:46,360 --> 00:25:48,160
All right, now a serious expression.

661
00:25:49,060 --> 00:25:49,893
- I can't, I'm feeling too good.

662
00:25:49,893 --> 00:25:51,490
- [Ann] Oh, come on now, this is serious.

663
00:25:51,490 --> 00:25:54,230
You're a man disgusted
with all of civilization.

664
00:25:54,230 --> 00:25:55,080
- With all of it?

665
00:25:55,080 --> 00:25:57,830
- [Ann] Yes, you're sore
at the world, come on now.

666
00:25:57,830 --> 00:25:59,380
- Oh, crabby guy, huh?
- Yeah.

667
00:26:02,430 --> 00:26:03,263
No, no.

668
00:26:05,788 --> 00:26:09,330
No. (laughing)

669
00:26:09,330 --> 00:26:10,320
No, look.

670
00:26:10,320 --> 00:26:12,508
You don't have to smell the world.

671
00:26:12,508 --> 00:26:14,424
(laughing)

672
00:26:14,424 --> 00:26:15,360
- With all those guys
in the bleachers think--

673
00:26:15,360 --> 00:26:16,620
- Nevermind those guys.

674
00:26:16,620 --> 00:26:18,080
All right, stand up.

675
00:26:18,080 --> 00:26:20,970
Now, let's see what you
look like when you protest.

676
00:26:20,970 --> 00:26:21,950
- Against what?

677
00:26:21,950 --> 00:26:23,733
- [Ann] Against anything, just protest.

678
00:26:25,450 --> 00:26:26,557
- You got me.

679
00:26:26,557 --> 00:26:28,520
- Oh, look, I'm the umpire

680
00:26:28,520 --> 00:26:30,910
and you just cut the heart of
the plate with your fast one

681
00:26:30,910 --> 00:26:31,760
and I called it a ball.

682
00:26:31,760 --> 00:26:33,130
What would you do?

683
00:26:33,130 --> 00:26:34,090
- Oh you did, huh?

684
00:26:34,090 --> 00:26:35,040
- [Ann] Yeah.

685
00:26:35,040 --> 00:26:37,057
- Why can't you call 'em right,
you boneheaded big-eared--

686
00:26:37,057 --> 00:26:39,808
- [Ann] Grab it, Eddie, grab it.

687
00:26:39,808 --> 00:26:42,558
(big band music)

688
00:26:46,146 --> 00:26:48,729
- [Newspaper Man] Extra, extra!

689
00:27:01,410 --> 00:27:02,796
(chattering)

690
00:27:02,796 --> 00:27:03,629
Extra.

691
00:27:04,901 --> 00:27:05,984
Extra, extra!

692
00:27:24,103 --> 00:27:26,520
(chattering)

693
00:27:31,710 --> 00:27:34,390
- I don't care whose
picture they're publishing.

694
00:27:34,390 --> 00:27:37,650
I still say that this
John Doe person is a myth,

695
00:27:37,650 --> 00:27:39,850
and you can quote me on that.

696
00:27:39,850 --> 00:27:40,810
And I'm going to insist

697
00:27:40,810 --> 00:27:43,200
on his being produced for questioning.

698
00:27:43,200 --> 00:27:45,990
You know as well as I
do that this whole thing

699
00:27:45,990 --> 00:27:48,740
is being engineered by a vicious man

700
00:27:48,740 --> 00:27:52,741
with a vicious purpose, Mr. D.B. Norton.

701
00:27:52,741 --> 00:27:54,513
(whistle blowing)

702
00:27:54,513 --> 00:27:57,346
(engines revving)

703
00:28:05,363 --> 00:28:08,196
(whistle blowing)

704
00:28:15,496 --> 00:28:17,170
- Mr. Connell and Miss
Mitchell are at the house, sir.

705
00:28:17,170 --> 00:28:18,180
- Oh, they are?

706
00:28:18,180 --> 00:28:19,413
All right, come on.

707
00:28:21,560 --> 00:28:22,540
- Personally, I think it's just

708
00:28:22,540 --> 00:28:23,900
plain stupidity to drop it now.

709
00:28:23,900 --> 00:28:25,497
You should see his fan mail, thousands.

710
00:28:25,497 --> 00:28:27,990
Why, it's going over like a house afire.

711
00:28:27,990 --> 00:28:29,470
- What are you afraid of, Connell?

712
00:28:29,470 --> 00:28:30,900
It's doubled our circulation.

713
00:28:30,900 --> 00:28:32,530
- Yeah, but it's got everybody sore.

714
00:28:32,530 --> 00:28:33,740
Ads are being pulled.

715
00:28:33,740 --> 00:28:35,300
The governor's starting a libel suit.

716
00:28:35,300 --> 00:28:37,253
What's more, they all
know John Doe's a phony,

717
00:28:37,253 --> 00:28:38,440
and they insist on seeing him.
- Well, what about it?

718
00:28:38,440 --> 00:28:39,293
Let them see him.

719
00:28:39,293 --> 00:28:40,996
We'll go 'em one better,
they can also hear him.

720
00:28:40,996 --> 00:28:41,829
You own a radio station, Mr. Norton.

721
00:28:41,829 --> 00:28:42,770
Why not put him on the air?

722
00:28:42,770 --> 00:28:44,293
- Watch out for this dame,
D.B., she'll drive you batty.

723
00:28:44,293 --> 00:28:45,760
- Oh--
- Look, we can't let 'em

724
00:28:45,760 --> 00:28:47,710
get to this bush-league
pitcher and start pumping him.

725
00:28:47,710 --> 00:28:48,620
Good night.

726
00:28:48,620 --> 00:28:50,700
No telling what that screwball might do.

727
00:28:50,700 --> 00:28:52,220
I walked in yesterday, here he is,

728
00:28:52,220 --> 00:28:55,510
standing on a table with a
fishing pole, fly-casting.

729
00:28:55,510 --> 00:28:56,900
Take my advice and get him outta town

730
00:28:56,900 --> 00:28:57,733
before this thing explodes in our faces.

731
00:28:57,733 --> 00:28:58,840
- If you do, Mr. Norton,

732
00:28:58,840 --> 00:29:00,680
you're just as much of
a dumb cluck as he is.

733
00:29:00,680 --> 00:29:01,513
Excuse me.

734
00:29:01,513 --> 00:29:02,346
- No, you've got yourself a meal ticket

735
00:29:02,346 --> 00:29:03,179
and you hate to let go.

736
00:29:03,179 --> 00:29:04,330
- Sure, it's a meal
ticket for me, I admit it.

737
00:29:04,330 --> 00:29:06,370
But it's also a windfall
for somebody like Mr. Norton

738
00:29:06,370 --> 00:29:08,130
who's trying to crash national politics.

739
00:29:08,130 --> 00:29:09,610
That's what you bought the
newspaper for, isn't it?

740
00:29:09,610 --> 00:29:11,140
You wanna reach a lotta people, don't you?

741
00:29:11,140 --> 00:29:12,260
Well, put John Doe on the air

742
00:29:12,260 --> 00:29:14,025
and you can reach 130 million of 'em.

743
00:29:14,025 --> 00:29:16,250
He can say anything he wants
and they'll listen to him.

744
00:29:16,250 --> 00:29:17,990
All right, we'll forget
the Governor and the Mayor

745
00:29:17,990 --> 00:29:19,560
and all the small fry like that.

746
00:29:19,560 --> 00:29:21,170
This can arouse national interest.

747
00:29:21,170 --> 00:29:22,003
If he made a hit around here,

748
00:29:22,003 --> 00:29:24,350
he can do it everyplace
else in the country.

749
00:29:24,350 --> 00:29:27,100
And you'll be pulling
the strings, Mr. Norton.

750
00:29:29,978 --> 00:29:32,038
- Go down to the office and
arrange for some radio time.

751
00:29:32,038 --> 00:29:32,871
- Why, D.B., you're not gonna fall--

752
00:29:32,871 --> 00:29:35,538
- I want it as soon as possible.

753
00:29:37,970 --> 00:29:38,830
- Okay.

754
00:29:38,830 --> 00:29:41,160
I just came in to get warm, myself.

755
00:29:41,160 --> 00:29:42,113
Come on, let's go.

756
00:29:43,070 --> 00:29:44,200
- Don't you go.

757
00:29:44,200 --> 00:29:45,200
I wanna talk to you.

758
00:29:52,140 --> 00:29:52,973
Sit down.

759
00:30:10,320 --> 00:30:13,672
Hey, this John Doe idea was yours, huh?

760
00:30:13,672 --> 00:30:14,505
- Yes sir.

761
00:30:20,947 --> 00:30:22,545
- How much money do you get?

762
00:30:22,545 --> 00:30:23,378
- $30.

763
00:30:24,320 --> 00:30:25,153
- $30.

764
00:30:26,480 --> 00:30:28,240
Well then, what are you after?

765
00:30:28,240 --> 00:30:31,459
I mean what do you want,
a journalistic career?

766
00:30:31,459 --> 00:30:32,292
- Money.

767
00:30:33,310 --> 00:30:34,221
- Money.

768
00:30:34,221 --> 00:30:36,473
Well, I'm glad to hear somebody admit it.

769
00:30:39,759 --> 00:30:42,472
Do you suppose you could
write a radio speech

770
00:30:42,472 --> 00:30:44,185
that would put that fellow over?

771
00:30:44,185 --> 00:30:45,018
- [Ann] Oh, I'm sure I can.

772
00:30:45,018 --> 00:30:47,367
- Do it, and I'll give you $100 a week.

773
00:30:47,367 --> 00:30:48,200
- $100--

774
00:30:48,200 --> 00:30:49,820
- That's only the beginning.

775
00:30:49,820 --> 00:30:50,653
You play your cards right

776
00:30:50,653 --> 00:30:53,630
and you'll never have to
worry about money again.

777
00:30:53,630 --> 00:30:54,617
Oh, I knew it.

778
00:30:54,617 --> 00:30:55,590
- Hello.
- Hello.

779
00:30:55,590 --> 00:30:57,980
Whenever there's a pretty woman around--

780
00:30:57,980 --> 00:30:59,980
This is my nephew, Ted
Sheldon, Miss Mitchell.

781
00:30:59,980 --> 00:31:01,260
- How do you do?
- How do you do?

782
00:31:01,260 --> 00:31:02,740
- All right, Casanova,
I'll give you a break.

783
00:31:02,740 --> 00:31:05,410
See that Miss Mitchell gets
a car to take her home.

784
00:31:05,410 --> 00:31:07,860
- Always reading my mind, aren't you?

785
00:31:07,860 --> 00:31:09,633
- Thank you very much for everything.

786
00:31:10,561 --> 00:31:12,410
- And, Miss Mitchell,

787
00:31:12,410 --> 00:31:15,540
I think from now on you'd
better work directly with me.

788
00:31:15,540 --> 00:31:16,373
- Yes, sir.

789
00:31:25,653 --> 00:31:30,653
(upbeat orchestral music)
(typewriter keys clacking)

790
00:31:37,485 --> 00:31:40,113
(groaning)

791
00:31:40,113 --> 00:31:43,030
(orchestral music)

792
00:31:50,638 --> 00:31:51,471
Hey!

793
00:31:52,539 --> 00:31:53,641
I thought you were asleep.

794
00:31:53,641 --> 00:31:54,474
- [Girl] We just wanted to say good night.

795
00:31:54,474 --> 00:31:55,457
- [Girl] Goodnight sis.

796
00:31:55,457 --> 00:31:56,299
(groaning)

797
00:31:56,299 --> 00:31:58,556
- Oh, you little brats,
you're just stalling.

798
00:31:58,556 --> 00:31:59,973
I said goodnight.

799
00:32:00,900 --> 00:32:01,960
- Come, come, come, children.

800
00:32:01,960 --> 00:32:03,066
It's past your bedtime.

801
00:32:03,066 --> 00:32:04,587
Go on, go on.
(children fussing)

802
00:32:04,587 --> 00:32:07,586
- Come on, pooch, come on.
- Come on, come on.

803
00:32:07,586 --> 00:32:08,419
- [Woman] Now scooch off to bed.

804
00:32:08,419 --> 00:32:10,070
- Stick a fork through me, I'm done.

805
00:32:10,070 --> 00:32:11,570
I'll never get this speech right.

806
00:32:11,570 --> 00:32:13,020
- Oh, yes you will, Ann, dear.

807
00:32:13,020 --> 00:32:14,323
You're very clever.

808
00:32:14,323 --> 00:32:15,630
- Yeah, I know.

809
00:32:15,630 --> 00:32:16,738
What are you looking for?

810
00:32:16,738 --> 00:32:18,900
- Your purse, I need $10.
- What for?

811
00:32:18,900 --> 00:32:20,880
I gave you $50 just the other day.

812
00:32:20,880 --> 00:32:23,320
- Yes, I know, dear, but Mrs.
Burke had her baby yesterday.

813
00:32:23,320 --> 00:32:24,831
Nine pounds.

814
00:32:24,831 --> 00:32:26,610
And there wasn't a thing in the house.

815
00:32:26,610 --> 00:32:28,069
And then the Community Chest lady came--

816
00:32:28,069 --> 00:32:29,439
- And 50's all gone, huh?

817
00:32:29,439 --> 00:32:30,740
Who is the 10 for?

818
00:32:30,740 --> 00:32:32,160
- The Websters.
- The Websters.

819
00:32:32,160 --> 00:32:33,410
- You remember, those lovely people

820
00:32:33,410 --> 00:32:35,160
your father used to take care of?

821
00:32:35,160 --> 00:32:37,240
I thought I'd buy them some groceries.

822
00:32:37,240 --> 00:32:39,090
Oh, Ann, dear, it's a shame, those poor--

823
00:32:39,090 --> 00:32:40,140
- You're marvelous, Ma.

824
00:32:40,140 --> 00:32:41,800
You're just like Father used to be.

825
00:32:41,800 --> 00:32:43,430
Do you realize a couple of weeks ago

826
00:32:43,430 --> 00:32:45,130
we didn't have enough to eat ourselves?

827
00:32:45,130 --> 00:32:48,260
- Well, yes, I know, dear, but
these people are in such need

828
00:32:48,260 --> 00:32:49,120
and we have plenty now.

829
00:32:49,120 --> 00:32:50,710
- If you're thinking of
that $1,000, forget it.

830
00:32:50,710 --> 00:32:51,830
It's practically all gone.

831
00:32:51,830 --> 00:32:53,080
We owed everybody in town.

832
00:32:53,080 --> 00:32:55,880
Now, you just gotta stop
giving all your money away.

833
00:32:55,880 --> 00:32:57,420
- Oh, Ann, dear.

834
00:32:57,420 --> 00:32:58,830
- Oh, I'm sorry, Ma.

835
00:32:58,830 --> 00:33:00,570
Oh, don't pay any attention to me.

836
00:33:00,570 --> 00:33:02,673
I guess I'm just upset about all this.

837
00:33:03,560 --> 00:33:04,470
Gee whiz, here I am with

838
00:33:04,470 --> 00:33:05,690
a great opportunity to get somewhere,

839
00:33:05,690 --> 00:33:08,380
to give us security for once
in our lives, and I'm stuck.

840
00:33:08,380 --> 00:33:11,727
If I can put this over, your
Mrs. Burke can have six babies.

841
00:33:11,727 --> 00:33:13,084
- You mean the speech you're writing?

842
00:33:13,084 --> 00:33:16,400
- Yeah, I don't know, I
simply can't get it to gel.

843
00:33:16,400 --> 00:33:17,440
I created somebody who was going

844
00:33:17,440 --> 00:33:18,870
to give up his life for a principle.

845
00:33:18,870 --> 00:33:19,734
Hundreds of thousands of people

846
00:33:19,734 --> 00:33:21,560
are gonna listen to him over the radio.

847
00:33:21,560 --> 00:33:23,540
And unless he says something that's,

848
00:33:23,540 --> 00:33:26,020
well, that's sensational,
it's just no good.

849
00:33:26,020 --> 00:33:27,580
- Well, honey, of course I don't know

850
00:33:27,580 --> 00:33:29,770
what kind of a speech
you're trying to write,

851
00:33:29,770 --> 00:33:32,050
but judging from the samples I've read,

852
00:33:32,050 --> 00:33:33,780
I don't think anybody'll listen.

853
00:33:33,780 --> 00:33:35,240
- What?

854
00:33:35,240 --> 00:33:38,830
- Darling, there are so many
complaining political speeches.

855
00:33:38,830 --> 00:33:39,890
People are tired of hearing

856
00:33:39,890 --> 00:33:42,050
nothing but doom and despair on the radio.

857
00:33:42,050 --> 00:33:43,910
If you're gonna have him say anything,

858
00:33:43,910 --> 00:33:46,780
why don't you let him say
something simple and real,

859
00:33:46,780 --> 00:33:48,080
something with hope in it?

860
00:33:49,742 --> 00:33:52,478
If your father were alive,
he'd know what to say.

861
00:33:52,478 --> 00:33:53,311
- Oh, yes.

862
00:33:54,493 --> 00:33:57,527
Father certainly would.

863
00:33:57,527 --> 00:34:02,527
- Wait a minute.
- Hmm?

864
00:34:15,854 --> 00:34:17,756
- That's your father's diary, Ann.

865
00:34:17,756 --> 00:34:18,795
- Father's--

866
00:34:18,795 --> 00:34:21,058
I never knew he had a diary.

867
00:34:21,058 --> 00:34:23,310
- There's enough in it for a 100 speeches,

868
00:34:23,310 --> 00:34:25,960
things people ought to hear nowadays.

869
00:34:25,960 --> 00:34:28,200
You'll be careful of it, won't you, dear?

870
00:34:28,200 --> 00:34:30,707
Always helped to keep
your father alive for me.

871
00:34:30,707 --> 00:34:33,276
- You bet I will, Ma.

872
00:34:33,276 --> 00:34:34,937
(angry chattering)

873
00:34:34,937 --> 00:34:38,410
- Wait a minute, John Doe
don't wanna sign no autographs.

874
00:34:38,410 --> 00:34:39,580
- Well, what does he do all day?

875
00:34:39,580 --> 00:34:41,300
- What does he do all day?

876
00:34:41,300 --> 00:34:42,960
He's writing out his memories.

877
00:34:42,960 --> 00:34:44,140
(angry chattering)

878
00:34:44,140 --> 00:34:45,780
- Sorry, lady, you can't see Mr. Doe.

879
00:34:45,780 --> 00:34:47,250
He wants to be alone.

880
00:34:47,250 --> 00:34:48,450
No.

881
00:34:48,450 --> 00:34:51,350
No, he just sits around all
day and commutes with himself.

882
00:34:54,550 --> 00:34:57,293
("Take Me Out to the Ballgame")

883
00:34:57,293 --> 00:34:58,126
Ball.

884
00:35:08,440 --> 00:35:09,368
- I don't know how you're gonna stand it

885
00:35:09,368 --> 00:35:11,018
around here till after Christmas.

886
00:35:15,240 --> 00:35:17,923
I'll bet you ain't heard a
train whistle in two weeks.

887
00:35:25,527 --> 00:35:26,360
- Strike.

888
00:35:40,413 --> 00:35:42,163
- I know why you're hanging around.

889
00:35:49,310 --> 00:35:50,743
You're stuck on the girl.

890
00:35:53,727 --> 00:35:56,527
That's all a guy needs is to
get hooked up with a woman.

891
00:36:14,490 --> 00:36:16,510
- What was that, a single?

892
00:36:16,510 --> 00:36:18,638
- First baseman dropped the ball.

893
00:36:18,638 --> 00:36:20,610
- Butterfingers.

894
00:36:20,610 --> 00:36:21,603
Tough luck, pal.

895
00:36:28,040 --> 00:36:30,250
- A guy has a woman on his
hands, first thing he knows,

896
00:36:30,250 --> 00:36:33,388
his life is balled up
with a lot more things.

897
00:36:33,388 --> 00:36:34,221
Furniture.

898
00:36:45,977 --> 00:36:46,963
- Did you get him?

899
00:36:48,400 --> 00:36:49,420
- You're out.

900
00:36:49,420 --> 00:36:50,830
- Swell.

901
00:36:50,830 --> 00:36:51,710
What's this, the end of the eighth?

902
00:36:51,710 --> 00:36:52,543
- [John] Ninth.

903
00:36:57,510 --> 00:36:59,290
- Hey, Beany, there's a couple
of lugs from the Chronicle

904
00:36:59,290 --> 00:37:00,459
snooping around out here.

905
00:37:00,459 --> 00:37:01,292
- Ah.

906
00:37:04,460 --> 00:37:05,880
Come on, Angelface.

907
00:37:07,790 --> 00:37:09,020
Gangway.

908
00:37:09,020 --> 00:37:11,233
- What's the score, Angelface?
- Three to two, our favor.

909
00:37:11,233 --> 00:37:12,900
- Gee, that's great.

910
00:37:19,213 --> 00:37:20,820
- Hey, you got swell form there.

911
00:37:20,820 --> 00:37:22,580
Must've been a pretty good pitcher.

912
00:37:22,580 --> 00:37:23,430
- Pretty good, yeah.

913
00:37:23,430 --> 00:37:24,700
I was just about ready
for the Major Leagues

914
00:37:24,700 --> 00:37:26,550
when I chipped a bone in my elbow.

915
00:37:26,550 --> 00:37:28,650
I got it pitching a 19 inning game.

916
00:37:28,650 --> 00:37:29,700
- 19?

917
00:37:29,700 --> 00:37:30,540
- Yep.

918
00:37:30,540 --> 00:37:32,467
There was a Major League
scout there watching me, too.

919
00:37:32,467 --> 00:37:34,867
And he came down after
the game with a contract.

920
00:37:35,701 --> 00:37:36,840
But you know what?

921
00:37:36,840 --> 00:37:38,540
I couldn't lift my arm to sign it.

922
00:37:39,600 --> 00:37:41,620
I'll be okay though as
soon as I get it fixed up.

923
00:37:41,620 --> 00:37:42,470
- That's too bad.

924
00:37:43,870 --> 00:37:45,003
- What do you mean, too bad?

925
00:37:45,003 --> 00:37:46,100
- Huh?

926
00:37:46,100 --> 00:37:48,640
Oh, that you'll never
be able to play again.

927
00:37:48,640 --> 00:37:49,523
- What are you talking about?

928
00:37:49,523 --> 00:37:50,356
I just told you I was gonna get my--

929
00:37:50,356 --> 00:37:52,150
- [Man] Well, you know
how they are in baseball

930
00:37:52,150 --> 00:37:53,750
if a guy's mixed up in a racket.

931
00:37:54,920 --> 00:37:56,210
- Racket, what do you mean?

932
00:37:56,210 --> 00:37:59,563
- Well, I was just thinking
about this John Doe business.

933
00:37:59,563 --> 00:38:02,350
Why, as soon as it comes
out it's all a fake,

934
00:38:02,350 --> 00:38:04,500
you'll be washed up in
baseball, won't you?

935
00:38:07,060 --> 00:38:07,893
- Yeah.

936
00:38:11,036 --> 00:38:13,464
Doggone, I never thought about that.

937
00:38:13,464 --> 00:38:18,464
("Funeral March" by Frederic
Chopin on harmonica)

938
00:38:19,102 --> 00:38:19,935
Gosh.

939
00:38:19,935 --> 00:38:22,202
- And another thing, what about
all the kids in the country,

940
00:38:22,202 --> 00:38:24,563
the kids that idolize ballplayers?

941
00:38:24,563 --> 00:38:27,859
What are they gonna think about you?

942
00:38:27,859 --> 00:38:28,692
- Yeah.

943
00:38:29,610 --> 00:38:30,739
Hey, Colonel.

944
00:38:30,739 --> 00:38:33,680
(harmonica music)

945
00:38:33,680 --> 00:38:35,130
Did you hear that?

946
00:38:35,130 --> 00:38:37,840
I got to figure some
way out of this thing.

947
00:38:37,840 --> 00:38:39,540
- The elevators are still running.

948
00:38:41,090 --> 00:38:43,080
- I know one way you can do it.

949
00:38:43,080 --> 00:38:44,610
- How?

950
00:38:44,610 --> 00:38:46,090
- Well, when you get
up there on the radio,

951
00:38:46,090 --> 00:38:48,770
all you have to do is say
the whole thing's a frame up.

952
00:38:48,770 --> 00:38:50,763
Make you a hero, sure as you're born.

953
00:38:52,180 --> 00:38:54,830
- Yeah, but how am I
going to get my arm fixed?

954
00:38:54,830 --> 00:38:55,683
- That's a cinch.

955
00:38:56,690 --> 00:38:58,810
I know somebody who'll give you $5,000

956
00:38:58,810 --> 00:39:00,647
just to get up on the
radio and tell the truth.

957
00:39:00,647 --> 00:39:02,320
- $5,000?

958
00:39:02,320 --> 00:39:04,500
- Yeah, $5,000 and he gets it right away.

959
00:39:04,500 --> 00:39:07,100
You don't have to wait till Christmas.

960
00:39:07,100 --> 00:39:09,763
- Look out, Long John,
they're closing in on you.

961
00:39:11,390 --> 00:39:13,860
- Say, who's putting up this dough?

962
00:39:13,860 --> 00:39:15,210
- Fella runs the Chronicle.

963
00:39:17,590 --> 00:39:21,907
Here's the speech you make
and it's all written for you.

964
00:39:21,907 --> 00:39:23,253
- $5,000.

965
00:39:26,376 --> 00:39:27,483
- [Man] It's on the level.

966
00:39:29,359 --> 00:39:32,859
(upbeat orchestral music)

967
00:39:38,476 --> 00:39:41,850
- No, I'm sorry, tickets for
the broadcast are all gone.

968
00:39:41,850 --> 00:39:43,030
Phone the Bulletin.

969
00:39:43,030 --> 00:39:44,785
- Sorry, no more tickets left.

970
00:39:44,785 --> 00:39:47,202
(chattering)

971
00:39:54,757 --> 00:39:56,350
- Here he is.

972
00:39:56,350 --> 00:39:58,670
- [Ann] Hello John, all
set for the big night?

973
00:39:58,670 --> 00:39:59,503
Swell.

974
00:39:59,503 --> 00:40:00,612
- Turn around.
- One moment.

975
00:40:00,612 --> 00:40:02,157
- Now stand still.
- Hold it, Mr. Doe.

976
00:40:02,157 --> 00:40:03,326
Big smile, Mr. Doe.

977
00:40:03,326 --> 00:40:04,159
Ah that's swell.

978
00:40:04,159 --> 00:40:04,992
- Okay, Beany, that's all,
take them out will you?

979
00:40:04,992 --> 00:40:05,967
- Alright, scram now.
- Come on.

980
00:40:05,967 --> 00:40:08,034
Can't I get another picture?

981
00:40:08,034 --> 00:40:09,290
- Now look John, here's the speech.

982
00:40:09,290 --> 00:40:10,460
It's in caps and double spaced.

983
00:40:10,460 --> 00:40:11,930
You won't have no trouble reading it.

984
00:40:11,930 --> 00:40:12,990
Not nervous, are you?
- No.

985
00:40:12,990 --> 00:40:14,853
- Of course not, he wouldn't be.

986
00:40:14,853 --> 00:40:16,870
- Who?
- John Doe, the one in there.

987
00:40:16,870 --> 00:40:17,950
- Hey don't let your knees rattle.

988
00:40:17,950 --> 00:40:18,783
It picks up on the mic.

989
00:40:18,783 --> 00:40:19,900
- Oh Beany.

990
00:40:19,900 --> 00:40:21,130
You needn't be nervous, John

991
00:40:21,130 --> 00:40:23,120
All you have to remember to be sincere.

992
00:40:23,120 --> 00:40:24,450
- Pick up that phone Miss
Mitchell it's for you.

993
00:40:24,450 --> 00:40:25,370
- [Ann] Okay.

994
00:40:25,370 --> 00:40:26,203
Hello?

995
00:40:26,203 --> 00:40:27,220
Yes, mother.

996
00:40:27,220 --> 00:40:28,327
- Oh, thank you, darling.

997
00:40:28,327 --> 00:40:30,794
Oh, there he is, the poor dear man.

998
00:40:30,794 --> 00:40:33,201
Oh good luck to you, Mr. Doe.

999
00:40:33,201 --> 00:40:35,732
We want you to know that
we're are all for you.

1000
00:40:35,732 --> 00:40:37,497
The girls all decided that

1001
00:40:37,497 --> 00:40:41,017
you're not to jump off any roof at all.

1002
00:40:41,017 --> 00:40:42,384
(chattering)

1003
00:40:42,384 --> 00:40:43,964
- You got the speech I gave you?

1004
00:40:43,964 --> 00:40:44,797
- Yeah.

1005
00:40:46,058 --> 00:40:48,363
- Now look, I'll give
the money to the Colonel

1006
00:40:48,363 --> 00:40:50,180
just as soon as you get started.

1007
00:40:50,180 --> 00:40:52,130
We'll have a car waiting at
the side entrance for you.

1008
00:40:52,130 --> 00:40:53,637
- Okay.
- How'd you get in here?

1009
00:40:53,637 --> 00:40:55,153
- Oh, I just came in to wish him luck.

1010
00:40:55,153 --> 00:40:56,883
- Come on, out, out.

1011
00:40:57,990 --> 00:40:59,560
Mother says good luck, too.

1012
00:40:59,560 --> 00:41:01,320
John, when you read the speech please,

1013
00:41:01,320 --> 00:41:02,940
please believe every word.

1014
00:41:02,940 --> 00:41:05,029
He turned out to be wonderful person John.

1015
00:41:05,029 --> 00:41:05,862
- Who?

1016
00:41:05,862 --> 00:41:06,960
- [Ann] John Doe, the one in the speech.

1017
00:41:06,960 --> 00:41:07,793
- Oh.

1018
00:41:07,793 --> 00:41:09,010
- [Ann] You know something?

1019
00:41:09,010 --> 00:41:10,910
I've actually fallen in love with him.

1020
00:41:12,280 --> 00:41:13,113
- All right, there he is, sister.

1021
00:41:13,113 --> 00:41:15,030
Now come, plenty of bulk.
- What's the idea?

1022
00:41:15,030 --> 00:41:16,522
- No, no, no, no, no that's too much.

1023
00:41:16,522 --> 00:41:18,700
That's not what, not so much, come on.

1024
00:41:18,700 --> 00:41:19,700
- [Man] That's it.

1025
00:41:19,700 --> 00:41:21,950
- Oh, this is no time for
cheap publicity, Mr. Connell.

1026
00:41:21,950 --> 00:41:24,940
- Listen if that guy lays an
egg I want something out of it.

1027
00:41:24,940 --> 00:41:26,480
I'm getting a Jane Doe ready.

1028
00:41:26,480 --> 00:41:27,721
- Ah come on, will you fellas?

1029
00:41:27,721 --> 00:41:29,113
Give me a chance, I have to--

1030
00:41:29,113 --> 00:41:30,106
- Just one more, will you please?

1031
00:41:30,106 --> 00:41:31,385
- Now that's fine honey, you go ahead.

1032
00:41:31,385 --> 00:41:32,497
After you.

1033
00:41:32,497 --> 00:41:33,850
You've got to be running, go ahead.

1034
00:41:33,850 --> 00:41:35,100
- How you doing?

1035
00:41:35,100 --> 00:41:36,910
- Alright Beany, bring them in.

1036
00:41:36,910 --> 00:41:39,956
- Holy smoke, a half a Heelot.

1037
00:41:39,956 --> 00:41:41,620
- There you are, boss,
just like you ordered.

1038
00:41:41,620 --> 00:41:42,790
Symbols of little people.

1039
00:41:42,790 --> 00:41:44,710
- Okay, get 'em up.
- Alright little fella.

1040
00:41:44,710 --> 00:41:46,240
- This is ridiculous, Mr. Connell.

1041
00:41:46,240 --> 00:41:47,073
Come on give me a chance.

1042
00:41:47,073 --> 00:41:48,995
The man's on the air in
only a few more minutes.

1043
00:41:48,995 --> 00:41:50,668
Oh, fellas I told you can have

1044
00:41:50,668 --> 00:41:52,468
all the pictures you want afterward.

1045
00:41:52,468 --> 00:41:53,630
No, come on, Ed.

1046
00:41:53,630 --> 00:41:54,704
And how about you?

1047
00:41:54,704 --> 00:41:55,590
Come on, come on, that's right.

1048
00:41:55,590 --> 00:41:56,909
Afterwards you can get
all the pictures you want.

1049
00:41:56,909 --> 00:41:58,340
- [Man] Oh I know but,
well thank you very much.

1050
00:41:58,340 --> 00:41:59,173
- Come on, will you?

1051
00:41:59,173 --> 00:42:00,150
That way, that's right.

1052
00:42:00,150 --> 00:42:00,983
- Come on Snooks.

1053
00:42:00,983 --> 00:42:02,139
You better bail out.

1054
00:42:02,139 --> 00:42:04,212
- Well, goodbye, Mr. Doe.

1055
00:42:04,212 --> 00:42:05,045
- Beany.

1056
00:42:05,045 --> 00:42:06,628
- Alright, alright.

1057
00:42:08,700 --> 00:42:09,982
- Better get ready, one minute to go.

1058
00:42:09,982 --> 00:42:12,277
- Wow, one minute to go and
the score's nothing to nothing.

1059
00:42:12,277 --> 00:42:13,990
Now please, John.

1060
00:42:13,990 --> 00:42:15,823
You won't let me down, will ya?

1061
00:42:17,342 --> 00:42:18,175
Will ya?

1062
00:42:19,350 --> 00:42:20,660
Of course you won't.

1063
00:42:20,660 --> 00:42:23,363
If you'll just think of
yourself as the real John Doe.

1064
00:42:24,240 --> 00:42:26,100
Listen, everything in that speech

1065
00:42:26,100 --> 00:42:28,040
are things a certain man believed in.

1066
00:42:28,040 --> 00:42:30,410
He was my father, John.

1067
00:42:30,410 --> 00:42:32,623
When he talked, people listened.

1068
00:42:32,623 --> 00:42:34,173
And they'll listen to you, too.

1069
00:42:35,450 --> 00:42:38,883
Funny, you know what my
mother said the other night?

1070
00:42:39,900 --> 00:42:43,333
She said to look into your eyes
and I'd see my father there.

1071
00:42:46,130 --> 00:42:47,950
- Hey what do you say?
- Okay, we're coming.

1072
00:42:47,950 --> 00:42:48,783
Come on.

1073
00:42:49,810 --> 00:42:51,730
Now listen, John, you're a pitcher.

1074
00:42:51,730 --> 00:42:53,163
Now get in there and pitch.

1075
00:42:55,160 --> 00:42:56,400
Good luck.

1076
00:42:56,400 --> 00:42:58,642
- [Beany] Give him room,
let him through, come on.

1077
00:42:58,642 --> 00:43:01,059
(chattering)

1078
00:43:13,203 --> 00:43:14,410
- Hey, let's get out of here.

1079
00:43:14,410 --> 00:43:15,361
There's the door right there.

1080
00:43:15,361 --> 00:43:16,470
- [Man] Hey what are you doing here?

1081
00:43:16,470 --> 00:43:17,600
- That's what I'd like to know.

1082
00:43:17,600 --> 00:43:18,550
- [Man] Come on, out, out.

1083
00:43:18,550 --> 00:43:19,383
- Hey, he's a friend of mine.

1084
00:43:19,383 --> 00:43:21,470
- Never mind, let him alone, he's alright.

1085
00:43:24,203 --> 00:43:27,620
I'll be right over there pulling for you.

1086
00:43:29,673 --> 00:43:31,006
John, over here.

1087
00:43:35,950 --> 00:43:36,893
- [Man] Stand by.

1088
00:43:38,642 --> 00:43:41,059
(applauding)

1089
00:44:08,858 --> 00:44:09,691
- Phone the Chronicle.

1090
00:44:09,691 --> 00:44:12,437
Tell 'em to start
getting those extras out.

1091
00:44:29,759 --> 00:44:32,676
(orchestral music)

1092
00:44:34,037 --> 00:44:35,510
- And good evening ladies and gentlemen,

1093
00:44:35,510 --> 00:44:37,830
this is Kenneth Fry speaking
for the New Bulletin.

1094
00:44:37,830 --> 00:44:40,860
Tonight we give you something
entirely new and different.

1095
00:44:40,860 --> 00:44:42,450
Standing beside me is the young man

1096
00:44:42,450 --> 00:44:44,070
who has declared publicly that

1097
00:44:44,070 --> 00:44:46,750
on Christmas Eve he
intends to commit suicide,

1098
00:44:46,750 --> 00:44:48,730
giving as his reason, quote,

1099
00:44:48,730 --> 00:44:51,420
I protest against the
state of civilization.

1100
00:44:51,420 --> 00:44:52,580
End quote.

1101
00:44:52,580 --> 00:44:55,010
Ladies and gentlemen, the
New Bulletin takes pleasure

1102
00:44:55,010 --> 00:44:56,740
in presenting the man who is fast becoming

1103
00:44:56,740 --> 00:45:00,750
the most talked about person
in the country, John Doe.

1104
00:45:00,750 --> 00:45:05,750
(orchestral music)
(applauding)

1105
00:45:23,934 --> 00:45:25,851
- Ladies and gentlemen.

1106
00:45:28,530 --> 00:45:31,073
I am the man you all know as John Doe.

1107
00:45:32,590 --> 00:45:35,747
I took that name because
it seems to describe--

1108
00:45:38,590 --> 00:45:42,912
Because it seems to describe
the average man, and that's me.

1109
00:45:42,912 --> 00:45:43,980
(clearing throat)

1110
00:45:43,980 --> 00:45:44,823
And that's me.

1111
00:45:49,130 --> 00:45:52,240
Well, it was me before I
said I was going to jump off

1112
00:45:52,240 --> 00:45:54,933
the City Hall roof at
midnight on Christmas Eve.

1113
00:45:56,350 --> 00:45:58,283
Now I guess I'm not average anymore.

1114
00:45:59,750 --> 00:46:02,493
Now I'm getting all sorts of
attention, from big shots too.

1115
00:46:02,493 --> 00:46:04,250
- We've been double crossed.
- We have?

1116
00:46:04,250 --> 00:46:07,030
- The Mayor and Governor, for instance,

1117
00:46:07,030 --> 00:46:08,773
they don't like those
articles I've been writing.

1118
00:46:08,773 --> 00:46:10,850
- You're an impostor, young fella.

1119
00:46:10,850 --> 00:46:12,700
That's a pack of lies you're telling.

1120
00:46:13,900 --> 00:46:16,099
Who wrote that speech for you?

1121
00:46:16,099 --> 00:46:17,878
(angry shouting)

1122
00:46:17,878 --> 00:46:20,177
- Beany, get that guy.

1123
00:46:20,177 --> 00:46:23,324
- [Man] A publicity
stunt for the Bulletin.

1124
00:46:23,324 --> 00:46:26,074
(angry shouting)

1125
00:46:27,096 --> 00:46:28,225
A publicity stunt for the Bulletin.

1126
00:46:28,225 --> 00:46:29,290
- Get him out.

1127
00:46:29,290 --> 00:46:30,180
- Spencer.

1128
00:46:30,180 --> 00:46:31,030
- [Radio announcer] Ladies and gentlemen,

1129
00:46:31,030 --> 00:46:32,350
the disturbance you just heard

1130
00:46:32,350 --> 00:46:33,950
was caused by someone in the audience

1131
00:46:33,950 --> 00:46:36,090
who tried to heckle Mr. Doe.

1132
00:46:36,090 --> 00:46:37,340
The speech will continue.

1133
00:46:42,260 --> 00:46:46,559
- Well, people like the Governor
people like the Governor--

1134
00:46:46,559 --> 00:46:50,130
people like the Governor and
that fella can stop worrying.

1135
00:46:50,130 --> 00:46:52,230
I'm not gonna talk about them.

1136
00:46:52,230 --> 00:46:56,463
I'm gonna talk about us, the
average guys, the John Does.

1137
00:46:57,630 --> 00:47:01,180
If anybody ever asked you what
the average John Doe is like,

1138
00:47:01,180 --> 00:47:04,233
you couldn't tell him because
he's a million and one things.

1139
00:47:05,150 --> 00:47:07,750
He's Mr. Big and Mr. Small.

1140
00:47:07,750 --> 00:47:09,540
He's simple and he's wise.

1141
00:47:09,540 --> 00:47:11,180
He's inherently honest,

1142
00:47:11,180 --> 00:47:13,390
but he's got a streak
of larceny in his heart.

1143
00:47:13,390 --> 00:47:16,280
He seldom walks up to a public
telephone without shoving

1144
00:47:16,280 --> 00:47:17,640
his finger in the slot to see

1145
00:47:17,640 --> 00:47:19,397
if somebody left a nickel there.

1146
00:47:19,397 --> 00:47:21,647
(laughing)

1147
00:47:30,710 --> 00:47:33,587
He's the man the ads are written for,

1148
00:47:33,587 --> 00:47:36,730
the fella everybody sells things to.

1149
00:47:36,730 --> 00:47:40,210
He's Joe Dokes, the
world's greatest stooge,

1150
00:47:40,210 --> 00:47:42,083
and the world's greatest strength.

1151
00:47:51,356 --> 00:47:52,189
Yes, sir.

1152
00:47:53,470 --> 00:47:56,450
Yes, sir, we're a great
family, the John Does.

1153
00:47:56,450 --> 00:48:00,073
We're the meek who are
supposed to inherit the earth.

1154
00:48:01,300 --> 00:48:02,970
You'll find us everywhere.

1155
00:48:02,970 --> 00:48:05,820
We raise the crops, we dig
the mines, work the factories,

1156
00:48:05,820 --> 00:48:09,130
keep the books, fly the
planes, and drive the buses.

1157
00:48:09,130 --> 00:48:11,710
And when a cop yells,
stand back there you,

1158
00:48:11,710 --> 00:48:13,773
he means us, the John Does.

1159
00:48:13,773 --> 00:48:15,360
- Well what kind of a speech is that?

1160
00:48:15,360 --> 00:48:16,640
Didn't you read it?

1161
00:48:16,640 --> 00:48:18,660
- We've existed since time began.

1162
00:48:18,660 --> 00:48:22,610
We built the Pyramids,
we saw Christ crucified,

1163
00:48:22,610 --> 00:48:25,150
pulled the oars for Roman emperors,

1164
00:48:25,150 --> 00:48:27,260
sailed the boats for Columbus,

1165
00:48:27,260 --> 00:48:29,840
retreated from Moscow with Napoleon,{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Moscow}

1166
00:48:29,840 --> 00:48:32,483
and froze with Washington at Valley Forge.{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Washington,_D.C.}

1167
00:48:35,670 --> 00:48:37,520
Yes, sir, we've been in there

1168
00:48:37,520 --> 00:48:41,500
dodging left hooks since
before History began to walk.

1169
00:48:41,500 --> 00:48:43,010
In our struggle for freedom

1170
00:48:43,010 --> 00:48:44,560
we've hit the canvas many a time,

1171
00:48:44,560 --> 00:48:46,230
but we always bounce back because

1172
00:48:46,230 --> 00:48:48,236
we're the people and we're tough.

1173
00:48:48,236 --> 00:48:50,653
(applauding)

1174
00:48:53,651 --> 00:48:56,520
They've started a lot of talk
about free people going soft,

1175
00:48:56,520 --> 00:48:57,890
that we can't take it.

1176
00:48:57,890 --> 00:48:58,990
That's a lot of hooey.

1177
00:49:00,410 --> 00:49:03,130
The free people can beat the
world at anything from war

1178
00:49:03,130 --> 00:49:06,814
to tiddlywinks if we all
pull in the same direction.

1179
00:49:06,814 --> 00:49:09,231
(applauding)

1180
00:49:14,250 --> 00:49:17,490
I know a lot of you are
saying, what can I do?

1181
00:49:17,490 --> 00:49:18,930
I'm just a little punk.

1182
00:49:18,930 --> 00:49:20,470
I don't count.

1183
00:49:20,470 --> 00:49:21,440
Well you're dead wrong.

1184
00:49:21,440 --> 00:49:24,160
The little punks have always
counted because in the long run

1185
00:49:24,160 --> 00:49:26,780
the character of a
country is the sum total

1186
00:49:26,780 --> 00:49:28,825
of the character of it's little punks.

1187
00:49:28,825 --> 00:49:31,242
(applauding)

1188
00:49:35,870 --> 00:49:37,307
But we've all got to
get in there and pitch.

1189
00:49:37,307 --> 00:49:41,020
We can't win the ball game
unless we have team work,

1190
00:49:41,020 --> 00:49:44,180
and that's where every John Doe comes in.

1191
00:49:44,180 --> 00:49:47,560
It's up to him to get
together with his team mate,

1192
00:49:47,560 --> 00:49:52,190
and your team mate, my friends,
is the guy next door to you.

1193
00:49:52,190 --> 00:49:54,580
Your neighbor, he's a
terribly important guy,

1194
00:49:54,580 --> 00:49:56,530
that guy next door.

1195
00:49:56,530 --> 00:49:57,420
You're gonna need him

1196
00:49:57,420 --> 00:49:59,780
and he's gonna need you so look him up.

1197
00:49:59,780 --> 00:50:01,730
If he's sick, call on him.

1198
00:50:01,730 --> 00:50:03,630
If he's hungry, feed him.

1199
00:50:03,630 --> 00:50:06,003
If he's out of a job, find him one.

1200
00:50:06,860 --> 00:50:08,930
To most of you, your
neighbor is a stranger.

1201
00:50:08,930 --> 00:50:11,960
A guy with a barking dog
and a high fence around him.

1202
00:50:11,960 --> 00:50:13,210
Now you can't be a stranger

1203
00:50:13,210 --> 00:50:15,750
to any guy that's on your own team.

1204
00:50:15,750 --> 00:50:18,540
So tear down the fence that separates you.

1205
00:50:18,540 --> 00:50:20,830
Tear down the fence and you'll tear down

1206
00:50:20,830 --> 00:50:22,850
a lot of hates and prejudices.

1207
00:50:22,850 --> 00:50:24,730
Tear down all the fences in the country

1208
00:50:24,730 --> 00:50:25,937
and you'll really have teamwork.

1209
00:50:25,937 --> 00:50:28,354
(applauding)

1210
00:50:35,166 --> 00:50:37,480
I know a lot of you are
saying to yourselves,

1211
00:50:37,480 --> 00:50:39,270
he's asking for a miracle to happen.

1212
00:50:39,270 --> 00:50:42,650
He's expecting people to
change all of a sudden.

1213
00:50:42,650 --> 00:50:43,483
Well, you're wrong.

1214
00:50:43,483 --> 00:50:44,480
It's no miracle.

1215
00:50:44,480 --> 00:50:47,150
It's no miracle because I see it happen

1216
00:50:47,150 --> 00:50:51,343
once a year and so do
you, at Christmas time.

1217
00:50:52,400 --> 00:50:55,350
There's something swell about
the spirit of Christmas.

1218
00:50:55,350 --> 00:50:59,030
To see what it does to
people, all kinds of people.

1219
00:50:59,030 --> 00:51:03,060
Now why can't that spirit, that
same warm Christmas spirit,

1220
00:51:03,060 --> 00:51:05,210
last the whole year round?

1221
00:51:05,210 --> 00:51:06,043
Gosh.

1222
00:51:07,060 --> 00:51:10,330
If it ever did, if each and every John Doe

1223
00:51:10,330 --> 00:51:14,220
would make that spirit last
365 days out of the year

1224
00:51:14,220 --> 00:51:15,820
we'd develop such a strength,

1225
00:51:15,820 --> 00:51:18,290
we'd create such a tidal wave of goodwill

1226
00:51:18,290 --> 00:51:20,593
that no human force
could stand against it.

1227
00:51:22,360 --> 00:51:23,473
Yes sir, my friends,

1228
00:51:24,900 --> 00:51:28,450
the meek can only inherit
the Earth when the John Does

1229
00:51:28,450 --> 00:51:29,900
start loving their neighbors.

1230
00:51:30,900 --> 00:51:32,303
You better start right now.

1231
00:51:33,310 --> 00:51:36,210
Don't wait till the game is
called on account of darkness.

1232
00:51:37,904 --> 00:51:40,304
Wake up, John Doe, you're
the hope of the world.

1233
00:51:49,300 --> 00:51:50,925
- John, you were wonderful.

1234
00:51:50,925 --> 00:51:55,592
(applauding)
(cheering)

1235
00:51:58,804 --> 00:52:02,554
- Let's get out of here.
- Now you're talking.

1236
00:52:04,004 --> 00:52:05,879
(chattering)

1237
00:52:05,879 --> 00:52:10,334
Gangway, you Heelots.
(chattering)

1238
00:52:10,334 --> 00:52:11,167
Hey.

1239
00:52:19,023 --> 00:52:20,050
(sighing)

1240
00:52:20,050 --> 00:52:21,900
I knew you'd wake up sooner or later.

1241
00:52:22,950 --> 00:52:25,290
Boy, am I glad we got out of that mess.

1242
00:52:25,290 --> 00:52:27,983
- I had the 5,000 all sewed up.

1243
00:52:27,983 --> 00:52:30,033
Could've been on my way to old Doc Brown.

1244
00:52:32,217 --> 00:52:33,467
"You're a pitcher, John" she says.

1245
00:52:33,467 --> 00:52:35,100
"Now go in there and pitch."

1246
00:52:35,100 --> 00:52:36,448
What a sucker.

1247
00:52:36,448 --> 00:52:38,650
- She's a Heelot just like the rest.

1248
00:52:38,650 --> 00:52:40,140
Lucky you got away from her.

1249
00:52:40,140 --> 00:52:43,550
- What was I doing making
a speech, anyway, me, huh?

1250
00:52:43,550 --> 00:52:45,450
Jeez, the more I think
about it the more I could--

1251
00:52:45,450 --> 00:52:47,210
- Tear town all the fences.

1252
00:52:47,210 --> 00:52:48,768
Why, if you tore one picket off

1253
00:52:48,768 --> 00:52:50,723
your neighbor's fence, he'd sue you.

1254
00:52:50,723 --> 00:52:52,850
- 5,000, had it right in my hand.

1255
00:52:52,850 --> 00:52:54,960
- What do you mean he ran away?

1256
00:52:54,960 --> 00:52:56,190
Well go after him, find him.

1257
00:52:56,190 --> 00:52:57,240
That man is terrific.

1258
00:52:58,167 --> 00:53:01,000
(train whistling)

1259
00:53:04,371 --> 00:53:05,954
- And here we come.

1260
00:53:07,362 --> 00:53:10,195
(harmonica music)

1261
00:53:12,291 --> 00:53:16,374
(mimicking a musical instrument)

1262
00:53:19,751 --> 00:53:22,584
(harmonica music)

1263
00:53:34,613 --> 00:53:37,446
(train whistling)

1264
00:53:38,526 --> 00:53:41,109
(groovy music)

1265
00:53:52,344 --> 00:53:53,743
Jitterbugs.

1266
00:53:53,743 --> 00:53:54,576
Yeah.

1267
00:53:55,410 --> 00:53:56,806
- How much money we got left?

1268
00:53:56,806 --> 00:53:57,900
- Four bits.

1269
00:53:57,900 --> 00:53:58,881
- Better make it doughnuts, huh?

1270
00:53:58,881 --> 00:53:59,714
- Yeah.

1271
00:54:03,473 --> 00:54:06,306
(harmonica music)

1272
00:54:12,269 --> 00:54:14,060
- What'll it be, gents?

1273
00:54:14,060 --> 00:54:14,893
- Have you got a couple of steaks

1274
00:54:14,893 --> 00:54:17,140
about that big and that thick?

1275
00:54:17,140 --> 00:54:19,288
- [Colonel] Yeah, with hash
brown potatoes and tomatoes

1276
00:54:19,288 --> 00:54:22,210
and apple pie and ice cream and coffee?

1277
00:54:22,210 --> 00:54:24,140
- And doughnuts, I know.

1278
00:54:24,140 --> 00:54:27,072
Hey, Ma, sinkers, a pair.

1279
00:54:27,072 --> 00:54:28,030
- [Woman] Sinkers, a pair, comin' up.

1280
00:54:28,930 --> 00:54:30,580
- Glad he took the T out of that.

1281
00:54:35,660 --> 00:54:37,153
- Hey Look.

1282
00:54:38,260 --> 00:54:41,789
- [Colonel] Join the John Doe Club.

1283
00:54:41,789 --> 00:54:46,789
- [John] John Doe Club?

1284
00:54:49,324 --> 00:54:52,940
- Uh-oh.

1285
00:54:52,940 --> 00:54:54,350
- Are you John Doe?
- Who?

1286
00:54:54,350 --> 00:54:55,183
- John Doe.

1287
00:54:55,183 --> 00:54:57,410
- [Colonel] Ah, you need glasses, buddy.

1288
00:54:57,410 --> 00:54:58,600
- It's the spitting image of John Doe.

1289
00:54:58,600 --> 00:55:00,197
- Yeah, but his name's Willoughby.

1290
00:55:00,197 --> 00:55:02,588
- [John] I'm John Willoughby,
I'm a baseball player.

1291
00:55:02,588 --> 00:55:04,970
- Ah, no, I'd know that voice anywhere.

1292
00:55:04,970 --> 00:55:06,850
You can't kid me.

1293
00:55:06,850 --> 00:55:08,523
You're John Doe.

1294
00:55:08,523 --> 00:55:09,695
Ma!

1295
00:55:09,695 --> 00:55:10,605
Ma.

1296
00:55:10,605 --> 00:55:12,477
That's John Doe.
- John Doe?

1297
00:55:12,477 --> 00:55:14,354
- Yeah, sitting right there, big as life.

1298
00:55:14,354 --> 00:55:15,392
- Who'd you say it was?

1299
00:55:15,392 --> 00:55:17,892
- John Doe, the big guy there.

1300
00:55:18,954 --> 00:55:23,454
(frantic chattering)
That is John Doe.

1301
00:55:29,647 --> 00:55:32,050
Hey Operator, Dan Beaver.

1302
00:55:32,050 --> 00:55:33,260
Call everybody in town.

1303
00:55:33,260 --> 00:55:35,100
John Doe was just in my place.

1304
00:55:35,100 --> 00:55:36,843
Yeah, he ordered doughnuts.

1305
00:55:44,465 --> 00:55:45,969
- [Man] John Doe.

1306
00:55:45,969 --> 00:55:48,223
Let's see John Doe.

1307
00:55:48,223 --> 00:55:50,852
(horn honking)

1308
00:55:50,852 --> 00:55:53,048
- You're all here to see John
Doe and you're all neighbors,

1309
00:55:53,048 --> 00:55:54,840
but my office is packed
like a sardine box.

1310
00:55:54,840 --> 00:55:56,570
- [Woman] What does John
Doe look like, Mr. Mayor?

1311
00:55:56,570 --> 00:55:59,300
- Oh he's one of those
great, big, outdoor type men.

1312
00:55:59,300 --> 00:56:01,560
No you can't see him, you didn't
vote for me the last time.

1313
00:56:01,560 --> 00:56:02,700
Shame on you, what are you doing here?

1314
00:56:02,700 --> 00:56:03,980
Get off my front porch.

1315
00:56:03,980 --> 00:56:05,750
Mr. Norton come yet?

1316
00:56:05,750 --> 00:56:06,906
Well wonder where he
is, what's keeping him?

1317
00:56:06,906 --> 00:56:08,755
He should've been here 15 minutes ago.

1318
00:56:08,755 --> 00:56:10,233
There he comes now.

1319
00:56:10,233 --> 00:56:13,700
(sirens blaring)

1320
00:56:13,700 --> 00:56:14,660
Now everybody on your dignity.

1321
00:56:14,660 --> 00:56:16,680
Don't do anything to
disgrace our little town.

1322
00:56:16,680 --> 00:56:17,580
We gotta show off.

1323
00:56:20,900 --> 00:56:21,733
- Better let me talk to him.

1324
00:56:21,733 --> 00:56:22,830
- Alright, but present it to him

1325
00:56:22,830 --> 00:56:24,410
as a great cause for the common man.

1326
00:56:24,410 --> 00:56:25,243
- [Man] Ah here he comes.

1327
00:56:25,243 --> 00:56:27,690
Give him room down there, give him room.

1328
00:56:27,690 --> 00:56:28,799
Come on now.

1329
00:56:28,799 --> 00:56:30,134
- Here they come, here they come.

1330
00:56:30,134 --> 00:56:31,572
Here's Mr. Norton.

1331
00:56:31,572 --> 00:56:32,670
How do you do, Mr. Norton?

1332
00:56:32,670 --> 00:56:33,603
I'm Mayor Hawkins.

1333
00:56:34,578 --> 00:56:36,747
- The crowd back there.
- Let me go you darn fool.

1334
00:56:36,747 --> 00:56:37,580
I'm the Mayor.

1335
00:56:37,580 --> 00:56:41,030
Mr. Norton, Mr. Norton, Mr.
Norton, I'm Mayor Hawkins.

1336
00:56:41,030 --> 00:56:42,700
Your office telephoned me to hold him.

1337
00:56:42,700 --> 00:56:43,950
- Well that's fine, how is he?

1338
00:56:43,950 --> 00:56:45,750
- Oh he's fine, he's
right in my office there.

1339
00:56:45,750 --> 00:56:48,340
You know it's a big honor having
John Doe here, and you too.

1340
00:56:48,340 --> 00:56:49,250
Haven't had so much excitement

1341
00:56:49,250 --> 00:56:51,500
since the old City Hall burnt down.

1342
00:56:51,500 --> 00:56:53,598
People so excited, they
nearly tore his clothes off.

1343
00:56:53,598 --> 00:56:55,280
Oh Matilda, phone the newspapers.

1344
00:56:55,280 --> 00:56:56,113
Tell them Mr. Norton's here.

1345
00:56:56,113 --> 00:56:58,090
Step inside, Mr. Norton, to my office.

1346
00:56:58,090 --> 00:56:59,703
It's very comfortable Mr. Norton.

1347
00:56:59,703 --> 00:57:00,762
Just had it air-conditioned.

1348
00:57:00,762 --> 00:57:03,009
Gangway please, make room for Mr. Norton.

1349
00:57:03,009 --> 00:57:04,593
Gangway, gangway.

1350
00:57:04,593 --> 00:57:06,400
Here he is Mr. Norton, well taken care of.

1351
00:57:06,400 --> 00:57:08,670
The neighbors are serving
him a light lunch.

1352
00:57:08,670 --> 00:57:09,503
- Hello, John.

1353
00:57:11,350 --> 00:57:12,820
- Hello.

1354
00:57:12,820 --> 00:57:14,140
- Mr. Mayor, if you don't mind,

1355
00:57:14,140 --> 00:57:15,520
we'd like to talk to him alone.

1356
00:57:15,520 --> 00:57:16,860
- Why certainly, certainly.

1357
00:57:16,860 --> 00:57:18,596
Alright everybody clear out.

1358
00:57:18,596 --> 00:57:20,147
Everybody, quickly now.

1359
00:57:20,147 --> 00:57:20,980
Come on.

1360
00:57:20,980 --> 00:57:21,813
Come on.

1361
00:57:23,505 --> 00:57:26,863
That's right, come on.

1362
00:57:28,600 --> 00:57:29,590
Don't argue with me here.

1363
00:57:29,590 --> 00:57:30,445
Wait till we get home.

1364
00:57:30,445 --> 00:57:31,761
- Don't you push me around like that

1365
00:57:31,761 --> 00:57:33,603
even though I am your wife.

1366
00:57:33,603 --> 00:57:35,670
- Look Mr. Norton, I think
you got a lot of nerve

1367
00:57:35,670 --> 00:57:37,393
having these people hold us here.

1368
00:57:38,800 --> 00:57:41,520
- There's nobody holding you here Mr. Doe.

1369
00:57:41,520 --> 00:57:42,353
It's only natural that--

1370
00:57:42,353 --> 00:57:44,610
- If there's nobody holding
us here let's get going.

1371
00:57:44,610 --> 00:57:46,520
Incidentally, my name
isn't Doe, it's Willoughby.

1372
00:57:46,520 --> 00:57:47,790
- Look, John.

1373
00:57:47,790 --> 00:57:49,550
Something important happened.

1374
00:57:49,550 --> 00:57:51,090
They're forming John Doe Clubs.

1375
00:57:51,090 --> 00:57:52,910
We know of eight already and they say--

1376
00:57:52,910 --> 00:57:54,155
- John Doe Clubs, what for?

1377
00:57:54,155 --> 00:57:55,240
- To carry out the principles

1378
00:57:55,240 --> 00:57:56,980
you talked about in your radio speech.

1379
00:57:56,980 --> 00:57:58,130
- I don't care what they're forming.

1380
00:57:58,130 --> 00:57:59,350
I'm on my way and I don't like

1381
00:57:59,350 --> 00:58:00,552
the idea of being stopped either.

1382
00:58:00,552 --> 00:58:02,470
- Oh, but you don't how big this thing is.

1383
00:58:02,470 --> 00:58:04,060
You should see the thousands of telegrams

1384
00:58:04,060 --> 00:58:06,440
we've received and what
they're saying about you.

1385
00:58:06,440 --> 00:58:09,210
- Look, it started as a
circulation stunt, didn't it?

1386
00:58:09,210 --> 00:58:10,467
Well you got your circulation.

1387
00:58:10,467 --> 00:58:11,880
Now why don't you let me alone?

1388
00:58:11,880 --> 00:58:14,700
- Oh it started as a circulation
stunt but it isn't anymore.

1389
00:58:14,700 --> 00:58:16,000
Mr. Norton wants to get in back of it

1390
00:58:16,000 --> 00:58:18,240
and sponsor clubs all over the country.

1391
00:58:18,240 --> 00:58:20,190
He wants to send you on a lecture tour.

1392
00:58:21,090 --> 00:58:22,013
- Me?
- Uh-huh.

1393
00:58:22,013 --> 00:58:22,846
- Certainly.

1394
00:58:22,846 --> 00:58:24,930
With your ability to influence people,

1395
00:58:24,930 --> 00:58:27,210
it might grow into a glorious movement.

1396
00:58:27,210 --> 00:58:29,360
- Then let's get something straight here.

1397
00:58:29,360 --> 00:58:31,495
I don't want any part of this.

1398
00:58:31,495 --> 00:58:32,930
If you got an idea I'm going around

1399
00:58:32,930 --> 00:58:35,410
lecturing to people why you're crazy.

1400
00:58:35,410 --> 00:58:37,610
Baseball's my racket
and I'm sticking to it.

1401
00:58:38,800 --> 00:58:40,381
Come on Colonel, let's get out of here.

1402
00:58:40,381 --> 00:58:41,214
- John--

1403
00:58:41,214 --> 00:58:43,950
- [Hawkins] Please, please
I just got rid of one crowd.

1404
00:58:43,950 --> 00:58:45,460
- But please, Mr. Mayor,

1405
00:58:45,460 --> 00:58:48,620
tell him the John Doe
Club wants to talk to him.

1406
00:58:48,620 --> 00:58:50,610
- Let them in, Mr.
Mayor, let them come in.

1407
00:58:50,610 --> 00:58:52,190
- Okay folks but remember your manners.

1408
00:58:52,190 --> 00:58:53,370
No stampeding.

1409
00:58:53,370 --> 00:58:56,213
Walk slow, like you do when
you come to pay your taxes.

1410
00:59:03,360 --> 00:59:04,193
- Okay.

1411
00:59:04,193 --> 00:59:05,790
All right, give me a chance.

1412
00:59:05,790 --> 00:59:06,833
- Come right in.

1413
00:59:09,276 --> 00:59:11,270
- My name's Bert Hansen, Mr. Doe.

1414
00:59:11,270 --> 00:59:15,170
I'm the head soda-jerker
at Schwabacher's drugstore.

1415
00:59:15,170 --> 00:59:18,800
Well, sir, you see me and my
wife, we heard your broadcast

1416
00:59:18,800 --> 00:59:20,490
and we got quite a bang out of it.

1417
00:59:20,490 --> 00:59:22,000
Especially my wife.

1418
00:59:22,000 --> 00:59:23,617
Kept me up half the night saying,

1419
00:59:23,617 --> 00:59:25,077
"that man's right, honey.

1420
00:59:25,077 --> 00:59:26,127
"Trouble with the world is

1421
00:59:26,127 --> 00:59:28,237
"nobody gives a hoot about his neighbor.

1422
00:59:28,237 --> 00:59:29,567
"That's why everybody in town

1423
00:59:29,567 --> 00:59:31,550
"is sore and cranky with each other."

1424
00:59:31,550 --> 00:59:33,297
I said, "that's fine,

1425
00:59:33,297 --> 00:59:35,357
"but how's a guy gonna go around lovin'

1426
00:59:35,357 --> 00:59:37,057
"the kind of neighbors we got?

1427
00:59:37,057 --> 00:59:38,757
"Old Sourpuss, for instance."

1428
00:59:38,757 --> 00:59:39,590
(laughing)

1429
00:59:39,590 --> 00:59:43,040
You see, Sourpuss Smithers
lives alone next door to us.

1430
00:59:43,040 --> 00:59:44,800
He's a cranky old man who runs

1431
00:59:44,800 --> 00:59:46,070
the second hand furniture store.

1432
00:59:46,070 --> 00:59:47,903
We haven't spoken to him for years.

1433
00:59:48,990 --> 00:59:50,390
I always figured he was

1434
00:59:50,390 --> 00:59:52,450
an ornery old gent that hated the world

1435
00:59:52,450 --> 00:59:54,770
'cause was always slamming his garage door

1436
00:59:54,770 --> 00:59:58,122
and playing the radio so loud
it kept half the neighbors up.

1437
00:59:58,122 --> 01:00:00,350
(laughing)

1438
01:00:00,350 --> 01:00:03,430
Well anyway, the next morning
I'm out watering the lawn

1439
01:00:03,430 --> 01:00:05,060
and I look over and there's Sourpuss

1440
01:00:05,060 --> 01:00:06,240
on the other side of the hedge

1441
01:00:06,240 --> 01:00:08,800
straightening out a dent in his fender.

1442
01:00:08,800 --> 01:00:11,950
And my wife yells to me out the window,

1443
01:00:11,950 --> 01:00:14,390
she says, "go on, speak to him, Bert."

1444
01:00:14,390 --> 01:00:16,590
I figured well heck I can't lose anything,

1445
01:00:16,590 --> 01:00:20,340
so I yelled over to him,
"good morning, Mr. Smithers."

1446
01:00:20,340 --> 01:00:22,520
He went right on pounding his fender.

1447
01:00:22,520 --> 01:00:23,530
Was I burned.

1448
01:00:23,530 --> 01:00:26,720
So I turned around to
give my wife a dirty look.

1449
01:00:26,720 --> 01:00:29,950
She said, "louder, he didn't hear you."

1450
01:00:29,950 --> 01:00:31,050
So in a voice you could've heard

1451
01:00:31,050 --> 01:00:32,417
in the next county I yelled,

1452
01:00:32,417 --> 01:00:33,696
"good morning, Mr. Smithers!"

1453
01:00:33,696 --> 01:00:36,520
(laughing)

1454
01:00:36,520 --> 01:00:39,390
Well sir, you could have
knocked me over with a feather.

1455
01:00:39,390 --> 01:00:42,350
Old Sourpuss turned
around, surprised like,

1456
01:00:42,350 --> 01:00:46,090
put on a big smile, came
over and took my hand

1457
01:00:46,090 --> 01:00:49,827
like an old lodge brother and
said, "good morning, Hansen.

1458
01:00:49,827 --> 01:00:51,450
"I've been wanting to
talk to you for years

1459
01:00:51,450 --> 01:00:52,990
"only I thought you didn't like me."

1460
01:00:52,990 --> 01:00:56,410
Then he started chatting
away like a happy little kid.

1461
01:00:56,410 --> 01:00:58,117
He got so excited his eyes--

1462
01:00:59,000 --> 01:01:01,270
Well, Mr. Doe before we got through

1463
01:01:01,270 --> 01:01:04,820
I found out Smithers is a swell
egg, only he's pretty deaf

1464
01:01:04,820 --> 01:01:06,890
and that accounts for all the noises.

1465
01:01:06,890 --> 01:01:10,050
He says it's a shame how little
we know about our neighbors.

1466
01:01:10,050 --> 01:01:11,650
And then he got an idea.

1467
01:01:11,650 --> 01:01:14,267
And he said, "how's about
inviting everybody some place

1468
01:01:14,267 --> 01:01:15,707
"where we can all get together

1469
01:01:15,707 --> 01:01:18,170
"and know each other a little better?"

1470
01:01:18,170 --> 01:01:20,730
Well I'm feeling so good by
this time I ripe for anything.

1471
01:01:20,730 --> 01:01:22,440
So Smithers goes around the neighborhood

1472
01:01:22,440 --> 01:01:24,700
inviting everybody to a
meeting at the schoolhouse.

1473
01:01:24,700 --> 01:01:26,023
And I tell everybody that comes

1474
01:01:26,023 --> 01:01:29,383
into the store, including
Mr. Schwabacher my boss.

1475
01:01:30,290 --> 01:01:31,123
I'm talking too much.

1476
01:01:31,123 --> 01:01:33,253
- Oh no.
(chattering)

1477
01:01:34,990 --> 01:01:38,730
- Well, I'll be doggone if
for 40 people don't show up.

1478
01:01:38,730 --> 01:01:40,010
Course, none of us knew what to do,

1479
01:01:40,010 --> 01:01:42,900
but we sure got a kick out
of seeing how glad everybody

1480
01:01:42,900 --> 01:01:45,501
was just to say hello to one another.

1481
01:01:45,501 --> 01:01:47,220
- Tell him about making
Sourpuss chairman, honey.

1482
01:01:47,220 --> 01:01:49,200
- Oh yeah, we made Sourpuss chairman

1483
01:01:49,200 --> 01:01:51,970
and decided to call
ourselves the John Doe Club.

1484
01:01:51,970 --> 01:01:53,690
Say, incidentally, this is my wife.

1485
01:01:53,690 --> 01:01:55,330
Come here, honey.

1486
01:01:55,330 --> 01:01:57,020
This is my wife, Mr. Doe.

1487
01:01:57,020 --> 01:01:58,270
- How do you do, Mr. Doe?

1488
01:01:59,332 --> 01:02:00,891
Sourpuss is here, too.

1489
01:02:00,891 --> 01:02:02,474
- [John] Oh, is he?

1490
01:02:03,978 --> 01:02:07,480
- This is Sourpuss, or excuse
me, Mr. Smithers, Mr. Doe.

1491
01:02:07,480 --> 01:02:09,310
- That's alright, if you
didn't call me Sourpuss,

1492
01:02:09,310 --> 01:02:10,488
it wouldn't feel natural.

1493
01:02:10,488 --> 01:02:12,580
(laughing)

1494
01:02:12,580 --> 01:02:14,910
- Well anyway, I guess nearly everybody

1495
01:02:14,910 --> 01:02:17,542
in the neighborhood came
except the Delaneys.

1496
01:02:17,542 --> 01:02:19,160
The Delaneys live in a big house

1497
01:02:19,160 --> 01:02:20,630
with an iron fence around it

1498
01:02:20,630 --> 01:02:22,880
and they always keep their blinds drawn.

1499
01:02:22,880 --> 01:02:25,830
And we always figured that he
was an old miser that sat back

1500
01:02:25,830 --> 01:02:29,870
counting his money, so why
bother about inviting him?

1501
01:02:29,870 --> 01:02:33,687
Until Grimes the milkman
spoke up and he said,

1502
01:02:33,687 --> 01:02:36,600
"say, you've got the Delaneys all wrong."

1503
01:02:36,600 --> 01:02:37,700
And then he tells us about

1504
01:02:37,700 --> 01:02:39,800
how they canceled their milk last week,

1505
01:02:39,800 --> 01:02:42,300
and he found it looked
kind of curious like

1506
01:02:43,300 --> 01:02:46,237
peeked in under the blinds
and found the house was empty.

1507
01:02:46,237 --> 01:02:48,920
"If you ask me", he
says, "they're starving."

1508
01:02:48,920 --> 01:02:50,840
- Old man Delaney's been bringing

1509
01:02:50,840 --> 01:02:52,920
his furniture over to my place at night,

1510
01:02:52,920 --> 01:02:54,753
one piece at a time and selling it.

1511
01:02:56,543 --> 01:02:58,960
- Well sir a half a dozen of
us went over there to fetch him

1512
01:02:58,960 --> 01:03:00,377
and we brought him to the meeting.

1513
01:03:00,377 --> 01:03:02,350
And what a reception they got.

1514
01:03:02,350 --> 01:03:06,040
Everybody shook hands with
them and made a fuss over them.

1515
01:03:06,040 --> 01:03:09,500
Well, finally Mr. and Mrs.
Delaney sat right down and cried.

1516
01:03:09,500 --> 01:03:10,333
- [Smithers] Then we started to find

1517
01:03:10,333 --> 01:03:11,580
out about a lot of other people.

1518
01:03:11,580 --> 01:03:13,942
- Yeah sure, well you know
Grubbel, for instance.

1519
01:03:13,942 --> 01:03:14,892
- Grubbel's here.

1520
01:03:14,892 --> 01:03:15,725
See?

1521
01:03:15,725 --> 01:03:17,818
- Yeah, that's him.

1522
01:03:17,818 --> 01:03:20,130
You don't know Grubbel, but he's the man

1523
01:03:20,130 --> 01:03:23,010
that everybody figured
was the worst no account

1524
01:03:23,010 --> 01:03:26,650
in the neighborhood because
he lived like a hermit.

1525
01:03:26,650 --> 01:03:28,760
Nobody would have anything to do with him

1526
01:03:28,760 --> 01:03:31,313
that is until Murphy the
postman told us the truth.

1527
01:03:32,237 --> 01:03:35,007
"Grubbel," he says, "he
lives out of garbage cans

1528
01:03:35,007 --> 01:03:36,487
"because he won't take charity.

1529
01:03:36,487 --> 01:03:39,720
"'Cause it'd ruin his
self respect" he says.

1530
01:03:39,720 --> 01:03:41,280
- Just like you said
on the radio, Mr. Doe.

1531
01:03:41,280 --> 01:03:44,300
- Well sir, about a dozen
families got together

1532
01:03:44,300 --> 01:03:46,100
and gave Grubbel a job
watering their lawns.

1533
01:03:46,100 --> 01:03:47,240
Isn't that wonderful?

1534
01:03:47,240 --> 01:03:49,470
And then we found jobs
for six other people

1535
01:03:49,470 --> 01:03:51,540
and they've all gone off relief.

1536
01:03:51,540 --> 01:03:53,060
- And my boss, Mr. Schwabacher,

1537
01:03:53,060 --> 01:03:55,280
made a job in his warehouse
for old man Delaney.

1538
01:03:55,280 --> 01:03:56,880
- And he gave you that $5 raise.

1539
01:03:56,880 --> 01:03:58,977
- Yeah, wasn't that swell?

1540
01:04:00,823 --> 01:04:03,160
- Why, Bert, I feel slighted.

1541
01:04:03,160 --> 01:04:06,170
I'd like to join, but nobody asked me.

1542
01:04:06,170 --> 01:04:08,570
- I'm sorry, Mayor, but we voted

1543
01:04:08,570 --> 01:04:10,490
that no politician could join.

1544
01:04:10,490 --> 01:04:13,023
- Just the John Does of the neighborhood.

1545
01:04:13,023 --> 01:04:14,683
You know how politicians are.

1546
01:04:14,683 --> 01:04:18,300
(laughing)
- Yeah.

1547
01:04:18,300 --> 01:04:22,030
- Well the reason we wanted
to tell you this, Mr. Doe,

1548
01:04:22,030 --> 01:04:25,950
was to give you an idea
of what you started.

1549
01:04:25,950 --> 01:04:28,240
From where I'm sitting, I don't see

1550
01:04:28,240 --> 01:04:31,059
any sense in you jumping off any building.

1551
01:04:31,059 --> 01:04:32,810
- [Group] No, no.

1552
01:04:32,810 --> 01:04:34,513
- Well, thank you for listening.

1553
01:04:36,070 --> 01:04:37,213
Goodbye, Mr. Doe.

1554
01:04:38,550 --> 01:04:40,230
You're a wonderful man.

1555
01:04:40,230 --> 01:04:42,210
It strikes me you could be mighty useful

1556
01:04:42,210 --> 01:04:43,703
walking around for a while.

1557
01:04:44,822 --> 01:04:47,239
- Goodbye.
- Goodbye, Mr. Doe.

1558
01:04:54,295 --> 01:04:56,970
- I'm Mrs. Delaney, Mr. Doe.

1559
01:04:56,970 --> 01:04:58,723
God bless you, my boy.

1560
01:05:32,998 --> 01:05:35,331
- Gee wiz, I'm all mixed up.

1561
01:05:38,750 --> 01:05:39,693
I don't get it.

1562
01:05:41,290 --> 01:05:42,670
Look, all those swell people

1563
01:05:42,670 --> 01:05:44,860
think I'm gonna jump off
a building or something.

1564
01:05:44,860 --> 01:05:46,513
I never had any such idea.

1565
01:05:49,400 --> 01:05:50,233
Gosh.

1566
01:05:51,220 --> 01:05:52,930
A fella would have to be
a fine example himself

1567
01:05:52,930 --> 01:05:55,740
to go around telling other people how to--

1568
01:05:55,740 --> 01:05:56,820
Say look, what happened the other night

1569
01:05:56,820 --> 01:05:58,220
was on account of Miss Mitchell here.

1570
01:05:58,220 --> 01:05:59,951
She wrote the stuff.

1571
01:05:59,951 --> 01:06:02,420
- Don't you see what a
wonderful thing this can be?

1572
01:06:02,420 --> 01:06:04,410
But we need you, John.
- You're hooked.

1573
01:06:04,410 --> 01:06:06,630
I can see that right now, they got you.

1574
01:06:06,630 --> 01:06:07,773
Well, I'm through.

1575
01:06:10,480 --> 01:06:11,970
For three years I've
been trying to get you

1576
01:06:11,970 --> 01:06:13,320
up to the Columbia River country.

1577
01:06:13,320 --> 01:06:15,890
First it was your glass
arm, then it was the radio

1578
01:06:15,890 --> 01:06:17,550
and now it's the John Doe Clubs.

1579
01:06:17,550 --> 01:06:19,400
Well, I ain't waiting another minute.

1580
01:06:20,708 --> 01:06:22,430
(chattering)

1581
01:06:22,430 --> 01:06:24,330
Gangway, you heelots!

1582
01:06:24,330 --> 01:06:26,000
(gasping)

1583
01:06:26,000 --> 01:06:27,319
- Hey Colonel, wait a minute.

1584
01:06:27,319 --> 01:06:30,066
(chattering)
Hey, Colonel!

1585
01:06:30,066 --> 01:06:31,280
- I want you personally to go along

1586
01:06:31,280 --> 01:06:32,490
with John Doe and Miss Mitchell

1587
01:06:32,490 --> 01:06:33,930
and handle the press and the radio.

1588
01:06:33,930 --> 01:06:34,763
- Me?
- Yes.

1589
01:06:34,763 --> 01:06:35,850
I won't take any chances.

1590
01:06:35,850 --> 01:06:37,100
And Johnson.
- Yes, D.B.?

1591
01:06:37,100 --> 01:06:38,800
- Your crew will do the mop up job.

1592
01:06:38,800 --> 01:06:40,350
They'll follow John Doe into every town,

1593
01:06:40,350 --> 01:06:41,930
see that the clubs are properly organized

1594
01:06:41,930 --> 01:06:43,520
and the charters issued.
- Right.

1595
01:06:43,520 --> 01:06:45,120
- There are eight flags up there now.

1596
01:06:45,120 --> 01:06:48,115
I want to see that map
covered before we get through.

1597
01:06:48,115 --> 01:06:51,615
(upbeat orchestral music)

1598
01:07:01,850 --> 01:07:04,100
(cheering)

1599
01:07:31,680 --> 01:07:35,013
(upbeat acoustic music)

1600
01:07:36,067 --> 01:07:38,716
(applauding)

1601
01:07:38,716 --> 01:07:43,549
(parade music)
(cheering)

1602
01:07:52,962 --> 01:07:54,850
- This has grown like wildfire.

1603
01:07:54,850 --> 01:07:56,413
If they made demands I'd understand it,

1604
01:07:56,413 --> 01:07:59,330
but the John Does ask for nothing.

1605
01:07:59,330 --> 01:08:00,640
- People are going off relief.

1606
01:08:00,640 --> 01:08:02,501
If this keeps up, I'll be out of a job.

1607
01:08:02,501 --> 01:08:03,560
- As soon as he gets strong enough

1608
01:08:03,560 --> 01:08:05,098
we'll find out what he wants.

1609
01:08:05,098 --> 01:08:07,870
30 every Thursday, 60 at 60.

1610
01:08:07,870 --> 01:08:09,530
Who knows what?

1611
01:08:09,530 --> 01:08:10,403
- I'm sorry, boss, they just won't let

1612
01:08:10,403 --> 01:08:11,963
anybody talk politics
with him, it's crazy.

1613
01:08:11,963 --> 01:08:13,865
- We've got to get to them.

1614
01:08:13,865 --> 01:08:16,646
They represent millions of voters.

1615
01:08:16,646 --> 01:08:19,063
(applauding)

1616
01:08:24,860 --> 01:08:26,070
- I tell you ladies and gentlemen,

1617
01:08:26,070 --> 01:08:28,370
this thing has been nothing
short of a prairie fire.

1618
01:08:28,370 --> 01:08:30,470
We've received so many
applications for charters

1619
01:08:30,470 --> 01:08:31,303
for the John Doe clubs we haven't

1620
01:08:31,303 --> 01:08:32,720
been able to take care of them.

1621
01:08:32,720 --> 01:08:34,603
- [Man] I'd hate to have
that many pins stuck in me.

1622
01:08:34,603 --> 01:08:35,580
(laughing)

1623
01:08:35,580 --> 01:08:37,250
- This John Doe convention is a natural.

1624
01:08:37,250 --> 01:08:39,420
It's going to put our city on the map.

1625
01:08:39,420 --> 01:08:41,320
Why over 2,400 John Doe clubs

1626
01:08:41,320 --> 01:08:43,440
are sending delegates,
can you imagine that?

1627
01:08:43,440 --> 01:08:45,010
You Mr. Mayor will be the official host.

1628
01:08:45,010 --> 01:08:46,800
You will make the arrangements
for decorating the city,

1629
01:08:46,800 --> 01:08:49,290
parades and a reception for
John Doe when he gets home.

1630
01:08:49,290 --> 01:08:51,177
And don't wear your high hat.

1631
01:08:51,177 --> 01:08:52,810
- No high hat?
- No high hat.

1632
01:08:52,810 --> 01:08:54,610
And from you Connell I want a special

1633
01:08:54,610 --> 01:08:57,400
John Doe edition every day
until the convention is over.

1634
01:08:57,400 --> 01:08:59,820
And now please step into the outer office

1635
01:08:59,820 --> 01:09:00,740
and look your prettiest

1636
01:09:00,740 --> 01:09:02,030
because there are photographers there

1637
01:09:02,030 --> 01:09:03,445
to take pictures of this committee.

1638
01:09:03,445 --> 01:09:05,350
- [Man] Don't worry, D.B.,
everything will be taken care of.

1639
01:09:05,350 --> 01:09:07,205
- Sure.
- Isn't it all too wonderful?

1640
01:09:07,205 --> 01:09:10,190
(chattering)

1641
01:09:10,190 --> 01:09:11,081
- [Photographer] Oh Mr.
Mayor, would you sit down

1642
01:09:11,081 --> 01:09:11,967
in the front row please?

1643
01:09:11,967 --> 01:09:13,910
You ladies get close to him.

1644
01:09:13,910 --> 01:09:16,010
- Well, I don't get it.
- Get what?

1645
01:09:16,010 --> 01:09:18,610
- Look D.B. I'm supposed
to know my way around.

1646
01:09:18,610 --> 01:09:20,860
This John Doe movement's
cost you a fortune.

1647
01:09:20,860 --> 01:09:23,020
Now this convention is
going to cost plenty.

1648
01:09:23,020 --> 01:09:23,853
- Well?

1649
01:09:24,920 --> 01:09:25,817
- I'm stuck with two and two

1650
01:09:25,817 --> 01:09:28,265
and I'm a sucker if I
can make four out of it.

1651
01:09:28,265 --> 01:09:29,315
Where do you come in?

1652
01:09:32,988 --> 01:09:34,870
- I have the satisfaction of knowing

1653
01:09:34,870 --> 01:09:37,393
that my money has been
spent for a worthy cause.

1654
01:09:38,288 --> 01:09:41,330
- I see, I better stick
to running the paper.

1655
01:09:41,330 --> 01:09:42,880
- I think maybe you better had.

1656
01:09:47,094 --> 01:09:50,580
Connell, I'd like to have
the John Doe contract,

1657
01:09:50,580 --> 01:09:51,700
all of the receipts for the money

1658
01:09:51,700 --> 01:09:53,430
we advanced for the
letter Miss Mitchell wrote

1659
01:09:53,430 --> 01:09:55,477
for which I gave her a $1,000.

1660
01:09:56,540 --> 01:09:57,633
- Yes, sure.

1661
01:09:59,280 --> 01:10:01,810
- Well we leave for the
airport in half an hour.

1662
01:10:01,810 --> 01:10:03,150
Is that Johnny boy's room?

1663
01:10:03,150 --> 01:10:03,983
Better hustle him up.

1664
01:10:03,983 --> 01:10:05,270
- He'll be ready on
time, he's packing now.

1665
01:10:05,270 --> 01:10:06,103
- Ah good.

1666
01:10:06,103 --> 01:10:08,020
Did you see his picture
on the cover of Time?

1667
01:10:08,020 --> 01:10:08,940
- Yeah.

1668
01:10:08,940 --> 01:10:10,400
- I gotta give you credit, Annie girl.

1669
01:10:10,400 --> 01:10:12,670
I've handled a good many
big promotions in my time,

1670
01:10:12,670 --> 01:10:14,710
from the World's Fair
to a channel swimmer,

1671
01:10:14,710 --> 01:10:17,750
but this one has
certainly got me spinning.

1672
01:10:17,750 --> 01:10:20,150
And now, a John Doe convention.

1673
01:10:20,150 --> 01:10:20,983
Wow.

1674
01:10:21,870 --> 01:10:23,370
Hey, if you could only get him

1675
01:10:23,370 --> 01:10:26,660
to jump off City Hall
roof on Christmas Eve,

1676
01:10:26,660 --> 01:10:28,909
I'd guarantee you a half
a million people there.

1677
01:10:28,909 --> 01:10:30,491
- Charlie?
- Hmm?

1678
01:10:30,491 --> 01:10:32,123
- What do you make of him?

1679
01:10:32,123 --> 01:10:33,113
- Who, Johnny boy?

1680
01:10:34,270 --> 01:10:36,663
Well, I don't know what angle you want

1681
01:10:36,663 --> 01:10:38,330
but I'll give it to you quick.

1682
01:10:38,330 --> 01:10:41,720
Number one, he's got great yokel
appeal but he's a nice guy.

1683
01:10:41,720 --> 01:10:44,070
Number two, he's beginning
to believe he really wrote

1684
01:10:44,070 --> 01:10:46,410
that original suicide
letter that you made up.

1685
01:10:46,410 --> 01:10:49,780
Number three, he thinks you're
Joan of Arc or something.

1686
01:10:49,780 --> 01:10:50,613
- Yeah I know.

1687
01:10:50,613 --> 01:10:54,610
- [Charlie] Number four, well,
you know what number four is.

1688
01:10:54,610 --> 01:10:55,620
He's nuts about you.

1689
01:10:55,620 --> 01:10:57,660
Yeah it's running out of his ears.

1690
01:10:57,660 --> 01:10:58,950
- You left out number five.

1691
01:10:58,950 --> 01:11:00,653
We're all heels, me especially.

1692
01:11:01,731 --> 01:11:02,881
- [Charlie] Holy smoke.

1693
01:11:03,800 --> 01:11:04,633
- Come in.

1694
01:11:10,761 --> 01:11:11,594
- [John] I'm all packed.

1695
01:11:11,594 --> 01:11:13,083
- Good, I'll go and get Beany boy.

1696
01:11:14,280 --> 01:11:16,091
- Okay, Charlie boy.

1697
01:11:16,091 --> 01:11:18,341
(laughing)

1698
01:11:29,290 --> 01:11:31,496
Do you care if I sit down, out here?

1699
01:11:31,496 --> 01:11:36,496
- [Ann] No.

1700
01:11:43,080 --> 01:11:46,043
- You know, I had a
crazy dream last night.

1701
01:11:48,920 --> 01:11:50,337
It was about you.

1702
01:11:51,518 --> 01:11:52,435
- About me?

1703
01:11:53,660 --> 01:11:54,493
- Sure was crazy.

1704
01:11:54,493 --> 01:11:56,323
I dreamt I was your father.

1705
01:12:01,846 --> 01:12:04,973
There was something I was
trying to stop you from doing.

1706
01:12:06,010 --> 01:12:10,360
So, I got up out of
bed, and I walked right

1707
01:12:10,360 --> 01:12:13,600
through the wall here, right
straight into your room.

1708
01:12:13,600 --> 01:12:14,833
You know how dreams are.

1709
01:12:16,190 --> 01:12:19,010
And there you were in bed,
but you were a little girl,

1710
01:12:19,010 --> 01:12:21,963
you know about 10 and very pretty, too.

1711
01:12:23,590 --> 01:12:26,810
So I shook you and the moment you opened

1712
01:12:26,810 --> 01:12:28,680
your eyes, you hopped out of bed

1713
01:12:28,680 --> 01:12:29,821
and started running like the devil.

1714
01:12:29,821 --> 01:12:30,654
In your nightgown.

1715
01:12:30,654 --> 01:12:31,487
You ran right out the window there

1716
01:12:31,487 --> 01:12:33,270
and you ran over the tops of buildings

1717
01:12:33,270 --> 01:12:36,187
and roofs and everything for
miles and I was chasing you.

1718
01:12:38,200 --> 01:12:40,440
And all the time you were running,

1719
01:12:40,440 --> 01:12:42,570
you kept growing bigger
and bigger and bigger.

1720
01:12:42,570 --> 01:12:44,080
And pretty soon you were as big

1721
01:12:44,080 --> 01:12:46,833
as you are now, you know, grown up?

1722
01:12:47,690 --> 01:12:50,620
And all the time I kept asking myself,

1723
01:12:50,620 --> 01:12:51,993
what am I chasing her for?

1724
01:12:53,199 --> 01:12:54,533
And I didn't know.

1725
01:12:56,302 --> 01:12:57,352
Isn't that a hot one?

1726
01:12:59,040 --> 01:13:01,570
Well anyway, you ran into some place

1727
01:13:01,570 --> 01:13:02,640
and then I ran in after you.

1728
01:13:02,640 --> 01:13:07,253
And when I got there there
you were getting married.

1729
01:13:10,519 --> 01:13:14,103
The nightgown had changed
into a beautiful wedding gown.

1730
01:13:16,651 --> 01:13:18,051
You sure looked pretty, too.

1731
01:13:19,570 --> 01:13:22,420
And then I knew what I was
trying to stop you from doing.

1732
01:13:23,700 --> 01:13:26,210
Dreams are sure crazy, aren't they?

1733
01:13:26,210 --> 01:13:31,210
Well, would you like to know
who it was you were marrying?

1734
01:13:32,043 --> 01:13:35,683
- Oh, a tall handsome Ubangi, I suppose.

1735
01:13:36,759 --> 01:13:37,670
- [John] No, not that bad.

1736
01:13:37,670 --> 01:13:40,360
It was the fella that sends
you flowers every day.

1737
01:13:40,360 --> 01:13:41,330
Yeah, what's his name?

1738
01:13:41,330 --> 01:13:42,583
Mr. Norton's nephew?

1739
01:13:44,550 --> 01:13:46,443
- Ted Sheldon.
- Yeah, that's the one.

1740
01:13:47,550 --> 01:13:50,200
- But here's the funniest part of it all.

1741
01:13:50,200 --> 01:13:52,120
I was the fella up there
doing the marrying.

1742
01:13:52,120 --> 01:13:54,230
You know, the Justice of
the Peace or something.

1743
01:13:54,230 --> 01:13:55,136
- You were?

1744
01:13:55,136 --> 01:13:56,750
I thought you were chasing me.

1745
01:13:56,750 --> 01:13:59,733
- Well, yes I was, but I was
your father then you see?

1746
01:14:00,610 --> 01:14:05,140
But the real me, John Doe,
that is Long John Willoughby,

1747
01:14:05,140 --> 01:14:08,040
I was the fella with a book.

1748
01:14:08,040 --> 01:14:10,410
You know what I mean?
- I guess so.

1749
01:14:10,410 --> 01:14:11,940
Then what happened?

1750
01:14:11,940 --> 01:14:16,546
- Well I took you across my
knee and I started spanking you.

1751
01:14:16,546 --> 01:14:19,090
That is, I didn't do
it, I mean I did do it.

1752
01:14:19,090 --> 01:14:21,393
But it wasn't me, see,
I was your father then.

1753
01:14:23,040 --> 01:14:25,567
Well I had you across my knee and I said,

1754
01:14:25,567 --> 01:14:30,493
"Annie, I won't allow you to
marry a man that's just rich

1755
01:14:31,867 --> 01:14:35,157
"or that has his secretary
send you flowers.

1756
01:14:35,157 --> 01:14:38,047
"The man you marry has got
to swim rivers for you,

1757
01:14:38,047 --> 01:14:40,667
"he's got to climb high mountains for you,

1758
01:14:40,667 --> 01:14:42,227
"he's got to slay dragons for you.

1759
01:14:42,227 --> 01:14:45,780
"He's got to perform
wonderful deeds for you."

1760
01:14:45,780 --> 01:14:47,660
Yes sir, and all the time,

1761
01:14:47,660 --> 01:14:50,170
the guy up there you
know with the book, me,

1762
01:14:50,170 --> 01:14:52,937
just stood there noddin'
his head and he said,

1763
01:14:52,937 --> 01:14:54,287
"go to it Pop, whack her one for me

1764
01:14:54,287 --> 01:14:57,060
"because that's the way
I feel about it too."

1765
01:14:57,910 --> 01:15:01,180
So he says, "come down
and whack her yourself."

1766
01:15:01,180 --> 01:15:04,410
So I came down and I
whacked you a good one, see?

1767
01:15:04,410 --> 01:15:06,930
The he whacked you and I
whacked you another one.

1768
01:15:06,930 --> 01:15:08,867
We both started whacking you like--

1769
01:15:10,609 --> 01:15:11,650
- Well if you're through whacking her

1770
01:15:11,650 --> 01:15:12,840
come on let's get going.

1771
01:15:12,840 --> 01:15:14,960
Okay fellas, right in here.

1772
01:15:14,960 --> 01:15:15,793
You go out the side entrance.

1773
01:15:15,793 --> 01:15:18,330
There's a bunch of
autograph seekers out front.

1774
01:15:18,330 --> 01:15:20,843
We'll be down with the bags in a minute.

1775
01:15:22,050 --> 01:15:24,860
Come on, don't make a
Government project out of this.

1776
01:15:27,910 --> 01:15:29,280
Hi, Beany.

1777
01:15:29,280 --> 01:15:31,119
- When's our plane take off again?

1778
01:15:31,119 --> 01:15:32,697
- Couple of minutes.

1779
01:15:32,697 --> 01:15:35,970
- How many people do you
think we've talked to already,

1780
01:15:35,970 --> 01:15:37,502
outside the radio, I mean?

1781
01:15:37,502 --> 01:15:40,822
- Oh, I don't know, about 300,000.

1782
01:15:40,822 --> 01:15:41,655
- 300,000.

1783
01:15:43,380 --> 01:15:44,730
What makes them do it, Ann?

1784
01:15:45,720 --> 01:15:47,156
What makes them come and listen

1785
01:15:47,156 --> 01:15:50,663
and get up their John Doe
Clubs the way they do?

1786
01:15:52,832 --> 01:15:53,820
I've been trying to figure it out.

1787
01:15:53,820 --> 01:15:56,960
- Look, John, what we're
handing them are platitudes

1788
01:15:56,960 --> 01:15:58,700
things they've heard a million times.

1789
01:15:58,700 --> 01:16:00,850
Love thy neighbor, clouds
have silver linings,

1790
01:16:00,850 --> 01:16:01,683
turn the other cheek.

1791
01:16:01,683 --> 01:16:02,516
It's just--

1792
01:16:02,516 --> 01:16:03,683
- Yeah I heard 'em a million times too,

1793
01:16:03,683 --> 01:16:08,683
but they are, maybe they're like me,

1794
01:16:08,920 --> 01:16:13,623
just beginning to get an
idea what those things mean.

1795
01:16:13,623 --> 01:16:15,560
I never really thought
much about people before.

1796
01:16:15,560 --> 01:16:18,173
They were always just somebody
to fill up the bleachers.

1797
01:16:19,050 --> 01:16:21,160
Only time I worried
about them is if they--

1798
01:16:21,160 --> 01:16:23,273
is when they didn't see me pitch.

1799
01:16:25,110 --> 01:16:27,520
You know, lately I've been

1800
01:16:27,520 --> 01:16:29,220
watching them when I talk to them.

1801
01:16:30,210 --> 01:16:32,033
I see something in their faces.

1802
01:16:33,570 --> 01:16:38,473
I could feel that they
were hungry for something.

1803
01:16:40,083 --> 01:16:41,400
You know what I mean?

1804
01:16:41,400 --> 01:16:42,610
Maybe that's why they came.

1805
01:16:42,610 --> 01:16:44,800
Maybe they're just lonely

1806
01:16:46,280 --> 01:16:48,080
and wanted somebody to say hello to.

1807
01:16:49,844 --> 01:16:51,945
I know how they feel.

1808
01:16:51,945 --> 01:16:54,283
I've been lonely and hungry for something

1809
01:16:54,283 --> 01:16:59,283
practically all my life.

1810
01:17:02,680 --> 01:17:04,530
- [Man] All aboard, folks.

1811
01:17:04,530 --> 01:17:05,420
- We'll have the city behind us.

1812
01:17:05,420 --> 01:17:06,757
- Yeah, that's true.

1813
01:17:07,694 --> 01:17:09,004
Oh, somebody else sitting here?

1814
01:17:09,004 --> 01:17:11,072
- No, that's your seat.

1815
01:17:11,072 --> 01:17:13,204
- And this is your coat.

1816
01:17:13,204 --> 01:17:14,340
- Mine?

1817
01:17:14,340 --> 01:17:17,086
- A little token of appreciation.

1818
01:17:17,086 --> 01:17:17,919
- Oh.

1819
01:17:19,993 --> 01:17:22,010
Oh, it's beautiful, D.B.

1820
01:17:22,010 --> 01:17:25,057
Well, I don't quite know what to say.

1821
01:17:25,057 --> 01:17:28,890
- Oh don't say anything
at all, just sit down.

1822
01:17:31,490 --> 01:17:33,163
- Go ahead and open it, open it.

1823
01:17:36,932 --> 01:17:37,765
- Oh.

1824
01:17:41,285 --> 01:17:42,770
Oh it's lovely.

1825
01:17:42,770 --> 01:17:44,593
- And a new contract goes with it.

1826
01:17:48,892 --> 01:17:51,337
- Well, come on spring it.

1827
01:17:51,337 --> 01:17:53,940
You've got something on your mind.

1828
01:17:53,940 --> 01:17:56,190
Must be stupendous.
(laughing)

1829
01:17:56,190 --> 01:17:57,863
- You know that's what I like about her.

1830
01:17:57,863 --> 01:18:02,152
Right to the point like that.

1831
01:18:02,152 --> 01:18:04,780
Alright practical Annie, here it is.

1832
01:18:04,780 --> 01:18:07,527
Tomorrow night, before
a crowd of 15,000 people

1833
01:18:07,527 --> 01:18:10,370
and talking over a
nationwide radio hook-up,

1834
01:18:10,370 --> 01:18:14,712
John Doe will announce the
formation of a third party.

1835
01:18:14,712 --> 01:18:16,030
- A third party?

1836
01:18:16,030 --> 01:18:18,610
- Yes, the John Doe Party,

1837
01:18:18,610 --> 01:18:20,170
devoted entirely to the interests

1838
01:18:20,170 --> 01:18:23,280
of all the John Does all over the country.

1839
01:18:23,280 --> 01:18:27,733
Which practically means 90% of the voters.

1840
01:18:28,590 --> 01:18:30,899
He will also announce the third party's

1841
01:18:30,899 --> 01:18:33,316
candidate for the presidency.

1842
01:18:34,505 --> 01:18:37,280
A man who he personally recommends.

1843
01:18:37,280 --> 01:18:38,750
A great humanitarian.

1844
01:18:38,750 --> 01:18:40,670
The best friend the John Does have.

1845
01:18:42,621 --> 01:18:44,395
- Mr. D.B. Norton.

1846
01:18:44,395 --> 01:18:49,395
- Yes.

1847
01:18:51,630 --> 01:18:52,463
- Wow.

1848
01:18:53,813 --> 01:18:58,043
♪ Glory, glory, hallelujah ♪

1849
01:18:58,043 --> 01:19:02,560
♪ Glory, glory, hallelujah ♪

1850
01:19:02,560 --> 01:19:06,587
♪ Glory, glory, hallelujah ♪

1851
01:19:06,587 --> 01:19:10,688
♪ His truth is marching on ♪

1852
01:19:10,688 --> 01:19:12,938
(cheering)

1853
01:19:18,389 --> 01:19:19,222
- [Radio Broadcaster]
And although the opening

1854
01:19:19,222 --> 01:19:20,880
of the convention is
hours off the delegates

1855
01:19:20,880 --> 01:19:23,350
are already pouring into
the ball park by the droves

1856
01:19:23,350 --> 01:19:26,490
with lunch baskets, banners
and petitions asking John Doe

1857
01:19:26,490 --> 01:19:28,155
not to jump off any roof.

1858
01:19:28,155 --> 01:19:28,988
- [Radio Broadcaster] No
matter how you look at it,

1859
01:19:28,988 --> 01:19:31,280
it's still a phenomenal movement.

1860
01:19:31,280 --> 01:19:33,500
These John Does or the hoy polloi,

1861
01:19:33,500 --> 01:19:34,763
as you've heard people call them,

1862
01:19:34,763 --> 01:19:36,910
have been laughed at and ridiculed,

1863
01:19:36,910 --> 01:19:39,080
but here they are gay and happy,

1864
01:19:39,080 --> 01:19:42,340
having traveled 1,000s of
miles, their expenses paid

1865
01:19:42,340 --> 01:19:44,960
by their neighbors to come to pay homage

1866
01:19:44,960 --> 01:19:46,810
to their hero John Doe.

1867
01:19:46,810 --> 01:19:49,520
- And in these days of wars and bombings,

1868
01:19:49,520 --> 01:19:51,279
it's a hopeful sign that a simple idea

1869
01:19:51,279 --> 01:19:53,200
like this can sweep the country.

1870
01:19:53,200 --> 01:19:57,040
An idea based on friendliness,
on giving and not taking,

1871
01:19:57,040 --> 01:19:59,880
on helping your neighbor and
asking nothing in return.

1872
01:19:59,880 --> 01:20:01,800
And if a thing like this can happen,

1873
01:20:01,800 --> 01:20:03,660
don't let any of your grumbling friends

1874
01:20:03,660 --> 01:20:06,700
tell you that humanity is falling apart.

1875
01:20:06,700 --> 01:20:09,090
This is John B. Hughes signing off now,

1876
01:20:09,090 --> 01:20:10,570
returning you to our main studio

1877
01:20:10,570 --> 01:20:13,603
until nine o'clock when the
convention will officially open.

1878
01:20:15,740 --> 01:20:17,140
- [Woman] Oh, John, come in.

1879
01:20:18,410 --> 01:20:19,720
- Say, I'm kind of...

1880
01:20:20,901 --> 01:20:22,651
It's raining out a little.
- That's all right.

1881
01:20:22,651 --> 01:20:25,679
It's good to see you, sit down.

1882
01:20:25,679 --> 01:20:26,512
- Thanks.

1883
01:20:42,030 --> 01:20:43,390
It's for Ann.

1884
01:20:43,390 --> 01:20:46,150
- [Woman] Oh how nice,
thank you very much.

1885
01:20:46,150 --> 01:20:46,983
- Flowers.

1886
01:20:48,540 --> 01:20:50,340
- I'm terribly sorry she isn't here.

1887
01:20:51,250 --> 01:20:52,083
- She isn't?

1888
01:20:52,083 --> 01:20:54,300
- No, she just left, I'm
surprised you didn't run into her.

1889
01:20:54,300 --> 01:20:56,100
She went over to Mr. Norton's house.

1890
01:20:56,936 --> 01:20:57,769
- Oh.

1891
01:20:59,530 --> 01:21:02,460
- Did you want to see her
about something important?

1892
01:21:02,460 --> 01:21:04,483
- Yeah, well, no, it'll wait.

1893
01:21:06,486 --> 01:21:07,820
Say he's a nice man, isn't he?

1894
01:21:07,820 --> 01:21:09,110
Mr. Norton, I mean.

1895
01:21:10,390 --> 01:21:12,063
He's done a lot for the...

1896
01:21:18,800 --> 01:21:21,763
Say, my coat's pretty wet.

1897
01:21:21,763 --> 01:21:23,753
I'm afraid I might have
wet the couch a little.

1898
01:21:29,573 --> 01:21:31,797
I'll I guess I'll see her
at the convention later.

1899
01:21:31,797 --> 01:21:35,068
- Yes of course, I'll see
that she gets the flowers.

1900
01:21:35,068 --> 01:21:35,901
- Thanks.

1901
01:21:35,901 --> 01:21:38,243
Good night, Mrs. Mitchell.
- Good night, John.

1902
01:21:44,863 --> 01:21:49,196
- Say Mrs. Mitchell I'm
kind of glad Ann isn't here.

1903
01:21:51,726 --> 01:21:56,150
You see, I came over here
hoping to see her alone

1904
01:21:56,150 --> 01:21:59,890
and kind of hoping I wouldn't
too you know what I mean?

1905
01:21:59,890 --> 01:22:02,513
There was something I
wanted to talk to her about.

1906
01:22:05,740 --> 01:22:07,413
Well it can wait, I guess.

1907
01:22:09,230 --> 01:22:10,983
- Good night.
- Good night, John.

1908
01:22:15,154 --> 01:22:18,780
- Say look Mrs. Mitchell,
have you ever been married?

1909
01:22:18,780 --> 01:22:21,000
Well, sure you have, gosh.

1910
01:22:21,000 --> 01:22:22,000
That's pretty silly.

1911
01:22:23,490 --> 01:22:25,790
Well I guess you must
think I'm kind of batty.

1912
01:22:27,475 --> 01:22:30,013
Well I guess I better be going at that.

1913
01:22:31,920 --> 01:22:35,753
- John, my husband said,

1914
01:22:35,753 --> 01:22:37,587
"I love you, will you marry me?"

1915
01:22:38,440 --> 01:22:39,273
- He did?

1916
01:22:40,650 --> 01:22:41,890
- What happened?

1917
01:22:41,890 --> 01:22:42,727
- I married him.

1918
01:22:44,520 --> 01:22:46,951
- Yeah, that's what I mean, see?

1919
01:22:46,951 --> 01:22:48,401
It was easy as all that, huh?

1920
01:22:50,300 --> 01:22:51,813
Yeah, but look Mrs. Mitchell.

1921
01:22:53,210 --> 01:22:56,200
You know I love Ann, and
it's gonna be awfully hard

1922
01:22:56,200 --> 01:23:01,200
for me to say it 'cause well
you know she's so wonderful

1923
01:23:01,600 --> 01:23:04,813
and well the best I ever was
was a bush league pitcher.

1924
01:23:06,540 --> 01:23:07,643
And you know I think she's in love

1925
01:23:07,643 --> 01:23:11,120
with another man, the one she made up.

1926
01:23:11,120 --> 01:23:12,593
You know, the real John Doe.

1927
01:23:13,605 --> 01:23:16,510
Well that's pretty tough competition.

1928
01:23:16,510 --> 01:23:18,030
I bet he'd know how to say it alright.

1929
01:23:18,030 --> 01:23:22,433
But me, I get up to it and
around and in back of it,

1930
01:23:22,433 --> 01:23:25,060
but I never get right to it.

1931
01:23:25,060 --> 01:23:26,810
You know what I mean?

1932
01:23:26,810 --> 01:23:28,950
So the only chance I've got is,

1933
01:23:28,950 --> 01:23:32,200
well, if somebody could
kind of give her a warning.

1934
01:23:32,200 --> 01:23:34,733
Sort of prepare her for the shock?

1935
01:23:36,180 --> 01:23:37,880
- You mean you'd like me to do it?

1936
01:23:39,520 --> 01:23:43,273
- Well, I was thinking,
yeah sort of break the ice.

1937
01:23:45,291 --> 01:23:46,541
- Of course I will, John.

1938
01:23:50,131 --> 01:23:51,481
- Thank you, Mrs. Mitchell.

1939
01:23:53,092 --> 01:23:53,942
Hey, you're okay.

1940
01:24:06,420 --> 01:24:07,560
- This John Doe meeting is gonna

1941
01:24:07,560 --> 01:24:09,232
be one of the biggest
things ever happened.

1942
01:24:09,232 --> 01:24:10,065
- Is that so?

1943
01:24:10,065 --> 01:24:12,500
- Why they're coming from all
over, trains, boxcars, wagons.

1944
01:24:12,500 --> 01:24:13,870
Look out!

1945
01:24:13,870 --> 01:24:15,670
- Hello, bodyguards, hey
have your dinner yet?

1946
01:24:15,670 --> 01:24:17,310
- Not yet.
- Oh that's all right.

1947
01:24:17,310 --> 01:24:18,430
- No, go and have dinner.

1948
01:24:18,430 --> 01:24:19,563
- Wait a minute, John.

1949
01:24:24,460 --> 01:24:27,110
- Hello, Mr. Connell.
- How are you, John?

1950
01:24:27,110 --> 01:24:29,363
John, I want to have a
little talk with you.

1951
01:24:31,380 --> 01:24:32,213
What's the matter?

1952
01:24:32,213 --> 01:24:33,294
Are you falling?

1953
01:24:33,294 --> 01:24:34,560
Come on.

1954
01:24:34,560 --> 01:24:36,800
- Hey, Boss.
- Oh, quiet, quiet, quiet.

1955
01:24:36,800 --> 01:24:38,697
So tell me something, did you read

1956
01:24:38,697 --> 01:24:40,720
the speech you're going to make tonight?

1957
01:24:40,720 --> 01:24:42,590
- No, I never read the
speeches before I make them.

1958
01:24:42,590 --> 01:24:44,110
I get more of a kick out of it that way.

1959
01:24:44,110 --> 01:24:46,587
- Uh-huh, it's just
exactly what I thought.

1960
01:24:46,587 --> 01:24:48,040
Beany, go on down to the office,

1961
01:24:48,040 --> 01:24:48,873
tell Pop to give you the speech.

1962
01:24:48,873 --> 01:24:50,400
There's a copy on my desk.

1963
01:24:50,400 --> 01:24:52,160
- Gee wiz boss, you know Mr. Norton

1964
01:24:52,160 --> 01:24:54,232
told me not to leave
him, even for a minute.

1965
01:24:54,232 --> 01:24:55,065
- Go on, go on, go on.

1966
01:24:55,065 --> 01:24:57,533
We'll be at Jim's Bar, up the street.

1967
01:24:58,430 --> 01:25:00,450
You're a nice guy, John.

1968
01:25:00,450 --> 01:25:04,480
I like you, you're gentle.

1969
01:25:04,480 --> 01:25:05,880
I always like gentle people.

1970
01:25:07,200 --> 01:25:09,640
Me, I'm hard.

1971
01:25:09,640 --> 01:25:10,763
Hard and tough.

1972
01:25:11,840 --> 01:25:13,727
I got no use for hard people.

1973
01:25:15,510 --> 01:25:17,300
Gotta be gentle to suit me.

1974
01:25:21,470 --> 01:25:22,803
Like you, for instance.

1975
01:25:30,540 --> 01:25:31,373
Yep, I'm hard.

1976
01:25:31,373 --> 01:25:34,060
But you want to know something?

1977
01:25:34,060 --> 01:25:35,797
I've got a weakness.

1978
01:25:35,797 --> 01:25:37,542
You'd never guess that would you?

1979
01:25:37,542 --> 01:25:39,365
Well, I have.

1980
01:25:39,365 --> 01:25:41,661
Wanna know what it is?

1981
01:25:41,661 --> 01:25:43,107
The Star Spangled Banner.

1982
01:25:44,310 --> 01:25:46,250
Screwy, huh?

1983
01:25:46,250 --> 01:25:47,740
Maybe it is.

1984
01:25:47,740 --> 01:25:51,900
Play The Star Spangled Banner
and I'm a sucker for it.

1985
01:25:51,900 --> 01:25:53,998
Always gets me right here.

1986
01:25:53,998 --> 01:25:55,492
You know what I mean?

1987
01:25:55,492 --> 01:25:58,077
- Yeah, it gets me right back here.

1988
01:25:58,077 --> 01:25:58,910
- Oh, back there, huh?

1989
01:26:00,030 --> 01:26:02,733
Well, every man to his own taste.

1990
01:26:06,099 --> 01:26:11,099
You weren't old enough for
the world war were you then?

1991
01:26:11,965 --> 01:26:14,139
No, no of course not.

1992
01:26:14,139 --> 01:26:15,639
You must have been just a kid.

1993
01:26:16,990 --> 01:26:17,823
I was.

1994
01:26:19,280 --> 01:26:24,053
I was just ripe and raring to go.

1995
01:26:25,167 --> 01:26:28,330
Know what my old man did when I joined up?

1996
01:26:28,330 --> 01:26:29,180
He joined up too.

1997
01:26:30,460 --> 01:26:31,393
Got to be a sergeant.

1998
01:26:31,393 --> 01:26:33,327
And here's a kick for you.

1999
01:26:34,200 --> 01:26:35,550
We were in the same outfit.

2000
01:26:36,664 --> 01:26:37,581
Funny, huh?

2001
01:26:43,447 --> 01:26:45,114
He was killed, John.

2002
01:26:47,580 --> 01:26:49,140
I saw him get it.

2003
01:26:51,230 --> 01:26:54,437
I was right there and
saw it with my own eyes.

2004
01:27:00,090 --> 01:27:02,513
Me, I came out without a scratch.

2005
01:27:04,600 --> 01:27:06,893
That is, excepting my ulcers.

2006
01:27:12,270 --> 01:27:15,023
I should be drinking milk,
you know this stuff is poison.

2007
01:27:16,116 --> 01:27:17,033
Hey, Jimmy.

2008
01:27:17,890 --> 01:27:19,062
- [Jim] Yes, Mr. Connell?

2009
01:27:19,062 --> 01:27:23,357
- What do you say, huh?
- All right.

2010
01:27:23,357 --> 01:27:26,237
- Yep, I'm a sucker for this country.

2011
01:27:26,237 --> 01:27:27,984
I'm a sucker for The Star Spangled Banner

2012
01:27:27,984 --> 01:27:30,000
and I'm a sucker for this country.

2013
01:27:30,000 --> 01:27:32,684
I like what we got here.

2014
01:27:32,684 --> 01:27:33,553
I like it.

2015
01:27:35,300 --> 01:27:38,960
A guy can say what he wants
and do what he wants without

2016
01:27:38,960 --> 01:27:41,540
having a bayonet shoved through his belly.

2017
01:27:41,540 --> 01:27:42,590
And that's all right.

2018
01:27:43,720 --> 01:27:45,267
- You betcha.
- Yeah.

2019
01:27:45,267 --> 01:27:49,319
And we don't anybody coming
around changing that, do we?

2020
01:27:49,319 --> 01:27:50,986
- No, sir.
- No, sir.

2021
01:27:51,850 --> 01:27:56,850
When they do, I get
mad, I get boiling mad.

2022
01:27:58,241 --> 01:27:59,450
Right now, I'm sizzling.

2023
01:28:01,940 --> 01:28:05,263
I get mad for a lot of
other guys besides myself.

2024
01:28:06,550 --> 01:28:08,883
I get mad for a guy named Washington.

2025
01:28:10,327 --> 01:28:13,950
And a guy named Jefferson and Lincoln.

2026
01:28:15,030 --> 01:28:16,463
Lighthouses, John.

2027
01:28:17,930 --> 01:28:20,573
Lighthouses in a foggy world.

2028
01:28:23,138 --> 01:28:25,322
You know what I mean?

2029
01:28:25,322 --> 01:28:26,655
- Yeah, you bet.

2030
01:28:27,980 --> 01:28:28,813
- Listen, pal.

2031
01:28:29,906 --> 01:28:33,100
This fifth column stuff is
pretty rotten, isn't it?

2032
01:28:33,100 --> 01:28:35,478
- Yeah, it certainly is.

2033
01:28:35,478 --> 01:28:36,934
- And you'd feel like an awful sucker

2034
01:28:36,934 --> 01:28:38,530
if you found yourself marching

2035
01:28:38,530 --> 01:28:40,323
right in the middle of it, wouldn't you?

2036
01:28:40,323 --> 01:28:45,323
You, of course you wouldn't
know it because you're gentle.

2037
01:28:45,475 --> 01:28:47,737
But that's what you're doing.

2038
01:28:47,737 --> 01:28:50,617
You're mixed up with a skunk, my boy.

2039
01:28:51,830 --> 01:28:54,393
A no good, dangerous skunk.

2040
01:28:57,870 --> 01:29:00,470
- You're not talking
about Mr. Norton are you?

2041
01:29:00,470 --> 01:29:03,143
- I'm not talking about his
grandfather's pet poodle.

2042
01:29:07,501 --> 01:29:09,450
- You must be wrong, Mr. Connell.

2043
01:29:09,450 --> 01:29:11,600
He's been marvelous
about the John Doe Clubs.

2044
01:29:11,600 --> 01:29:13,910
- Yeah. (laughing)

2045
01:29:13,910 --> 01:29:16,703
Say, you're sold on this John Doe idea?

2046
01:29:17,684 --> 01:29:18,517
- Sure.

2047
01:29:18,517 --> 01:29:20,615
- Yeah sure, I don't blame you, sure.

2048
01:29:20,615 --> 01:29:24,750
It's a beautiful miracle, a
miracle that can only happen

2049
01:29:24,750 --> 01:29:29,113
right here in the good old
USA, and I think it's terrific.

2050
01:29:29,113 --> 01:29:30,640
What do you think of that?

2051
01:29:30,640 --> 01:29:34,873
Me, hard-boiled Connell, and
I think it's plenty terrific.

2052
01:29:36,470 --> 01:29:37,310
Alright.

2053
01:29:37,310 --> 01:29:40,270
Now supposing a certain
unmentionable worm,

2054
01:29:40,270 --> 01:29:43,200
who's initials are D.B.,

2055
01:29:43,200 --> 01:29:45,190
was trying to use that to shove

2056
01:29:45,190 --> 01:29:47,680
his way into the White House.{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White_House}

2057
01:29:47,680 --> 01:29:49,703
So he could put the screws on.

2058
01:29:50,640 --> 01:29:54,263
So he could turn out the
lights in those lighthouses.

2059
01:29:57,830 --> 01:29:59,460
What would you say about that?

2060
01:30:00,672 --> 01:30:01,505
- Nobody's gonna do that Mr. Connell.

2061
01:30:02,993 --> 01:30:06,210
They can't use the John
Doe Clubs for politics.

2062
01:30:06,210 --> 01:30:08,010
That's the main idea.
- Is that so?

2063
01:30:09,060 --> 01:30:10,780
Then what's a big political boss

2064
01:30:10,780 --> 01:30:12,287
like Hammett doing in town?

2065
01:30:12,287 --> 01:30:14,857
And a labor leader like Bennett.

2066
01:30:14,857 --> 01:30:15,690
And lots of other big shots that

2067
01:30:15,690 --> 01:30:18,068
are up at D.B.'s house right now.

2068
01:30:18,068 --> 01:30:20,003
Wolves, John, wolves.

2069
01:30:20,840 --> 01:30:22,763
Waiting to cut up the John Does.

2070
01:30:24,770 --> 01:30:25,640
Wait till you get a gander

2071
01:30:25,640 --> 01:30:27,640
at that speech you're gonna make tonight.

2072
01:30:27,640 --> 01:30:28,800
- You're all wet.

2073
01:30:28,800 --> 01:30:30,090
Miss Mitchell writes those speeches

2074
01:30:30,090 --> 01:30:32,130
and nobody can make her
write that kind of stuff.

2075
01:30:32,130 --> 01:30:33,493
- They can't huh?

2076
01:30:33,493 --> 01:30:36,660
Who do you think writes 'em, my aunt Emma?

2077
01:30:36,660 --> 01:30:38,630
I know she writes them and gets

2078
01:30:38,630 --> 01:30:40,890
a big bonus for doing them, too.

2079
01:30:40,890 --> 01:30:43,700
A mink coat and diamond bracelet.

2080
01:30:43,700 --> 01:30:45,571
Don't write 'em?

2081
01:30:45,571 --> 01:30:47,170
Why that gold-grabbing dame

2082
01:30:47,170 --> 01:30:48,600
would double cross her own mother

2083
01:30:48,600 --> 01:30:50,920
for a handful of Chinese yams.

2084
01:30:50,920 --> 01:30:51,753
- Shut up.

2085
01:30:53,220 --> 01:30:55,470
If you weren't drunk, I'd--

2086
01:30:55,470 --> 01:30:56,363
- Hey, Boss.

2087
01:30:57,410 --> 01:30:59,310
Here's the speech, Boss.

2088
01:30:59,310 --> 01:31:00,850
Hey.

2089
01:31:00,850 --> 01:31:03,630
- Go on and read it John,
and then start sockin'.

2090
01:31:05,950 --> 01:31:07,893
- Hey wait a minute, Mr. Doe.

2091
01:31:09,623 --> 01:31:11,090
- Jimmy.
- Yes, sir.

2092
01:31:11,090 --> 01:31:13,113
- Better bring me a glass of milk.

2093
01:31:15,353 --> 01:31:17,030
I'm smoking too much.

2094
01:31:21,220 --> 01:31:23,100
- Yes, Charlie, everything all set?

2095
01:31:23,100 --> 01:31:23,933
Fine.

2096
01:31:23,933 --> 01:31:25,662
John Doe been take care of?

2097
01:31:25,662 --> 01:31:26,960
Good.

2098
01:31:26,960 --> 01:31:30,030
How many people will be there, 15,000?

2099
01:31:30,030 --> 01:31:32,330
Oh my, that's fine.

2100
01:31:32,330 --> 01:31:33,510
Now listen, Charlie.

2101
01:31:33,510 --> 01:31:35,554
As soon as John Doe stops talking about me

2102
01:31:35,554 --> 01:31:37,350
I want you to start that demonstration,

2103
01:31:37,350 --> 01:31:38,617
and make it a big one you understand?

2104
01:31:38,617 --> 01:31:40,660
- Don't worry about that,
D.B. my boys are there.

2105
01:31:40,660 --> 01:31:41,920
They'll take care of it.

2106
01:31:41,920 --> 01:31:42,866
- What?

2107
01:31:42,866 --> 01:31:45,413
Yes, I'll be there 15 minutes
after I get your call.

2108
01:31:46,630 --> 01:31:48,480
- [Man] Why, Mr. Doe.

2109
01:31:48,480 --> 01:31:49,718
- Where are they?

2110
01:31:49,718 --> 01:31:51,018
- In the dining room, sir.

2111
01:31:56,320 --> 01:31:57,630
- Gentlemen, I think we're about

2112
01:31:57,630 --> 01:31:59,380
ready to throw that great big bombshell.

2113
01:31:59,380 --> 01:32:01,080
- [Man] Yeah, it's about time.

2114
01:32:01,080 --> 01:32:02,760
Even a conservative estimate shows

2115
01:32:02,760 --> 01:32:04,730
that we can count on anywhere between 10

2116
01:32:04,730 --> 01:32:07,090
and 20 million John Doe votes.

2117
01:32:07,090 --> 01:32:10,507
Add that to the labor vote
that Mr. Bennett will throw in.

2118
01:32:10,507 --> 01:32:12,070
And the votes controlled by Mr. Hammett

2119
01:32:12,070 --> 01:32:14,200
and the rest of you gentlemen
in your own territories

2120
01:32:14,200 --> 01:32:15,170
and nothing can stop us.

2121
01:32:15,170 --> 01:32:16,929
- As I said before, I'm with you,

2122
01:32:16,929 --> 01:32:19,610
providing you can guarantee
the John Doe vote.

2123
01:32:19,610 --> 01:32:20,767
- [D.B] Don't worry about that.

2124
01:32:20,767 --> 01:32:23,330
- You can count on me, on one condition.

2125
01:32:23,330 --> 01:32:25,080
Little Bennett's gotta be taken care of.

2126
01:32:25,080 --> 01:32:25,913
- [D.B.] Didn't I tell you that everybody

2127
01:32:25,913 --> 01:32:27,910
in this room would be taken care of?

2128
01:32:27,910 --> 01:32:30,210
My agreement with you gentlemen stands.

2129
01:32:30,210 --> 01:32:32,320
- I'm with you, D.B., but I still think

2130
01:32:32,320 --> 01:32:34,390
it's a very daring thing we're attempting.

2131
01:32:34,390 --> 01:32:36,550
- These are daring times, Mr. Barrington.

2132
01:32:36,550 --> 01:32:38,640
We're coming to a new order of things.

2133
01:32:38,640 --> 01:32:40,349
There's too much talk been
going in this country.

2134
01:32:40,349 --> 01:32:41,182
- That's right.

2135
01:32:41,182 --> 01:32:42,015
- [D.B] Too many
concessions have been made.

2136
01:32:42,015 --> 01:32:42,848
- [Man] True.

2137
01:32:42,848 --> 01:32:44,010
- What the American people
need is an iron hand.

2138
01:32:44,010 --> 01:32:45,320
- You're right D.B.
- That's true.

2139
01:32:45,320 --> 01:32:46,700
- You're quite right, D.B.

2140
01:32:46,700 --> 01:32:48,783
Quite right.
- Discipline.

2141
01:32:49,857 --> 01:32:53,313
And now may I offer a little
toast to Miss Ann Mitchell,

2142
01:32:54,466 --> 01:32:56,150
the brilliant and beautiful lady

2143
01:32:56,150 --> 01:32:57,970
who is responsible for all this.

2144
01:32:57,970 --> 01:32:59,320
- [Everyone] Miss Mitchell.

2145
01:33:00,211 --> 01:33:02,672
- Mr. Norton, I'd like to talk
to you alone for a minute.

2146
01:33:02,672 --> 01:33:05,250
- Oh, Miss Mitchell has
something to say to us.

2147
01:33:05,250 --> 01:33:06,667
- Speech, speech.

2148
01:33:11,504 --> 01:33:12,337
- Hello.

2149
01:33:20,888 --> 01:33:22,136
- John, I'm so glad to see you.

2150
01:33:22,136 --> 01:33:25,450
I was terribly worried.
- Did you write this?

2151
01:33:25,450 --> 01:33:29,468
- Yes I did, John, but I had
no idea what was going on.

2152
01:33:29,468 --> 01:33:30,968
- You didn't?
- No.

2153
01:33:33,300 --> 01:33:35,350
- That's a swell bracelet you're wearing.

2154
01:33:39,440 --> 01:33:41,233
- John, why aren't you at the convention?

2155
01:33:58,310 --> 01:33:59,143
Is there anything wrong?

2156
01:33:59,143 --> 01:34:00,653
- No, no, nothing's wrong.

2157
01:34:01,540 --> 01:34:02,503
Everything's fine.

2158
01:34:03,980 --> 01:34:06,370
So there's going to be a
new order of things, huh?

2159
01:34:06,370 --> 01:34:07,430
Everybody's gonna cut himself

2160
01:34:07,430 --> 01:34:09,720
a nice fat slice of the John Does eh?

2161
01:34:11,040 --> 01:34:13,260
You forgot one detail, Mr. Big Shot.

2162
01:34:13,260 --> 01:34:16,640
You forgot me, the prize
stooge of the world.

2163
01:34:16,640 --> 01:34:18,253
Why if you or anybody thinks

2164
01:34:18,253 --> 01:34:19,887
he's gonna use the John Doe Clubs

2165
01:34:19,887 --> 01:34:21,130
for his own rotten purpose

2166
01:34:21,130 --> 01:34:23,050
he's going to have to
do it over my dead body.

2167
01:34:23,050 --> 01:34:24,980
- Now hold on a minute, young man.

2168
01:34:24,980 --> 01:34:27,150
Hold on, that's rather big talk.

2169
01:34:27,150 --> 01:34:30,000
I started the John Doe Clubs with my money

2170
01:34:30,000 --> 01:34:32,430
and I'll decide whether or not
they're being properly used.

2171
01:34:32,430 --> 01:34:33,263
- No, you won't.

2172
01:34:33,263 --> 01:34:34,610
You're through deciding anything.

2173
01:34:34,610 --> 01:34:37,370
And what's more, I'm going
down to that convention

2174
01:34:37,370 --> 01:34:39,633
and I'm gonna tell those
people exactly what you

2175
01:34:39,633 --> 01:34:41,670
and all your fine feathered friends here

2176
01:34:41,670 --> 01:34:43,840
are trying to cook up for them.

2177
01:34:43,840 --> 01:34:46,203
And I'm gonna say it in
my own words this time.

2178
01:34:48,625 --> 01:34:50,893
- Stop him D.B.
(angry chattering)

2179
01:34:50,893 --> 01:34:52,200
- [Man] He'll ruin us, D.B..

2180
01:34:52,200 --> 01:34:54,080
- Wait a minute, wait a minute fella.

2181
01:34:54,080 --> 01:34:55,727
My uncle wants to talk to you.

2182
01:34:55,727 --> 01:34:57,710
- Listen to me, my son.

2183
01:34:57,710 --> 01:34:59,860
Before you lose your head completely,

2184
01:34:59,860 --> 01:35:02,710
may I remind you that I picked
you up out of the gutter,

2185
01:35:02,710 --> 01:35:05,000
and I can throw you
right back there again.

2186
01:35:05,000 --> 01:35:07,760
You've got a nerve
accusing people of things.

2187
01:35:07,760 --> 01:35:09,470
These gentlemen and I know what's best

2188
01:35:09,470 --> 01:35:10,980
for the John Does of America

2189
01:35:10,980 --> 01:35:13,083
regardless of what tramps like you think.

2190
01:35:14,630 --> 01:35:16,220
Get off that righteous horse of yours

2191
01:35:16,220 --> 01:35:17,810
and come to your senses.

2192
01:35:17,810 --> 01:35:18,890
You're the fake.

2193
01:35:18,890 --> 01:35:20,780
We believe in what we're doing.

2194
01:35:20,780 --> 01:35:23,100
You're the one that was paid
the 30 pieces of silver.

2195
01:35:23,100 --> 01:35:23,933
Have you forgotten that?

2196
01:35:23,933 --> 01:35:25,380
Well, I haven't.

2197
01:35:25,380 --> 01:35:28,060
You're a fake, John
Doe, and I can prove it.

2198
01:35:28,060 --> 01:35:29,378
You're the big hero that's supposed

2199
01:35:29,378 --> 01:35:31,970
to jump off tall buildings and things?

2200
01:35:31,970 --> 01:35:32,950
You remember?

2201
01:35:32,950 --> 01:35:34,740
What do you suppose
your precious John Does

2202
01:35:34,740 --> 01:35:36,530
will say when they find out that you never

2203
01:35:36,530 --> 01:35:37,940
had any intention of doing it?

2204
01:35:37,940 --> 01:35:39,900
That you were paid to say so?

2205
01:35:39,900 --> 01:35:42,600
You're lucky they don't
run you out of the country.

2206
01:35:42,600 --> 01:35:44,380
With the newspapers and the radio stations

2207
01:35:44,380 --> 01:35:45,770
that these gentlemen control,

2208
01:35:45,770 --> 01:35:48,220
we can kill the John Doe
movement deader than a doornail.

2209
01:35:48,220 --> 01:35:50,620
We'll do it too the moment
you step out of line.

2210
01:35:52,410 --> 01:35:54,380
Now if you still want
to go that convention

2211
01:35:54,380 --> 01:35:56,843
and shoot your trap off,
you go ahead and do it.

2212
01:36:05,080 --> 01:36:07,501
- You mean, you'd try to
kill the John Doe movement

2213
01:36:07,501 --> 01:36:10,300
if you can't use it to get what you want?

2214
01:36:10,300 --> 01:36:12,493
- [D.B.] You bet your
bottom dollar we would.

2215
01:36:18,940 --> 01:36:20,763
- Well that certainly is a new low.

2216
01:36:22,920 --> 01:36:24,663
I guess I've seen everything now.

2217
01:36:27,070 --> 01:36:29,250
You sit there back with your big cigars

2218
01:36:29,250 --> 01:36:30,430
and think of deliberately killing

2219
01:36:30,430 --> 01:36:33,740
an idea that's made millions
of people a little bit happier.

2220
01:36:33,740 --> 01:36:35,680
An idea that's brought thousands of them

2221
01:36:35,680 --> 01:36:37,470
here from all over the country.

2222
01:36:37,470 --> 01:36:40,650
By bus, and by freight
and jalopy and on foot

2223
01:36:40,650 --> 01:36:42,080
so they can pass on to each other

2224
01:36:42,080 --> 01:36:44,930
their own simple little experiences.

2225
01:36:44,930 --> 01:36:48,320
Why look, I'm just a mug and I know it,

2226
01:36:48,320 --> 01:36:50,680
but I'm beginning to
understand a lot of things.

2227
01:36:50,680 --> 01:36:52,370
Why your types are as old as history.

2228
01:36:52,370 --> 01:36:55,270
If you can't lay your dirty
fingers on a decent idea,

2229
01:36:55,270 --> 01:36:56,463
and twist it and squeeze it and stuff it

2230
01:36:56,463 --> 01:36:59,280
into your pocket you
slap it down like dogs,

2231
01:36:59,280 --> 01:37:02,050
if you can't eat it, you bury it.

2232
01:37:02,050 --> 01:37:05,060
Why this is the one worthwhile
thing that's come along.

2233
01:37:05,060 --> 01:37:06,590
People are finally finding out

2234
01:37:06,590 --> 01:37:08,714
that the guy next door isn't a bad egg.

2235
01:37:08,714 --> 01:37:09,908
That's simple, isn't it?

2236
01:37:09,908 --> 01:37:11,810
And yet a thing like that
has got a chance of spreading

2237
01:37:11,810 --> 01:37:14,237
till it touches last doggone
human being in the world,

2238
01:37:14,237 --> 01:37:17,140
and you talk about killing it.

2239
01:37:17,140 --> 01:37:19,590
Well, when this fire dies
down, what's going to be left?

2240
01:37:19,590 --> 01:37:22,110
More misery, more hunger and more hate.

2241
01:37:22,110 --> 01:37:24,850
And what's to prevent that
from starting all over again?

2242
01:37:24,850 --> 01:37:27,450
Nobody knows the answer to
that one and certainly not you,

2243
01:37:27,450 --> 01:37:29,970
with those slimy, bollixed
up theories you got.

2244
01:37:29,970 --> 01:37:32,290
The John Doe idea may
be the answer though.

2245
01:37:32,290 --> 01:37:33,940
It may be the one thing capable

2246
01:37:33,940 --> 01:37:35,100
of saving this cockeyed world,

2247
01:37:35,100 --> 01:37:37,270
yet you sit back there on your fat hulks

2248
01:37:37,270 --> 01:37:39,260
and tell me you'll kill
it if you can't use it.

2249
01:37:39,260 --> 01:37:40,670
Well, go ahead and try!

2250
01:37:40,670 --> 01:37:42,940
You couldn't do it in a
million years with all your

2251
01:37:42,940 --> 01:37:45,820
radio stations and all your
power because it's bigger than

2252
01:37:45,820 --> 01:37:48,160
whether I'm a fake, it's
bigger than your ambitions

2253
01:37:48,160 --> 01:37:49,330
and bigger than all the bracelets

2254
01:37:49,330 --> 01:37:50,441
and fur coats in the world.

2255
01:37:50,441 --> 01:37:51,594
- You bet it is, John!

2256
01:37:51,594 --> 01:37:52,427
- And that's exactly what I'm going

2257
01:37:52,427 --> 01:37:54,200
down there to tell those people.

2258
01:37:54,200 --> 01:37:56,617
(applauding)

2259
01:37:57,917 --> 01:37:59,570
- I'm gonna, you ungrateful rat.

2260
01:37:59,570 --> 01:38:00,586
My uncle's been too good to--

2261
01:38:00,586 --> 01:38:03,340
(thumping)
(suspenseful music)

2262
01:38:03,340 --> 01:38:04,997
- He's getting away.
(whistle blowing)

2263
01:38:04,997 --> 01:38:06,217
- John!

2264
01:38:06,217 --> 01:38:08,506
- Get me the Bulletin.

2265
01:38:08,506 --> 01:38:09,891
- John.

2266
01:38:09,891 --> 01:38:12,523
- I told you, D.B., you're
playing with dynamite.

2267
01:38:14,433 --> 01:38:16,712
- Don't let that girl get away.

2268
01:38:16,712 --> 01:38:18,950
- Before he gets through
tonight, he'll ruin us all.

2269
01:38:18,950 --> 01:38:20,420
I'll stop him, I'll stop him cold.

2270
01:38:20,420 --> 01:38:21,699
Don't worry, I've been ready for this.

2271
01:38:21,699 --> 01:38:22,843
- John.

2272
01:38:22,843 --> 01:38:24,537
Oh John, please listen to me.

2273
01:38:24,537 --> 01:38:26,680
Please I can explain everything John.

2274
01:38:26,680 --> 01:38:27,747
I didn't know what they were going to do.

2275
01:38:27,747 --> 01:38:30,053
Let me go with you John.

2276
01:38:30,053 --> 01:38:31,130
John, please.

2277
01:38:31,130 --> 01:38:32,645
- Go ahead, driver, ball park.

2278
01:38:32,645 --> 01:38:34,393
- Let me explain it to you John.

2279
01:38:34,393 --> 01:38:36,163
Please let me go with you.

2280
01:38:36,163 --> 01:38:37,246
Please, John.

2281
01:38:38,293 --> 01:38:40,160
- Mr. Norton wants to see you.

2282
01:38:40,160 --> 01:38:42,090
- Listen to me, Mayor I
want you to do as I say.

2283
01:38:42,090 --> 01:38:43,300
I want them both arrested.

2284
01:38:43,300 --> 01:38:44,453
You tell the police
department to pick up Connell.

2285
01:38:44,453 --> 01:38:45,612
I've got the girl here.

2286
01:38:45,612 --> 01:38:46,445
- I've got the Bulletin.

2287
01:38:46,445 --> 01:38:47,310
- I don't care what you charge them with.

2288
01:38:47,310 --> 01:38:48,373
If you're worried let
them go in the morning,

2289
01:38:48,373 --> 01:38:50,575
but keep them in jail overnight.

2290
01:38:50,575 --> 01:38:51,440
Hello, Bulletin?

2291
01:38:51,440 --> 01:38:52,816
Put Buck Meyer on.

2292
01:38:52,816 --> 01:38:54,608
♪ With my banjo on my knee ♪

2293
01:38:54,608 --> 01:38:55,989
♪ I'm going to Louisiana ♪

2294
01:38:55,989 --> 01:38:58,115
♪ My true love for to see ♪

2295
01:38:58,115 --> 01:39:00,023
♪ It rained all night the day I left ♪

2296
01:39:00,023 --> 01:39:01,672
♪ The weather it was dry ♪

2297
01:39:01,672 --> 01:39:03,410
♪ The sun so hot I froze to death ♪

2298
01:39:03,410 --> 01:39:05,614
♪ Susanna, don't you cry ♪

2299
01:39:05,614 --> 01:39:08,986
♪ Oh Susanna, oh don't you cry for me ♪

2300
01:39:08,986 --> 01:39:12,890
♪ I've come from Alabama
with my banjo on my knee ♪

2301
01:39:12,890 --> 01:39:16,285
♪ Oh Susanna, oh don't you cry for me ♪

2302
01:39:16,285 --> 01:39:19,929
♪ I've come from Alabama
with my banjo on my knee ♪

2303
01:39:19,929 --> 01:39:23,342
♪ I came from Alabama
with my banjo on my knee ♪

2304
01:39:23,342 --> 01:39:27,060
♪ I'm going to Louisiana,
my true love for to see ♪

2305
01:39:27,060 --> 01:39:28,850
♪ It rained all night the day I left ♪

2306
01:39:28,850 --> 01:39:31,100
(cheering)

2307
01:39:43,323 --> 01:39:45,670
- Three cheers for John Doe.

2308
01:39:45,670 --> 01:39:48,420
- [Crowd] Hurray, hurray, hurray.

2309
01:39:53,020 --> 01:39:57,081
♪ My country 'tis of thee ♪

2310
01:39:57,081 --> 01:40:01,664
♪ Sweet land of liberty of thee I sing ♪

2311
01:40:07,275 --> 01:40:12,088
♪ Land where my fathers died ♪

2312
01:40:12,088 --> 01:40:17,025
♪ Land of the pilgrims' pride ♪

2313
01:40:17,025 --> 01:40:21,942
♪ From every mountainside
let freedom ring ♪

2314
01:40:29,358 --> 01:40:31,608
(cheering)

2315
01:40:38,460 --> 01:40:39,963
- Ladies and gentlemen.

2316
01:40:39,963 --> 01:40:41,546
- One moment, John.

2317
01:40:42,528 --> 01:40:45,111
We'll begin with a soft prayer.

2318
01:40:48,098 --> 01:40:49,265
Quiet, please.

2319
01:40:50,711 --> 01:40:53,597
Ladies and gentlemen, let's have a moment

2320
01:40:53,597 --> 01:40:56,830
of silent prayer for the John Does

2321
01:40:56,830 --> 01:41:01,830
all over the world, many of
whom are homeless and hungry.

2322
01:41:04,200 --> 01:41:05,330
Rise, please.

2323
01:41:06,985 --> 01:41:08,235
Everybody rise.

2324
01:41:36,612 --> 01:41:39,695
(frantic chattering)

2325
01:41:59,843 --> 01:42:00,693
- [Radio Broadcaster] News boys, hundreds

2326
01:42:00,693 --> 01:42:02,407
of yelling news boys are swarming

2327
01:42:02,407 --> 01:42:03,804
into the crowd like locusts.

2328
01:42:03,804 --> 01:42:05,157
- [Man] They're yelling John Doe's a fake.

2329
01:42:05,157 --> 01:42:05,990
- Fake?

2330
01:42:05,990 --> 01:42:08,907
(angry chattering)

2331
01:42:13,200 --> 01:42:16,283
- [Newspaper Boys] John Doe's a fake!

2332
01:42:18,579 --> 01:42:21,251
- Federal investigation
urged by Chamber of Commerce.

2333
01:42:21,251 --> 01:42:22,787
- How could he be a fake?

2334
01:42:22,787 --> 01:42:24,587
- It must be some kind of a gag.

2335
01:42:24,587 --> 01:42:26,587
- A what?
- A gag, a gag.

2336
01:42:28,127 --> 01:42:33,127
(whistle blowing)
(sirens blaring)

2337
01:42:37,877 --> 01:42:41,086
- Come on, come on step on it.

2338
01:42:41,086 --> 01:42:42,381
Step on it, step on it.

2339
01:42:42,381 --> 01:42:44,112
You all know you're places
now so let's get going.

2340
01:42:44,112 --> 01:42:45,164
Wait for the signal.

2341
01:42:45,164 --> 01:42:46,902
- Hey mister will you
autograph my balloon?

2342
01:42:46,902 --> 01:42:48,189
- Sure.

2343
01:42:48,189 --> 01:42:51,272
(frantic chattering)

2344
01:42:57,236 --> 01:43:00,712
- Ladies and gentlemen,
this is exactly what

2345
01:43:00,712 --> 01:43:04,295
I came down here to
tell you about tonight.

2346
01:43:05,176 --> 01:43:07,608
Please, if you all would just
be quiet for a few minutes,

2347
01:43:07,608 --> 01:43:10,775
I can explain this whole thing to you.

2348
01:43:11,635 --> 01:43:13,637
As you all know, this paper is published

2349
01:43:13,637 --> 01:43:16,528
by a man by the name of D.B. Norton.

2350
01:43:16,528 --> 01:43:18,538
- [Man] Get back, you.

2351
01:43:18,538 --> 01:43:20,788
- Everybody, wait a minute.

2352
01:43:21,818 --> 01:43:26,816
Wait a minute ladies and
gentlemen, my name is D.B. Norton.

2353
01:43:26,816 --> 01:43:28,708
You all know me.

2354
01:43:28,708 --> 01:43:31,230
I accuse this man of being a faker.

2355
01:43:31,230 --> 01:43:32,449
We've been taken for a lot of suckers

2356
01:43:32,449 --> 01:43:34,610
and I'm the biggest of the lot.

2357
01:43:34,610 --> 01:43:36,400
I spent a fortune backing this man

2358
01:43:36,400 --> 01:43:38,380
in what I believed to be a sincere

2359
01:43:38,380 --> 01:43:40,644
and worthy cause just as you all did

2360
01:43:40,644 --> 01:43:43,210
and now I find out it's
nothing but a cheap racket,

2361
01:43:43,210 --> 01:43:45,250
cooked up by him and two of my employees

2362
01:43:45,250 --> 01:43:47,420
for the sole purpose of collecting dues

2363
01:43:47,420 --> 01:43:49,172
from John Does all over the country.

2364
01:43:49,172 --> 01:43:50,460
- That's a lie!
- It's not a lie.

2365
01:43:50,460 --> 01:43:52,710
Nickel and dimes to stuff
into their own pockets.

2366
01:43:52,710 --> 01:43:54,280
You can read all about it
in the newspapers there.

2367
01:43:54,280 --> 01:43:55,760
- That's a lie, listen--

2368
01:43:55,760 --> 01:43:57,360
- Let go of me.
- Don't believe--

2369
01:43:57,360 --> 01:43:59,110
- This man had no intention of jumping off

2370
01:43:59,110 --> 01:44:00,240
the the top of a building.

2371
01:44:00,240 --> 01:44:01,360
He was paid to say so.

2372
01:44:01,360 --> 01:44:02,560
Do you deny that?

2373
01:44:02,560 --> 01:44:04,140
- That's got nothing to do with it.

2374
01:44:04,140 --> 01:44:05,520
- Were you paid or weren't you?

2375
01:44:05,520 --> 01:44:06,550
- Yes, I was paid, but--

2376
01:44:06,550 --> 01:44:08,100
- And what about the suicide note.

2377
01:44:08,100 --> 01:44:09,328
You didn't write that either.

2378
01:44:09,328 --> 01:44:10,161
- What difference does that make?

2379
01:44:10,161 --> 01:44:11,324
- Did you write it or didn't you?

2380
01:44:11,324 --> 01:44:12,608
- No, I didn't I didn't write it.

2381
01:44:12,608 --> 01:44:13,951
- You bet your life you didn't.

2382
01:44:13,951 --> 01:44:15,794
You look in your papers
ladies and gentlemen

2383
01:44:15,794 --> 01:44:17,175
and find you'll find Miss Mitchell's

2384
01:44:17,175 --> 01:44:19,237
signed confession that she
was the one who wrote it.

2385
01:44:19,237 --> 01:44:21,307
- Listen folks it's a fact that
I didn't write the letter--

2386
01:44:21,307 --> 01:44:22,140
- Now you see, he admits it.

2387
01:44:22,140 --> 01:44:24,320
You're a fake, John Doe.

2388
01:44:24,320 --> 01:44:27,117
And for what you have done to
all these people good people

2389
01:44:27,117 --> 01:44:29,068
they ought to run you out of the country.

2390
01:44:29,068 --> 01:44:30,901
And I hope they do it.

2391
01:44:39,781 --> 01:44:41,947
- [Man] Speak up, John, we believe you.

2392
01:44:41,947 --> 01:44:43,979
- Please listen, folks.

2393
01:44:43,979 --> 01:44:46,580
Now that he's through
shooting off his face,

2394
01:44:46,580 --> 01:44:49,119
I got couple of things to tell you about.

2395
01:44:49,119 --> 01:44:50,600
- Come on, the rest of
you get in here and riot.

2396
01:44:50,600 --> 01:44:51,800
Break this crowd up, come on.

2397
01:44:51,800 --> 01:44:53,800
- I'm sorry folks, but we
can't hear him anymore.

2398
01:44:53,800 --> 01:44:56,580
Something's gone wrong
with the loudspeakers.

2399
01:44:56,580 --> 01:44:57,720
- Doe's a fake.

2400
01:44:57,720 --> 01:44:58,553
Boo!

2401
01:44:59,540 --> 01:45:00,786
- Please, they can't hear me.

2402
01:45:00,786 --> 01:45:03,014
- Boo!
(yelling)

2403
01:45:03,014 --> 01:45:04,950
- This thing's not working.

2404
01:45:04,950 --> 01:45:06,000
Ladies and gentlemen.

2405
01:45:07,156 --> 01:45:11,406
(booing)
(yelling)

2406
01:45:24,290 --> 01:45:26,490
This thing's bigger
than whether I'm a fake.

2407
01:45:28,630 --> 01:45:31,243
Look Bert, you believe me, don't ya?

2408
01:45:32,106 --> 01:45:34,270
- Sure, I believe you.

2409
01:45:34,270 --> 01:45:38,640
Walking my legs off picking up
5,000 signatures for a phony.

2410
01:45:38,640 --> 01:45:40,080
There you are, Mr. Doe.

2411
01:45:40,080 --> 01:45:43,500
5,000 names asking you
not to jump off any roof.

2412
01:45:43,500 --> 01:45:44,764
- Makes no difference, Bert.

2413
01:45:44,764 --> 01:45:45,940
The idea's still good.

2414
01:45:45,940 --> 01:45:47,214
We don't have to give up our club.

2415
01:45:47,214 --> 01:45:49,972
- Yeah, well you can have it.

2416
01:45:49,972 --> 01:45:51,467
(shouting)

2417
01:45:51,467 --> 01:45:52,580
- They're starting to throw things.

2418
01:45:52,580 --> 01:45:53,880
Somebody's going to get hurt.

2419
01:45:53,880 --> 01:45:56,227
I'm afraid it will be John Doe.

2420
01:45:56,227 --> 01:45:57,709
Listen to that mob.

2421
01:45:57,709 --> 01:46:00,221
(shouting)

2422
01:46:00,221 --> 01:46:01,981
- I've got to go to him.

2423
01:46:01,981 --> 01:46:03,476
- Sorry, lady, I can't let you out.

2424
01:46:03,476 --> 01:46:05,171
- Let me go.

2425
01:46:05,171 --> 01:46:07,118
Oh please, please let me go.

2426
01:46:07,118 --> 01:46:08,070
They're crucifying him.

2427
01:46:08,070 --> 01:46:08,924
I can help him.

2428
01:46:08,924 --> 01:46:10,209
- [Man] Sorry sister, we
got orders to hold you.

2429
01:46:10,209 --> 01:46:11,042
- Orders from who?

2430
01:46:11,042 --> 01:46:12,476
Can't you see it's a frame up?

2431
01:46:12,476 --> 01:46:13,309
- Ann darling.

2432
01:46:13,309 --> 01:46:15,320
- [Ann] Oh Mother, they won't let me go.

2433
01:46:15,320 --> 01:46:17,690
They won't let me go.

2434
01:46:17,690 --> 01:46:18,673
(shouting)

2435
01:46:18,673 --> 01:46:20,006
- Listen, folks.

2436
01:46:21,072 --> 01:46:23,143
You gotta--
- Back to the jungle you hobo.

2437
01:46:23,143 --> 01:46:25,644
- Just another racket.

2438
01:46:25,644 --> 01:46:26,991
- Stick to your clubs.

2439
01:46:26,991 --> 01:46:30,071
- We've been fed baloney so
long we're getting used to it.

2440
01:46:30,071 --> 01:46:32,321
(shouting)

2441
01:46:38,677 --> 01:46:40,760
- The idea is still good.

2442
01:46:42,810 --> 01:46:44,310
Believe me, folks.

2443
01:46:46,962 --> 01:46:50,901
Listen John Does were
the hope of the world.

2444
01:46:50,901 --> 01:46:53,651
(angry shouting)

2445
01:47:26,321 --> 01:47:27,154
- [Radio Broadcaster]
They just finally managed

2446
01:47:27,154 --> 01:47:28,346
to get him out of the park.

2447
01:47:28,346 --> 01:47:30,515
And if that boy isn't
hurt it'll be a miracle.

2448
01:47:30,515 --> 01:47:32,053
Ladies and gentlemen this certainly looks

2449
01:47:32,053 --> 01:47:35,463
like the end of the John Doe movement.

2450
01:47:35,463 --> 01:47:40,296
- Well, boys, chalk up another
one to the Pontius Pilates.

2451
01:47:46,592 --> 01:47:49,240
- I should have been there.

2452
01:47:49,240 --> 01:47:50,440
I could have helped him.

2453
01:47:52,703 --> 01:47:54,383
He was so all alone.

2454
01:47:58,104 --> 01:47:58,937
- A lot of us are going to be mighty

2455
01:47:58,937 --> 01:48:00,860
ashamed of ourselves after tonight.

2456
01:48:01,954 --> 01:48:04,504
We certainly didn't give
that man much of a chance.

2457
01:48:14,230 --> 01:48:16,193
- [Colonel] Want sore coffee, Long John?

2458
01:48:17,070 --> 01:48:18,707
- No thanks, Colonel.

2459
01:48:26,867 --> 01:48:30,534
(dramatic orchestral music)

2460
01:48:38,127 --> 01:48:39,439
- Fake.
- Racketeer.

2461
01:48:39,439 --> 01:48:40,610
- Liar.
- Cheat.

2462
01:48:40,610 --> 01:48:43,040
- Impostor.
- Why don't you jump?

2463
01:48:43,040 --> 01:48:46,557
- Christmas Eve midnight. (laughing)

2464
01:48:46,557 --> 01:48:48,140
- Goodbye, Mr. Doe.

2465
01:48:48,977 --> 01:48:51,510
You're a wonderful man.

2466
01:48:51,510 --> 01:48:53,843
- And God bless you, my boy.

2467
01:48:54,745 --> 01:48:57,162
- Now get in there and pitch.

2468
01:49:00,082 --> 01:49:02,080
- You're a fake John
Doe and I can prove it.

2469
01:49:02,080 --> 01:49:04,440
You're the big hero that's meant to jump

2470
01:49:04,440 --> 01:49:07,380
off tall buildings and
things, do you remember?

2471
01:49:07,380 --> 01:49:08,570
What do you suppose your precious

2472
01:49:08,570 --> 01:49:10,540
John Does will say when they find out

2473
01:49:10,540 --> 01:49:12,240
that you never had any
intention of doing it?

2474
01:49:12,240 --> 01:49:13,970
That you were paid to say so?

2475
01:49:13,970 --> 01:49:17,134
- [Ann] Christmas Eve,
at midnight. (laughing)

2476
01:49:17,134 --> 01:49:22,134
♪ Silent night, holy night ♪

2477
01:49:24,903 --> 01:49:29,903
♪ Shepherds quake at the sight ♪

2478
01:49:31,988 --> 01:49:36,155
♪ Glories stream from heaven afar ♪

2479
01:49:39,284 --> 01:49:40,784
- Merry Christmas.

2480
01:49:42,409 --> 01:49:43,909
- Merry Christmas.

2481
01:49:47,241 --> 01:49:52,241
♪ Christ, the Savior is born ♪

2482
01:49:54,912 --> 01:49:58,662
♪ Christ, the Savior is born ♪

2483
01:50:04,144 --> 01:50:07,225
(chattering)

2484
01:50:07,225 --> 01:50:09,642
- [Carolers] Merry Christmas.

2485
01:50:10,686 --> 01:50:13,380
- Who, John Doe?

2486
01:50:13,380 --> 01:50:15,810
Is that screwball still around?

2487
01:50:15,810 --> 01:50:17,800
- That dame's been calling all day.

2488
01:50:17,800 --> 01:50:19,330
- Sure, sure, I know.

2489
01:50:19,330 --> 01:50:21,690
Yeah, at midnight, huh? (laughing)

2490
01:50:21,690 --> 01:50:25,783
Okay, lady, we'll have the
place surrounded with nets.

2491
01:50:25,783 --> 01:50:27,743
- They're laughing at me.

2492
01:50:33,680 --> 01:50:34,820
- [Man] You're a sick girl, Ann.

2493
01:50:34,820 --> 01:50:36,020
You better take it easy.

2494
01:50:38,730 --> 01:50:40,143
- Who are you calling now?

2495
01:50:41,310 --> 01:50:44,163
You called that number,
not 10 minutes ago.

2496
01:50:45,257 --> 01:50:46,753
- Hello. Mr. Connell.

2497
01:50:46,753 --> 01:50:47,586
Have you seen him yet?

2498
01:50:47,586 --> 01:50:49,520
- Now listen, Ann, he can't possibly

2499
01:50:49,520 --> 01:50:51,480
get in without our seeing him.

2500
01:50:51,480 --> 01:50:52,313
I'm watching the side door

2501
01:50:52,313 --> 01:50:54,563
and the Colonel's out
front so stop worrying.

2502
01:50:55,724 --> 01:50:57,057
- Oh, thank you.

2503
01:51:04,546 --> 01:51:07,379
- Why Ann.
- Ann, don't be foolish.

2504
01:51:11,040 --> 01:51:13,417
- If this isn't the
craziest, the battiest,

2505
01:51:13,417 --> 01:51:15,590
the looniest wild goose
chase I ever heard of.

2506
01:51:15,590 --> 01:51:16,423
- Oh shut up, Bert.

2507
01:51:16,423 --> 01:51:17,500
Sourpuss is right.

2508
01:51:17,500 --> 01:51:19,430
- Yeah well if he is, I'm a banana split.

2509
01:51:19,430 --> 01:51:21,490
- That man's gonna be on that roof.

2510
01:51:21,490 --> 01:51:23,145
Don't ask me how I know I just know

2511
01:51:23,145 --> 01:51:24,364
and you know it as well as you do.

2512
01:51:24,364 --> 01:51:26,870
- Sure, sure I'd like to
believe in fairy tales,

2513
01:51:26,870 --> 01:51:28,870
but a guy that's a fake isn't
gonna jump off any roof.

2514
01:51:28,870 --> 01:51:30,130
- I don't think he was a fake.

2515
01:51:30,130 --> 01:51:31,550
Not with that face.

2516
01:51:31,550 --> 01:51:33,490
At any rate what he
stood for wasn't a fake.

2517
01:51:33,490 --> 01:51:35,063
- Okay, honey, okay.

2518
01:51:41,586 --> 01:51:44,586
(humming musically)

2519
01:51:53,820 --> 01:51:56,247
- Elevators ain't runnin'.

2520
01:51:56,247 --> 01:51:57,080
- Colonel.

2521
01:51:57,080 --> 01:51:58,802
- [Colonel] You should've
got out of bed Miss.

2522
01:51:58,802 --> 01:51:59,899
- Has he been here?

2523
01:51:59,899 --> 01:52:01,159
- No.
- Have you seen him?

2524
01:52:01,159 --> 01:52:02,310
- Haven't seen him for a week.

2525
01:52:02,310 --> 01:52:03,143
- Where's Connell?

2526
01:52:03,143 --> 01:52:04,270
- He's watching the other door.

2527
01:52:04,270 --> 01:52:06,463
- Oh, you're swell.

2528
01:52:07,787 --> 01:52:08,620
Oh.

2529
01:52:11,910 --> 01:52:13,230
- No sense in going up there.

2530
01:52:13,230 --> 01:52:14,210
I've been here for hours.

2531
01:52:14,210 --> 01:52:15,043
He ain't here.

2532
01:52:15,043 --> 01:52:16,320
- [Ann] Let me go, will ya?

2533
01:52:20,510 --> 01:52:23,293
- Now that's crazy, it's 14 floors.

2534
01:53:06,464 --> 01:53:08,210
- It's as far as the elevator goes.

2535
01:53:08,210 --> 01:53:09,863
We gotta walk up to the tower.

2536
01:54:00,703 --> 01:54:02,810
- That tramp is probably
full of Christmas cheer

2537
01:54:02,810 --> 01:54:04,310
and asleep in some flophouse.

2538
01:54:04,310 --> 01:54:06,660
- [Man] Let's go, I've
got to decorate my tree.

2539
01:54:11,668 --> 01:54:14,335
(bells chiming)

2540
01:54:18,430 --> 01:54:20,113
- [Man] Well, I give up.

2541
01:54:20,980 --> 01:54:23,270
I don't what gave us the idea

2542
01:54:23,270 --> 01:54:24,740
he'd attempt anything like this.

2543
01:54:24,740 --> 01:54:26,020
- [Man] I guess you're right.

2544
01:54:26,020 --> 01:54:28,733
I'm afraid the joke's on us, let's go.

2545
01:54:28,733 --> 01:54:31,400
- I hope nobody finds out we've been here.

2546
01:56:14,340 --> 01:56:16,240
I wouldn't do that if I were you John.

2547
01:56:28,450 --> 01:56:29,500
It'll do you no good.

2548
01:56:30,587 --> 01:56:34,130
The Mayor has policemen
downstairs with instructions

2549
01:56:34,130 --> 01:56:36,560
to remove all marks of identification

2550
01:56:36,560 --> 01:56:37,723
you have on your person.

2551
01:56:39,430 --> 01:56:41,404
You'll be buried in Potter's Field

2552
01:56:41,404 --> 01:56:44,487
and you'll have accomplished nothing.

2553
01:56:53,932 --> 01:56:56,099
- I've taken care of that.

2554
01:56:57,120 --> 01:57:00,103
I've already mailed a copy of
this letter to Mr. Connell.

2555
01:57:03,180 --> 01:57:05,120
- John, why don't you
forget this foolishness?

2556
01:57:05,120 --> 01:57:06,780
- Stop right where you are, Mr. Norton,

2557
01:57:06,780 --> 01:57:08,880
if you don't want to go overboard with me.

2558
01:57:13,730 --> 01:57:17,360
I'm glad you gentlemen are here.

2559
01:57:17,360 --> 01:57:20,323
You killed the John
Doe movement all right,

2560
01:57:21,617 --> 01:57:24,030
but you're gonna see
it born all over again.

2561
01:57:31,881 --> 01:57:35,333
Now take a good look, Mr. Norton.

2562
01:57:35,333 --> 01:57:36,166
- John!

2563
01:57:37,143 --> 01:57:37,976
John.

2564
01:57:38,890 --> 01:57:41,064
Oh John, don't do it.

2565
01:57:41,064 --> 01:57:43,850
I love you, I love you darling.

2566
01:57:43,850 --> 01:57:44,783
Oh please.

2567
01:57:45,960 --> 01:57:47,550
Please don't give up.

2568
01:57:47,550 --> 01:57:49,370
We'll start all over again.

2569
01:57:49,370 --> 01:57:51,570
Just you and I, it isn't too late.

2570
01:57:51,570 --> 01:57:53,653
The John Doe movement isn't dead yet.

2571
01:57:55,111 --> 01:57:57,520
You see John it isn't dead
or they wouldn't be here.

2572
01:57:57,520 --> 01:57:58,947
It's alive in them.

2573
01:57:58,947 --> 01:58:01,443
They kept it alive by being afraid of it.

2574
01:58:01,443 --> 01:58:03,410
That's why they came up here.

2575
01:58:03,410 --> 01:58:04,583
Oh, darling.

2576
01:58:05,620 --> 01:58:06,720
Sure it should have been killed.

2577
01:58:06,720 --> 01:58:08,049
It was dishonest.

2578
01:58:08,049 --> 01:58:10,060
But we can start clean now.

2579
01:58:10,060 --> 01:58:11,430
Just you and I.

2580
01:58:11,430 --> 01:58:13,630
It'll grow, John, and it'll grow big

2581
01:58:13,630 --> 01:58:15,553
because it'll be honest this time.

2582
01:58:17,050 --> 01:58:22,010
Oh John, if it's worth dying
for, it's worth living for.

2583
01:58:22,010 --> 01:58:23,133
Oh please, John.

2584
01:58:24,600 --> 01:58:25,433
Oh.

2585
01:58:27,030 --> 01:58:28,830
Oh please.

2586
01:58:28,830 --> 01:58:31,093
Please God, help me.

2587
01:58:34,003 --> 01:58:35,416
John.

2588
01:58:35,416 --> 01:58:37,050
John, look at me.

2589
01:58:37,050 --> 01:58:39,415
You want to be honest, don't you?

2590
01:58:39,415 --> 01:58:42,580
Well you don't have to die to
keep the John Doe idea alive.

2591
01:58:42,580 --> 01:58:44,800
Someone already died for that once.

2592
01:58:44,800 --> 01:58:46,468
The first John Doe.

2593
01:58:46,468 --> 01:58:49,890
And he's kept that idea
alive for nearly 2,000 years.

2594
01:58:49,890 --> 01:58:52,570
It was he who kept it alive
in them and he'll go on

2595
01:58:52,570 --> 01:58:54,644
keeping it alive forever and always.

2596
01:58:54,644 --> 01:58:56,830
For every John Doe movement these men kill

2597
01:58:56,830 --> 01:58:58,910
a new one will be born.

2598
01:58:58,910 --> 01:59:01,260
That's why those bells are ringing John.

2599
01:59:01,260 --> 01:59:02,630
They're calling to us.

2600
01:59:02,630 --> 01:59:04,470
Not to give up, but to keep on fighting,

2601
01:59:04,470 --> 01:59:05,927
to keep on pitching.

2602
01:59:05,927 --> 01:59:08,350
Oh don't you see, darling?

2603
01:59:08,350 --> 01:59:10,620
This is no time to give up.

2604
01:59:10,620 --> 01:59:12,950
You and I John, we...

2605
01:59:14,020 --> 01:59:15,827
Oh no.

2606
01:59:15,827 --> 01:59:19,320
No, John, if you die, I want to die, too.

2607
01:59:19,320 --> 01:59:20,660
Oh.

2608
01:59:20,660 --> 01:59:22,305
Oh, I love you. (crying)

2609
01:59:22,305 --> 01:59:23,138
Oh John.

2610
01:59:36,560 --> 01:59:39,550
- Mr. Doe, you don't have to...

2611
01:59:40,670 --> 01:59:42,680
We're with you, Mr. Doe.

2612
01:59:42,680 --> 01:59:45,130
We just lost our heads
and acted like a mob.

2613
01:59:45,130 --> 01:59:48,712
- What Bert's trying to say
is that we need you Mr. Doe.

2614
01:59:48,712 --> 01:59:49,545
There were a lot of us that

2615
01:59:49,545 --> 01:59:51,680
didn't believe what that man said.

2616
01:59:51,680 --> 01:59:52,750
We were going to start up our

2617
01:59:52,750 --> 01:59:55,013
John Doe Club again,
whether we saw you or not.

2618
01:59:55,013 --> 01:59:56,480
Weren't we, Bert?

2619
01:59:56,480 --> 01:59:57,313
And there were a lot of others

2620
01:59:57,313 --> 01:59:59,000
that were gonna do the same thing.

2621
01:59:59,000 --> 02:00:00,223
Well, Mr. Sourpuss even got

2622
02:00:00,223 --> 02:00:01,810
a letter from his cousin in Toledo--

2623
02:00:01,810 --> 02:00:03,390
- [Smithers] I got it right here, Mr. Doe.

2624
02:00:03,390 --> 02:00:06,285
- Only, it'd be a lot easier with you.

2625
02:00:06,285 --> 02:00:07,118
Please.

2626
02:00:09,496 --> 02:00:11,913
Please come with us, Mr. Doe.

2627
02:00:32,597 --> 02:00:36,097
(upbeat orchestral music)

2628
02:00:37,154 --> 02:00:38,987
- Long John.
- Mr. Doe.

2629
02:00:40,136 --> 02:00:41,447
Here we'll help you with her.

2630
02:00:41,447 --> 02:00:42,280
She'll be all right.

2631
02:00:42,280 --> 02:00:45,328
- [Man] We'll take her down to the car.

2632
02:00:45,328 --> 02:00:47,061
We gotta car right back down there.

2633
02:00:47,061 --> 02:00:52,052
- There you are, Norton, the
people, try and lick that.

2634
02:00:52,052 --> 02:00:55,385
(upbeat dramatic music)
